const ACHV = {
'翼骑兵': { g:'tech', c:'波兰-立陶宛', y:[1576,1776], n:['翼骑兵','The winged hussars'],
  t:['背上装着一排羽毛的重骑兵——两百年里几乎没打输过,直到火器彻底改写战场。','Heavy cavalry with a rack of feathers on the back — for two centuries they almost never lost, until firearms rewrote the battlefield.'],
  a:[['装备是一手四五米长的空心长矛,比对方的步兵长枪还长:第一击刺穿之后长矛就折断,再换马刀。长矛做成空心是为了轻','Their weapon was a hollow lance four or five metres long, longer than the infantry pikes they charged; it shattered on the first thrust and they went on with the sabre. It was made hollow to keep it light'],
     ['背后那对插满羽毛的木架说法很多:吓马、防套索、冲锋时呼呼作响吓人,也可能只是身份的排场——同时代的记载没有统一答案','The wooden frames of feathers behind them have many explanations: to frighten horses, to foil lassos, to whistle terrifyingly in a charge, or simply as display — contemporary accounts do not agree'],
     ['1683 年维也纳城下,约三千名翼骑兵参与了史上规模最大的一次骑兵冲锋,奥斯曼的围城当天崩溃','Below Vienna in 1683 some three thousand of them took part in the largest cavalry charge in history, and the Ottoman siege collapsed that day']],
  s:['它是「骑兵最后的高光」:在长矛与马刀还能决定胜负的最后两百年里,这支部队是欧洲战场上最可怕的东西。此后燧发枪与刺刀普及,密集的步兵方阵不再怕冲锋,重骑兵就退成了仪仗。今天波兰仍把翼骑兵当作国家象征之一。','This was cavalry\'s last great moment: in the final two centuries when lance and sabre could still decide a battle, they were the most feared thing on a European field. Once the flintlock and bayonet spread, close infantry squares no longer feared a charge and heavy cavalry became ceremonial. Poland still treats the winged hussars as a national emblem.'] },
'象形文字': { g:'tech', c:'古埃及', y:[-3200,394], n:['象形文字','Hieroglyphs'],
  t:['一套用了三千六百年的字,失传一千四百年后靠一块石头重新读懂。','A script used for three thousand six hundred years, lost for fourteen hundred, and read again thanks to one stone.'],
  a:[['它不是「画什么就是什么」:同一个符号可以表意、可以表音、还可以当作分类的提示符,一个字往往三种用法叠在一起','It is not a matter of a picture meaning the thing it shows: one sign can stand for a meaning, for a sound, or as a silent marker of category — often all three at once'],
     ['最后一篇有确切纪年的碑文刻于公元 394 年,此后一千四百年无人能读;中世纪的欧洲人以为那是某种神秘的图画寓意','The last dated inscription was cut in AD 394 and for fourteen hundred years no one could read them; medieval Europe took them for mystical picture-symbols'],
     ['1799 年拿破仑远征军在罗塞塔挖出一块石碑,同一段诏令刻了三种文字;商博良比对二十多年,1822 年宣布读通','In 1799 Napoleon\'s expedition dug up a slab at Rosetta with the same decree in three scripts; Champollion worked on it for twenty years and announced in 1822 that he could read it']],
  s:['破译它这件事本身开创了一门学科——埃及学,也第一次证明失传的文字是可以被系统地找回来的。此后线形文字 B、玛雅文字的破译都沿用同一套办法:找双语对照、认出人名、再倒推读音。今天我们知道的古埃及,几乎全部来自这些字。','Cracking it created a discipline — Egyptology — and proved for the first time that a lost script could be systematically recovered. Linear B and the Maya glyphs were later read by the same method: find a bilingual text, identify the names, work back to the sounds. Almost everything we know about ancient Egypt comes from these signs.'] },
'共和政制': { g:'inst', c:'罗马共和国', y:[-509,-27], n:['共和政制','The Roman republic'],
  t:['一套「谁也不能一个人说了算」的设计——现代宪法里的分权与制衡,源头在这里。','A design meant to stop any one man deciding alone — the separation of powers in modern constitutions starts here.'],
  a:[['最高官职执政官同时设两个、任期只有一年,两人互相有否决权;卸任后要接受审查,这几条是为了防止有人变成国王','There were always two consuls at the top, each for one year only and each able to veto the other; on leaving office they could be called to account — all of it designed to stop anyone becoming a king'],
     ['元老院掌财政与外交,公民大会选官与立法,平民选出的保民官可以一票否决对平民不利的提案——三股力量互相拉扯','The senate held finance and foreign affairs, the assemblies elected magistrates and passed laws, and the tribunes the plebeians elected could veto anything against them — three forces pulling on each other'],
     ['遇到紧急状况可以任命独裁官,但法定任期只有六个月。这个制度用了四百年才被打破:恺撒 前 44 年成为终身独裁官,共和随之结束','In an emergency a dictator could be appointed, but only for six months by law. It took four centuries to break: Caesar was made dictator for life in 44 BC, and the republic ended with him']],
  s:['美国立宪时几乎逐条参考它:两名执政官变成一位总统加一部宪法,元老院变成参议院(senate 就是这个词),否决权、任期限制、权力互相牵制的思路全部继承下来。「共和」(res publica,公共的事)这个词本身也是罗马人的。','The framers of the American constitution worked from it almost clause by clause: two consuls became one president under a written constitution, and the senate kept its Roman name. Veto, fixed terms and the checking of power by power all came down from it — as did the word republic itself, res publica, the public thing.'] },
'唐诗': { g:'idea', c:'唐', y:[618,907], n:['唐诗','Tang poetry'],
  t:['两千多位诗人留下近五万首——中国人至今背的第一首诗,多半出自这三百年。','Some two thousand poets left nearly fifty thousand poems — the first poem a Chinese child learns is usually from these three centuries.'],
  a:[['科举考试要考写诗,于是读书人从小练;写诗也是社交:送别、赴任、宴饮、题壁,场合本身就是题目','The civil service examinations tested poetry, so anyone studying practised it from childhood; and poems were social — for partings, postings, banquets, or written on a wall, the occasion itself being the subject'],
     ['李白与杜甫风格几乎相反:一个写「飞流直下三千尺」,一个写「朱门酒肉臭,路有冻死骨」,而两人是朋友,同一场安史之乱把他们的一生劈成两半','Li Bai and Du Fu were near opposites — one wrote of a waterfall dropping three thousand feet, the other of meat rotting behind red gates while men froze in the road — and they were friends, both of their lives cut in two by the same rebellion'],
     ['清代编的《全唐诗》收了四万八千多首、两千两百多位作者,而这还只是流传下来的部分','The Complete Tang Poems compiled under the Qing gathered over forty-eight thousand poems by more than two thousand two hundred authors — and that is only what survived']],
  s:['它成了后世一千多年的标准:格律、意象、典故都从这里定型,日本、朝鲜、越南的文人也照着写汉诗。对今天的中国人来说,它还是共同记忆的一部分——「床前明月光」这样的句子,不需要注释就能懂,而它已经一千三百年了。','It set the standard for the next thousand years — metre, imagery and allusion all fixed here — and literati in Japan, Korea and Vietnam wrote Chinese verse to the same rules. For Chinese today it is still shared memory: a line like moonlight before my bed needs no gloss, and it is thirteen hundred years old.'] },
'史上最大陆地帝国': { g:'inst', c:'蒙古帝国', y:[1206,1294], n:['史上最大陆地帝国','The largest contiguous land empire'],
  t:['一个只有百万人口的草原民族,在两代人里打下了从太平洋到黑海的连片疆土。','A steppe people of perhaps a million took, in two generations, an unbroken stretch of land from the Pacific to the Black Sea.'],
  a:[['鼎盛时约两千四百万平方公里,是地球陆地的六分之一,连片而不隔断——这一点至今没有第二个例子(大英帝国更大,但是分散在各洲)','At its height some twenty-four million square kilometres, a sixth of the land on earth, and all of it joined — no other empire has matched that (the British was larger but scattered across continents)'],
     ['靠的不只是骑射:每名骑兵带三到五匹马轮换,日行可达百余公里;攻城则征用中原与波斯的工匠,把回回炮这样的重型器械一路带着走','It was not only archery from horseback: each rider led three to five remounts and could cover over a hundred kilometres a day, while for sieges they conscripted Chinese and Persian engineers and carried counterweight trebuchets with them'],
     ['管理上不强求同化:各地照旧用自己的官、自己的法、自己的宗教,只要按时纳税出兵。这既让扩张变快,也让帝国在忽必烈之后迅速分裂成四块','They did not insist on assimilation: each region kept its own officials, laws and religion so long as taxes and troops came on time. That made expansion fast — and made the empire split into four after Kublai']],
  s:['它把欧亚大陆变成了一个可以走通的整体:一个世纪里商队、使节、传教士、工匠沿驿路来回,马可·波罗、拉班·扫马这些人的旅行才成为可能;火药、印刷、纸币、瘟疫也一起走。今天中亚与东欧的许多边界,仍能看出这次冲击留下的痕迹。','It turned Eurasia into something one could cross: for a century caravans, envoys, missionaries and craftsmen moved along its post roads, which is what made journeys like Marco Polo\'s and Rabban Bar Sauma\'s possible — and gunpowder, printing, paper money and plague travelled with them. Many borders in Central Asia and Eastern Europe still show the marks of that shock.'] },
'哥特建筑': { g:'obj', c:'中世纪西欧', y:[1140,1500], n:['哥特建筑','Gothic architecture'],
  t:['一套让石头墙可以开满窗的结构——教堂里第一次亮了起来。','A way of building that let stone walls fill up with windows — the first time the inside of a church was bright.'],
  a:[['三样东西一起用才成立:尖拱把力往下导、肋架券把屋顶的重量集中到几个点、飞扶壁在外面把侧推力顶回去。墙因此不必再承重,可以掏空','Three things had to work together: the pointed arch to carry the load downward, the ribbed vault to gather the roof\'s weight onto a few points, and the flying buttress outside to push back against the outward thrust. The wall no longer carried the building, so it could be opened up'],
     ['墙掏空之后填进去的是彩色玻璃。当时的人不识字,窗上的画就是给他们看的圣经;沙特尔大教堂十三世纪的玻璃至今还在原位','What filled the openings was coloured glass. Most people could not read, and the windows were their Bible; the thirteenth-century glass at Chartres is still in place'],
     ['1140 年前后从巴黎北边的圣但尼修道院开始,一百年里传遍法国、英国、德国与西班牙,教堂越盖越高,一度成了城市之间的竞赛','It began about 1140 at the abbey of Saint-Denis north of Paris and spread within a century across France, England, Germany and Spain, the churches climbing higher and higher as cities competed']],
  s:['「哥特」这个名字是文艺复兴的人起的,带贬义,意思是「哥特蛮族的」——他们觉得这种建筑不古典、不讲比例。这个贬称后来变成了中性的风格名,是艺术史上少见的一次翻案。技术上,尖拱与肋架券的那套算法后来被文艺复兴和近代工程继承下去;而「让光进来」这件事,是欧洲建筑此后八百年一直在做的题目。','The name was coined in the Renaissance as an insult — Gothic meaning of the Goths, that is, barbarous, neither classical nor properly proportioned. The slur later became a neutral name for the style, one of art history\'s rarer reversals. Technically, the reasoning behind the pointed arch and the ribbed vault passed on to Renaissance and then modern engineering; and letting the light in has been the theme of European building ever since.'] },
'《拿破仑法典》': { g:'inst', c:'法兰西', y:[1804,2025], n:['《拿破仑法典》','The Napoleonic Code'],
  t:['第一部普通人也读得懂的民法典——把「法律面前人人平等」写成了可以照抄的条文。','The first civil code an ordinary person could read — it turned equality before the law into text other countries could copy.'],
  a:[['1804 年颁布。此前法国南北法律不同、各省各行其是,贵族与平民不同法;法典把这些一次扫掉,全国一套法、所有人同一部法','Promulgated in 1804. Before it, north and south France had different law, every province its own, and noble and commoner were judged differently; the code swept that away — one law for the country and the same law for everyone'],
     ['写法上刻意求短、求直白:两千多条,句子简单,不用拉丁术语,拿破仑要求「让农民也读得懂」,他在圣赫勒拿岛说过自己真正的荣耀是这部法典而不是四十场胜仗','It was deliberately short and plain: some two thousand articles, simple sentences, no Latin — Napoleon wanted a farmer to be able to read it. On Saint Helena he said his real glory was not his forty battles but this code'],
     ['内容并不都进步:确认私有财产与契约自由的同时,把已婚妇女置于丈夫的法律监护之下,这一条到二十世纪才陆续改掉','Not all of it was progressive: alongside private property and freedom of contract, it placed married women under their husband\'s legal authority, a provision only undone piece by piece in the twentieth century']],
  s:['法国军队走到哪里,法典就抄到哪里:比利时、荷兰、意大利、德国西部、西班牙,再由西班牙、葡萄牙传到整个拉丁美洲,又经日本影响到东亚。今天世界上大部分国家的民法都属于「大陆法系」,源头就在这里(另一支是英美的普通法)。法国本土的现行民法典,骨架仍是 1804 年那一部。','Wherever the French armies went the code was copied: Belgium, the Netherlands, Italy, western Germany, Spain — and through Spain and Portugal to the whole of Latin America, and by way of Japan to East Asia. Most of the world\'s civil law today belongs to the civil-law tradition that starts here (the other branch being English common law). The civil code in force in France is still, in its bones, the one of 1804.'] },
'东印度公司': { g:'inst', c:'荷兰共和国', y:[1602,1799], n:['东印度公司','The Dutch East India Company'],
  t:['史上第一家向公众发股票的公司——也是第一家拥有军队、能自己开战签约的公司。','The first company in history to sell shares to the public — and the first with its own army, free to make war and sign treaties.'],
  a:[['1602 年成立,荷兰议会授予它在好望角以东的贸易垄断权,连同募兵、筑城、开战、缔约、审判的权力:它是一家公司,行使的却是国家的权力','Founded in 1602 with a monopoly on trade east of the Cape granted by the Dutch parliament, along with the right to raise troops, build forts, wage war, sign treaties and hold courts: a company exercising the powers of a state'],
     ['它的股票可以自由转让,阿姆斯特丹因此出现了世界上第一个持续运转的证券交易所,买卖股票、也做期货与做空——现代金融市场的基本玩法在这里成型','Its shares were freely transferable, and Amsterdam grew the first continuously operating stock exchange in the world, dealing in shares, futures and short sales — the basic moves of a modern financial market took shape here'],
     ['垄断是用武力维持的:1621 年为独占肉豆蔻,它几乎清空了班达群岛的原住人口,幸存者被卖为奴隶。这不是失控,是当时被记录在案的经营决定','The monopoly was held by force: in 1621, to corner the nutmeg trade, it all but emptied the Banda Islands of their people, survivors sold into slavery. This was not a loss of control — it was a business decision, minuted at the time']],
  s:['它把「公司」这种东西的上限抬到了国家的高度,存在了近两百年,鼎盛时有上万名雇员、上百条船。1799 年因腐败与债务解散,资产由荷兰政府接手,变成了荷属东印度殖民地——公司的边界最终还是变回了国家的边界。今天的股份公司、股票交易所、乃至「有限责任」这套办法,源头都能追到它。','It raised the ceiling of what a company could be to the level of a state, lasted nearly two centuries and at its height employed tens of thousands and sailed hundreds of ships. Dissolved in 1799 amid corruption and debt, its assets passed to the Dutch government and became the Netherlands East Indies — the company\'s borders turned back into a country\'s. Joint-stock companies, stock exchanges and limited liability all trace back to it.'] },
'工业革命': { g:'tech', c:'大英帝国·英国', y:[1760,1840], n:['工业革命','The Industrial Revolution'],
  t:['人类第一次不再只靠人力、畜力、风和水干活——机器开始烧煤自己转。','The first time humans stopped depending on muscle, wind and water alone: machines began to turn on coal.'],
  a:[['关键一步是 1769 年瓦特的分离冷凝器:此前的蒸汽机每一冲程都要把气缸烧热再浇冷,浪费得惊人,分开之后效率翻了几倍,蒸汽机才从矿井抽水的笨机器变成什么都能带的动力','The key step was Watt\'s separate condenser of 1769: earlier engines heated and cooled the same cylinder every stroke, wasting most of the fuel; separating the two multiplied the efficiency and turned the steam engine from a clumsy mine pump into power for anything'],
     ['纺织业最先被改造:飞梭、珍妮机、水力纺纱机、走锭精纺机一步步把纺纱从家里搬进工厂,棉布价格几十年里跌到原来的零头','Textiles went first: the flying shuttle, spinning jenny, water frame and mule moved spinning out of the cottage and into the factory, and the price of cotton cloth fell to a fraction of what it had been within decades'],
     ['代价同时到来:童工、十二小时以上的工时、烟尘里的城市。1833 年英国的《工厂法》限制童工工时,并第一次派出专职的工厂视察员——此前 1802 年就有过一部管学徒的法,但没人去查','The costs arrived with it: child labour, days of twelve hours and more, cities in smoke. Britain\'s Factory Act of 1833 limited children\'s hours and, for the first time, sent inspectors to enforce it — an earlier act of 1802 had covered apprentices but no one checked']],
  s:['在此之前几千年,一个社会能产出多少,大体由有多少人和多少地决定;此后不再是。人均产出第一次持续、长期地增长,世界从此分成「工业化了的」和「还没有的」——今天国与国之间的贫富差距,大半是从这一段拉开的。它也是气候问题的起点:大气里的二氧化碳浓度,正是从 18 世纪末开始离开它几千年的水平。','For thousands of years before this, what a society could produce was set by how many people and how much land it had; after it, that stopped being true. Output per person began to rise steadily for the first time, and the world divided into the industrialised and the not — most of today\'s gap between rich and poor countries opened up from here. It is also where the climate problem starts: the concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere left its long-standing level in the late eighteenth century and has not returned.'] },
'戏剧': { g:'idea', c:'古希腊', y:[-534,-300], n:['戏剧','Theatre'],
  t:['一群人坐下来,看另一群人假装成别人——两千五百年前雅典发明的这件事,今天叫电影、电视、游戏。','A crowd sits down to watch other people pretend to be someone else. Athens invented that two and a half thousand years ago; today we call it film, television, games.'],
  a:[['起点是祭祀酒神的合唱队。传统说法是公元前 534 年泰斯庇斯第一个从合唱队里走出来单独说话,「演员」这个角色就此出现;后来埃斯库罗斯加到两个演员,索福克勒斯加到三个','It began with a chorus singing at the festival of Dionysus. Tradition has Thespis step out of the chorus to speak alone in 534 BC, which is where the actor comes from; Aeschylus later added a second actor and Sophocles a third'],
     ['它是城邦的公共活动,不是娱乐生意:每年在酒神节上比赛,由城邦出钱、富人赞助,几千甚至上万公民一起看,看完还要评奖','It was a civic occasion, not show business: a competition each year at the Dionysia, paid for by the city and by wealthy sponsors, watched together by thousands of citizens, with prizes awarded at the end'],
     ['悲剧演的多是人人都知道结局的老故事,看点不在情节而在「他为什么做这个选择」;喜剧则可以指名道姓地骂在世的政治家,阿里斯托芬当着本人的面演过苏格拉底','The tragedies retold stories everyone already knew the end of, so the interest lay not in what happens but in why a person chooses as he does; comedy could attack living politicians by name, and Aristophanes staged Socrates with Socrates in the audience']],
  s:['「三一律」、幕、场、主角与配角这套结构,西方戏剧照着用了两千多年,再由戏剧传给了电影与电视。更要紧的是它留下的问题:《安提戈涅》问「城邦的法与家里的义务哪个大」,《俄狄浦斯王》问「不知情的罪算不算罪」——这些题目今天的法庭还在处理。剧场本身也留下来了:雅典卫城南坡的狄俄尼索斯剧场遗址还在,埃皮达鲁斯的剧场至今能演。','The structure of acts and scenes, protagonist and supporting parts, was used by Western drama for two thousand years and passed from drama to film and television. What matters more are the questions it left: the Antigone asks whether the law of the city outweighs duty to one\'s family, the Oedipus asks whether guilt without knowledge is guilt — courts still work on these. The buildings survive too: the theatre of Dionysus on the south slope of the Acropolis is still there, and the one at Epidaurus can still be played in.'] },
'长船': { g:'tech', c:'维京·北欧', y:[800,1100], n:['长船','The longship'],
  t:['一条能横渡北大西洋、又能划进内河的船——维京人能走那么远,靠的就是它。','A ship that could cross the North Atlantic and still be rowed up a river — this is what let the Vikings go as far as they did.'],
  a:[['船身用叠压的木板一层压一层钉成(叫「叠板法」),整条船有弹性,能在大浪里弯曲而不散架','The hull was built of overlapping planks — clinker-built — so that the whole ship flexed with the waves instead of breaking up'],
     ['吃水浅到只有一米左右,既能冲上沙滩不用码头,又能划进塞纳河、伏尔加河这样的内河,这是它比任何同时代船只都可怕的地方','It drew only about a metre, so it could run straight up onto a beach without a harbour and could also be rowed up rivers like the Seine and the Volga — which is what made it more dangerous than any other ship of its day'],
     ['一面方帆加上两侧的桨,顺风用帆、无风用人,船尾一支侧舵掌向;奥塞贝格与戈克斯塔德两条船是从墓里挖出来的,现藏奥斯陆的维京时代博物馆(改建中,计划 2027 年重开)','One square sail plus oars along both sides — sail with the wind, row without it — and a side rudder at the stern; the Oseberg and Gokstad ships were dug out of burial mounds and are in Oslo\'s Museum of the Viking Age, closed for rebuilding until 2027']],
  s:['它把「海」从障碍变成了路。冰岛、格陵兰、诺曼底、罗斯,维京人住下来的每一处都是这条船带过去的;公元 1000 年前后他们靠它到了纽芬兰,是最早到达美洲的欧洲人。船的造法后来传给了整个北欧,汉萨同盟的商船、乃至更晚的北欧渔船,骨子里都还是它。','It turned the sea from a barrier into a road. Iceland, Greenland, Normandy, the land of the Rus — every place the Vikings settled, this ship carried them to; around 1000 AD it took them to Newfoundland, the first Europeans in the Americas. The way of building it passed to the whole of northern Europe, and the Hansa\'s merchantmen and later Nordic fishing boats are its descendants.'] },
'木板教堂': { g:'obj', c:'北欧诸王国', y:[1130,1350], n:['木板教堂','The stave churches'],
  t:['整座教堂用立起来的木板搭成,不用一根铁钉,屋脊上却刻着龙头。','A whole church built of upright planks without a single iron nail — and with dragon heads carved along the roof ridge.'],
  a:[['名字来自承重的立柱(挪威语 stav):先立柱,再把木板竖着嵌进柱与梁的槽里,整座建筑靠木工接口咬合,不靠钉子','The name comes from the load-bearing posts — stav in Norwegian: the posts go up first, then planks are set upright into grooves in posts and beams, the whole thing held by joinery rather than nails'],
     ['底部架在石基上不与地面接触,木料先在林中站着剥皮几年让松脂浸透——这两件事是它能站近九百年的原因','It sits on stone footings so the wood never touches the ground, and the timber was barked while still standing in the forest and left for years to let the resin soak through — those two things are why it is still up after nearly nine hundred years'],
     ['门框与屋脊上的雕刻还是维京人的纹样:缠斗的野兽、龙头。这是基督教的建筑,手艺和图案却来自上一个时代','The carving on the doorframes and roof ridges is still Viking: interlaced beasts and dragon heads. The building is Christian, the craft and the ornament come from the age before']],
  s:['中世纪的挪威建过上千座,今天还剩 28 座——不是被拆就是被更大的石教堂替掉了。最老的乌尔内斯教堂立了近九百年,1979 年列入世界遗产。它是欧洲木构建筑活到今天的最长一条线索:同时代的欧洲大陆几乎全用石头,木头的那一套只在北欧留了下来。','Over a thousand were built in medieval Norway and twenty-eight survive — the rest were pulled down or replaced by larger stone churches. The oldest, at Urnes, has stood nearly nine hundred years and was listed as World Heritage in 1979. It is the longest surviving thread of wooden building in Europe: the continent built in stone, and only the north kept the timber tradition alive.'] },
'青金石': { g:'obj', c:'阿富汗', y:[-4000,2025], n:['青金石','Lapis lazuli'],
  t:['一种深蓝色的石头,几乎全世界的古代青金石都出自阿富汗东北的同一片山。','A deep blue stone — almost all the lapis of the ancient world came from one stretch of mountains in north-eastern Afghanistan.'],
  a:[['产地是巴达赫尚的萨里桑矿,海拔两千多米,开采了六千多年,是世界上开采时间最长的矿场之一','The source is the Sar-i Sang mines of Badakhshan, over two thousand metres up, worked for more than six thousand years — among the longest-worked mines on earth'],
     ['公元前三千纪就沿商路卖到两河流域与埃及:乌尔王陵的头饰、图坦卡蒙金面具的眉毛与眼眶镶的都是它','By the third millennium BC it was travelling the trade roads to Mesopotamia and Egypt: the headdresses in the royal tombs of Ur, and the eyebrows and eye surrounds of Tutankhamun\'s golden mask, are all set with it'],
     ['磨成粉就是「群青」,中世纪欧洲最贵的颜料,一度比黄金还贵,所以画里只有圣母的袍子舍得用','Ground to powder it becomes ultramarine, the most expensive pigment in medieval Europe — at times dearer than gold, which is why in paintings only the Virgin\'s robe was worth it']],
  s:['一个内陆山国,四千多年前就和尼罗河边连着——这块石头是最早的跨大陆贸易的实物证据之一。它也说明「稀缺」是怎么形成的:全世界只有这一处出好料,于是从苏美尔到文艺复兴,想要这个蓝色的人都得沿着同一条路找过来。今天矿还在开,只是那条路上多了战争。','A landlocked mountain country was connected to the banks of the Nile four thousand years ago — this stone is among the earliest physical evidence of trade across continents. It also shows how scarcity works: one place in the world had the good material, so from Sumer to the Renaissance anyone who wanted this blue had to come down the same road. The mines are still worked; it is the road that has war on it now.'] },
'已知最早的硬币': { g:'obj', c:'吕底亚', y:[-640,-547], n:['已知最早的硬币','The earliest known coins'],
  t:['一块金子,学会了自报身价。','A lump of gold that learned to state its own worth.'],
  a:[['以弗所阿尔忒弥斯神庙的地基里埋着一批最早期的琥珀金币,1904—05 年发掘出土——它是给「硬币诞生」断代的头号锚点','In the foundations of the temple of Artemis at Ephesus lay a hoard of the very earliest electrum coins, excavated in 1904\u201305 — the number-one anchor for dating the birth of coinage'],
     ['最早的币一面有纹样,另一面只是打压留下的方坑;面额从一斯塔特到九十六分之一,最小的比指甲盖还小,只有零点一克多重','The earliest pieces carry a design on one face and only punch marks on the other; denominations ran from a stater down to a ninety-sixth, the smallest lighter than two-tenths of a gram and smaller than a fingernail'],
     ['同一个时代的中国在走自己的路:东周的布币、刀币由农具和刀削变形而来——「硬币」这件事,欧亚大陆两头是各自独立发明的','China in the same age went its own way: the spade and knife monies of the Eastern Zhou grew out of farm tools and blades — coinage was invented independently at the two ends of Eurasia'],],
  s:['以弗所出土的那批开山小币,今天大多藏在伊斯坦布尔考古博物馆——玻璃柜里几粒不起眼的金子,就是「钱」的起点。','Most of the pioneering pieces from Ephesus are kept today in the Istanbul Archaeology Museum — a few unassuming grains of gold behind glass, and the starting point of money.'] },

'采珍珠': { g:'tech', c:'海湾诸邦', y:[1500,1935], n:['采珍珠','Pearl diving'],
  t:['全城的家底,是一群憋着一口气的人从十几米深的海底捞上来的。','The whole town\'s wealth was brought up from ten metres down by men holding a single breath.'],
  a:[['采珠季从六月到九月,几百条船出海,一去三四个月;船上分工固定:船长(nokhitha)、拉绳人(seib)、潜水人(ghais)','The season ran June to September and hundreds of boats went out for three or four months at a time, with fixed roles aboard: the nokhitha who captained, the seib who hauled, the ghais who dived'],
     ['潜水人脚上系一块石头往下沉,鼻子上夹一只龟甲做的鼻夹,不带任何呼吸装备,一口气一分多钟,一天下潜三四十次,靠船上的人拉绳子拽上来','The diver went down with a stone tied to his foot and a clip of turtle shell on his nose, carrying no breathing gear at all — a minute or more on one breath, thirty or forty dives a day, hauled back up on a rope'],
     ['挣的常常不够还船主先垫的钱,欠债一年年滚,有的传给儿子;船上也有从非洲被卖过来的人','What a diver earned was often less than the captain had advanced him, and the debt rolled on year after year and sometimes passed to his sons; there were also men aboard who had been bought and brought from Africa']],
  s:['1930 年代日本人学会了养珍珠,天然珠一下没了买主,这门生意几年就见了底——而石油在南岸这一段还要再等二三十年。这套分工与船上唱的劳动号子被人记了下来、传了下去,没跟着这门生意一起消失。','In the 1930s the Japanese learned to farm pearls, buyers for natural ones vanished, and the trade was finished within a few years — while oil on this stretch of shore was still twenty or thirty years away. The division of labour and the songs sung aboard were kept and handed on — they did not die with the trade.'] },
'道路与引水渠': { g:'tech', c:'罗马帝国', y:[-312,400], n:['道路与引水渠','Roads and aqueducts'],
  t:['罗马人真正的本事不是打赢,是打赢之后把路和水修到那里。','What the Romans were really good at was not winning — it was the roads and the water that arrived after they won.'],
  a:[['道路分层铺:先挖沟、垫碎石、再压碎砖与灰浆、最上面铺石板,中间略高好排水;帝国盛期的干道加起来八万公里以上,有些路段两千年后还能走','Roads were laid in layers — a trench, then rubble, then crushed brick and mortar, then paving stones, crowned in the middle so water ran off; at its height the empire had over eighty thousand kilometres of main road, and some stretches can still be walked two thousand years later'],
     ['引水渠靠的是极缓的坡度把山泉引进城,坡降有的每公里只有二三十厘米,全程要保持不断——加尔桥那样的三层石拱,是为了跨过一条河而不改变那个坡度','The aqueducts worked by a very slight and unbroken gradient carrying spring water into the city, sometimes dropping only twenty or thirty centimetres per kilometre; the three tiers of arches at the Pont du Gard exist to carry that gradient across a river without changing it'],
     ['罗马城最多时有十一条引水渠同时供水,公共浴场、厕所与街边水池全靠它;水不是给富人的,是给全城的','At its peak the city of Rome was fed by eleven aqueducts at once, supplying the public baths, latrines and street fountains — the water was not for the rich, it was for the city']],
  s:['「条条大路通罗马」不是比喻,是行政制度:路是为了军队快速到达和公文传递修的,商队和后来的传教士只是顺便用上了。中世纪欧洲有几百年修不出同等水平的路与水利,直到 19 世纪才重新赶上。今天欧洲不少公路仍走在罗马路的路线上,而「水要修到全城」这个想法,是罗马留给城市最实在的一件东西。','All roads leading to Rome was not a figure of speech but an administrative fact: the roads were built so that armies could arrive fast and despatches could travel, and merchants and later missionaries simply used what was there. Medieval Europe went centuries without building roads or waterworks of the same standard and only caught up in the nineteenth century. Many European highways still follow Roman lines — and the idea that water should be piped to the whole city is the most practical thing Rome left to city life.'] },
'金凳': { g:'obj', c:'阿散蒂', y:[1700,1902], n:['金凳','The Golden Stool'],
  t:['一张没有人坐过的凳子——阿散蒂人相信整个民族的灵魂都在里面。','A stool no one has ever sat on: the Asante hold that the soul of the whole nation is inside it.'],
  a:[['约1700年,祭司奥科姆福·阿诺基宣称金凳自天而降落在首任国王奥赛·图图膝上,自此成为王权与民族统一的象征','Around 1700 the priest Okomfo Anokye declared that the stool had come down from the sky onto the lap of the first king, Osei Tutu, and from then on it stood for kingship and for the unity of the nation'],
     ['它从不用于就座:即使国王也只是象征性地在它上方悬空一坐,平时放在自己的座椅上,不着地','It is never sat upon: even the king only hovers above it in a symbolic gesture, and the rest of the time it rests on a chair of its own, never on the ground'],
     ['1900年英国总督要求把它交出来供自己就座,阿散蒂人随即起兵,史称金凳之战——由太后雅雅·阿散蒂瓦领导','In 1900 the British governor demanded it be handed over for him to sit on; the Asante went to war over it, led by the queen mother Yaa Asantewaa']],
  s:['英国人没有理解他们要的是什么:金凳不是王座,而是民族本身的容器,交出它等于交出存在。这场因一件器物而起的战争是英阿之间的最后一次,阿散蒂战败并入殖民地,但金凳始终没有落入英国人手中——直到今天仍由阿散蒂王室保管。它是一个很清楚的例子:一件东西的意义由谁来定义,可以直接决定一场战争打不打得起来。','The British had not grasped what they were asking for: the Golden Stool is not a throne but the vessel of the nation itself, and to surrender it would have been to surrender existence. The war it caused was the last between Britain and Asante; Asante lost and was absorbed into the colony, but the stool never fell into British hands and is still kept by the Asante royal house. It is a very clear case of how much can turn on who gets to say what an object means — including whether a war happens at all.'] },
'巨型方尖碑': { g:'obj', c:'阿克苏姆', y:[300,400], n:['阿克苏姆方尖碑','The stelae of Aksum'],
  t:['整块花岗岩凿出来的高塔,表面刻成一栋多层楼房的样子,底下是王陵。','Towers cut from single blocks of granite, their faces carved to look like multi-storey buildings, with royal tombs beneath.'],
  a:[['约4世纪立,最大的一根长约三十三米、重五百吨上下,是人类试图竖立过的最大整块石头之一——它很可能在竖立时就断了,真正立起来的最高一根约二十四米','Raised around the fourth century; the largest is some thirty-three metres and around five hundred tonnes, among the largest single stones people have ever tried to stand upright — it probably broke in the attempt, and the tallest that actually stands is about twenty-four metres'],
     ['碑面刻着假门与一排排假窗,把一栋高楼的立面刻在石头上,越往上层数越多','The faces carry a false door and rows of false windows, the elevation of a tall building cut into the stone, with more storeys as it rises'],
     ['1937年意大利占领期间掠走一根运到罗马,2005年运回、2008年重新竖起','One was taken to Rome during the Italian occupation in 1937, returned in 2005 and re-erected in 2008']],
  s:['它们立在基督教成为国教前后的年代,是阿克苏姆国力的直接量度:能开采、运输并竖起这种体量的石头,意味着有稳定的组织与充足的余粮。碑下是王陵,王国同期还自铸金币——在当时的红海世界,这是大国才有的排场。1980年列入世界遗产。','They were raised around the time Christianity became the state religion, and they are a direct measure of what Aksum could do: quarrying, moving and standing up stone on this scale means stable organisation and surplus food. Beneath them lie royal tombs, and the kingdom struck its own gold coin — the trappings of a major power in the Red Sea world of its day. A World Heritage site since 1980.'] },
'廷巴克图经学院与手稿库': { g:'inst', c:'马里帝国', y:[1300,1600], n:['廷巴克图的经学院与手稿','Timbuktu\'s schools and manuscripts'],
  t:['撒哈拉南缘的一座商城,同时是抄书、藏书与讲学的地方。','A trading city on the southern edge of the Sahara that was also a place for copying, keeping and teaching from books.'],
  a:[['盐、金与书一起过沙漠:商队从北非带回纸与手稿,当地抄写、注释再流通,书本身成了贵重商品','Salt, gold and books crossed the desert together: caravans brought paper and manuscripts from North Africa, local scribes copied and annotated them, and books themselves became valuable goods'],
     ['桑科雷等清真寺附设的讲学圈规模很大,内容涵盖教法、天文、数学、医学与诗歌','The teaching circles attached to the Sankore and other mosques were large, and covered law, astronomy, mathematics, medicine and poetry'],
     ['手稿多由家族世代私藏,至今仍有数十万件分散在廷巴克图与周边','Most of the manuscripts stayed in family hands down the generations, and hundreds of thousands are still scattered around Timbuktu and its region']],
  s:['它推翻了一个流传很广的印象——撒南非洲只有口传、没有文字传统。这些手稿是当地人用阿拉伯字母书写、包括用它拼写本地语言的成文记录。2012年武装组织占领廷巴克图并焚毁部分藏书时,当地图书馆员与居民把大批手稿装箱、藏进民宅、再分批用船与摩托运往巴马科,救出约三十五万件——这件事本身已成为这座城市历史的一部分。','It overturns a widely held impression — that sub-Saharan Africa had only oral tradition and no written one. These manuscripts are written records made locally in Arabic script, including local languages spelled with it. When an armed group took Timbuktu in 2012 and burned part of the holdings, librarians and residents crated up the manuscripts, hid them in private houses and moved them out to Bamako by boat and motorbike in batches, saving something like 350,000 items — and that rescue is now itself part of the city\'s history.'] },
'盐金贸易体系': { g:'inst', c:'加纳帝国', y:[700,1240], n:['盐金贸易','The salt-and-gold trade'],
  t:['北边的沙漠出盐,南边的森林出金,加纳坐在中间收过路费。','Salt came from the desert in the north, gold from the forest in the south, and Ghana sat in between and taxed what passed.'],
  a:[['盐在西非内陆极缺、在撒哈拉却成矿成板地产出,骆驼商队把盐板南运,换回黄金','Salt was desperately short in the West African interior and came out of the Sahara in slabs; camel caravans carried it south and brought gold back'],
     ['加纳王不产金也不产盐,靠对进出货物两头征税与垄断金块(碎金归民间、大块归王室)维持','The kings of Ghana produced neither, and lived on taxing goods in both directions and on a monopoly of nuggets — gold dust to the people, large pieces to the crown'],
     ['关于它的记载多出自阿拉伯地理学家之手,本地并无同时代文字史料——所以细节要谨慎对待','Almost all the descriptions come from Arab geographers, with no contemporary local written source, so the details need caution']],
  s:['这条商路把西非接进了地中海与伊斯兰世界的经济体系,伊斯兰教也顺着它南下传播——先在商人与宫廷,再逐步扩散。它同时说明一件事:控制资源不一定要拥有资源,控制通道就够了,而这正是后来马里与桑海继续做的生意。西非的黄金一度是地中海世界金币的主要来源之一,曼萨·穆萨1324年赴麦加途中散金过多、扰乱开罗金价的记载,就是这个体系体量的一个侧写。','The route plugged West Africa into the Mediterranean and Islamic economies, and Islam moved south along it — first among merchants and courts, then more widely. It also shows that controlling a resource need not mean owning it: controlling the passage is enough, which is the same business Mali and Songhai carried on. West African gold was for a time one of the main sources of coin in the Mediterranean world, and the reports of Mansa Musa disturbing the price of gold in Cairo on his way to Mecca in 1324 are one measure of the scale involved.'] },
'独立发明零': { g:'idea', c:'玛雅', y:[292,900], n:['玛雅的零','The Maya zero'],
  t:['一个贝壳形状的符号表示「没有」,让玛雅人能写出几十万年的日期。','A shell-shaped sign standing for nothing, which let the Maya write dates hundreds of thousands of years long.'],
  a:[['一点表示1、一横表示5、贝壳形表示0,三个符号就够记数——记数法以二十为进位','A dot for one, a bar for five, a shell for zero: three signs are enough, in a system that counts by twenties'],
     ['已知最早的长纪历日期刻于公元前1世纪,出自玛雅地区以西——长纪历一般认为早于玛雅;最早确凿属于玛雅的一条刻在公元292年的蒂卡尔石碑上,与印度那一支各自独立、互不知情','The earliest Long Count dates are from the first century BC and come from west of the Maya area — the count is generally thought to predate the Maya; the earliest securely Maya one is on a stela at Tikal from AD 292. It was arrived at quite independently of the Indian line, and in ignorance of it'],
     ['它主要服务于历法:长纪历要标出从神话起点算起的天数,不能容忍某一位空着','It served the calendar above all: the Long Count records the number of days since a mythical starting point, and no place in it can be left blank']],
  s:['同一个想法在彼此不接触的几个文明里各自出现过,这件事本身值得记住——它说明零不是某个天才的偶得,而是「用位值制记大数」这个需求逼出来的。区别在于后来:印度那一支经阿拉伯世界传到欧洲,成了今天全世界通用的算术底子;玛雅这一支没能传出中美洲,西班牙人到来后,连读懂它的人也几乎断绝,直到20世纪才被重新破译。','That the same idea arose more than once, in civilisations that never met, is worth holding onto — it says zero was not one genius\'s lucky thought but something forced by the need to write large numbers in place-value. The difference is what happened next: the Indian line travelled through the Arab world into Europe and became the arithmetic the whole world now uses, while the Maya line never left Mesoamerica, and after the Spanish arrived the ability to read it nearly died out altogether, to be recovered only in the twentieth century.'] },
'马丘比丘': { g:'obj', c:'印加', y:[1450,1530], n:['马丘比丘','Machu Picchu'],
  t:['两千四百米高的山脊上一座石城,西班牙人没有拆过它。','A stone settlement on a ridge 2,400 metres up, which the Spanish never pulled down.'],
  a:[['约1450年在帕查库特克时期建成,规模不大,一般认为是王室的庄园与宗教场所,不是城市或要塞','Built around 1450 under Pachacuti; it is small, and is generally taken for a royal estate and religious site rather than a city or a fortress'],
     ['石墙不用灰浆,石块磨到严丝合缝,地震时石块会跳动再落回原位——这一带地震频繁','The walls use no mortar and the stones are ground to fit exactly; in an earthquake they jump and settle back, and this is earthquake country'],
     ['1911年宾厄姆把它介绍给外界,而当地农户一直住在附近并知道它——「发现」是外部世界的说法','Bingham brought it to outside attention in 1911, though local farmers lived nearby and knew perfectly well it was there; discovery is the outside world\'s word for it']],
  s:['它之所以完整,恰恰因为不重要:西班牙人追的是黄金与都城,这处山中庄园不在他们的路线上,躲过了系统性的拆毁——库斯科的印加神庙被拆下来当教堂的地基,而这里没有。梯田、水渠与排水系统保存完好,是理解印加如何在陡坡上组织农业的最好实物。1983年列入世界遗产,今天要限流才承受得住游客量。','It survives intact precisely because it did not matter: the Spanish were after gold and capitals, this mountain estate was not on their route, and it escaped the systematic dismantling — the Inca temples of Cusco were taken apart to found churches, and here nothing was. Its terraces, channels and drainage are intact, and are the best physical evidence of how the Inca farmed a steep slope. A World Heritage site since 1983, it now needs a visitor cap to survive the numbers.'] },
'结绳记事': { g:'tech', c:'印加', y:[1400,1570], n:['奇普(结绳记事)','The quipu'],
  t:['一根主绳上垂下许多细绳,绳上打结——结的位置和形状就是数字。','Cords hanging from a main cord, with knots tied in them: where the knot sits and what shape it is are the number.'],
  a:[['数字用十进位值制记录:同一根绳上,靠上的结代表更大的位,与今天写数字的道理一样','Numbers are recorded in decimal place value: on one cord, a knot higher up stands for a higher place, on the same principle as writing digits today'],
     ['印加没有文字,全国的人口、赋税、仓储、劳役都靠奇普记账,由专职的「奇普卡马约克」保管与解读','The Inca had no writing, and the population, tax, stores and labour service of the whole state were kept on quipus by specialists trained to make and read them'],
     ['数字部分已被破译,而少数不像账目的奇普是否记录了叙事,至今没有定论','The numerical part has been read; whether a minority of quipus that do not look like accounts record narrative is still unsettled']],
  s:['它逼人重新想一遍「什么算文字」:如果一套符号能可靠地记录、传递并被他人准确复原,那么它与文字的差别在哪里?西班牙人把大量奇普当作偶像崇拜之物销毁,现存一千余件。有意思的是,它记数的方式与印度—阿拉伯数字是同一个原理——十进位值制——而两边毫无往来——位值制在互不相通的地方被想到过好几次。','It forces the question of what counts as writing to be asked again: if a system reliably records information, moves it, and lets someone else recover it exactly, where is the line? The Spanish destroyed great numbers of quipus as idolatrous objects, and somewhat over a thousand survive. What is striking is that its way of recording numbers rests on the same principle as Hindu-Arabic numerals — decimal place value — with no contact whatever between them; place value was reached independently in several places that never met.'] },
'浮田农业': { g:'tech', c:'阿兹特克', y:[1200,1521], n:['奇南帕(浮田)','Chinampa farming'],
  t:['在浅湖里围出一块块长条田,用湖泥堆高、四周种柳树固定——一年能收好几季。','Long strips of field fenced off in the shallow lake, built up with lake mud and held in place by willows planted around them — several harvests a year.'],
  a:[['先在湖底打木桩、编枝条围出长方形,再一层层填入湖泥与水草,直到高出水面几十厘米','Stakes were driven into the lake bed and withies woven into rectangles, then filled layer by layer with mud and water weed until they stood a few tens of centimetres above the water'],
     ['四周种柳树,根系把田块锁住;沟渠里的水随时可取,湖泥又是现成的肥料','Willows planted along the edges lock the plot together with their roots; the channels put water always within reach, and the lake mud is fertiliser already on site'],
     ['它不「浮」——名字容易误导,田块是坐在湖底上的,只是被水包围','It does not actually float, whatever the name suggests: the plots sit on the lake bed and are merely surrounded by water']],
  s:['特诺奇蒂特兰建在湖中的岛上,人口达到二十万上下,而周边并没有多少可耕的旱地——奇南帕是这座城能存在的物质前提。它的单位产量在前工业时代的农业里是很高的,靠的不是新作物或新工具,而是把水、土、肥三件事在空间上叠在一起。今天墨西哥城南的霍奇米尔科仍有人这样种地,2017年被联合国粮农组织列为全球重要农业文化遗产。','Tenochtitlan stood on islands in a lake and reached something like 200,000 people, with little dry farmland around it — the chinampas are the material precondition for that city existing at all. Their yield per unit area is high by pre-industrial standards, achieved with no new crop and no new tool, simply by stacking water, soil and fertiliser in the same place. People still farm this way at Xochimilco in the south of Mexico City, and the system was recognised by the FAO as a globally important agricultural heritage system in 2017.'] },
'民主制': { g:'inst', c:'古希腊', y:[-508,-322], n:['雅典民主制','Athenian democracy'],
  t:['公民自己开会决定城邦的事,官职大多靠抽签,不靠选举。','Citizens met and decided the city\'s business themselves, and most offices were filled by lot rather than by election.'],
  a:[['公元前508年前后克里斯提尼改革,把公民按居住地而非血缘重新编组,打散了旧贵族的地盘','Cleisthenes\' reforms around 508 BC regrouped citizens by where they lived instead of by kinship, breaking up the old aristocratic blocs'],
     ['公民大会人人可发言、可表决;五百人议事会与陪审团由抽签产生——抽签被认为比选举更平等,因为选举有利于有名望的人','Any citizen could speak and vote in the assembly, while the council of five hundred and the juries were chosen by lot — the lot was held to be fairer than election, which favours the well known'],
     ['「公民」不包括女性、奴隶与外邦居民,估计只占全部居民的一到两成','Citizens did not include women, slaves or resident foreigners; the franchise is thought to have covered only one or two people in ten']],
  s:['把它叫作现代民主的祖先要小心:雅典是直接参与、抽签任职、且把多数人排除在外,与今天的代议制差别极大。真正传下来的是几个更基本的东西——公共事务应当公开讨论、权力应当轮流、决定应当由被决定的人做出。它自己也知道风险:苏格拉底就是被这个制度依程序判死的,而这件事此后两千年一直是反对民主的人最常举的例子。','Calling it the ancestor of modern democracy needs care: Athens was direct, filled offices by lot, and shut most people out — very unlike representative government today. What did carry forward is more basic: that public matters should be argued in public, that power should rotate, and that decisions should be made by those they fall on. It knew its own risks too — Socrates was condemned to death by this system, in due form, and for two thousand years afterwards that was the example opponents of democracy reached for first.'] },
'奥运会': { g:'inst', c:'古希腊', y:[-776,393], n:['古代奥林匹克运动会','The ancient Olympic Games'],
  t:['每四年在奥林匹亚办一次,办了一千一百多年——比任何一个希腊城邦活得都久。','Held at Olympia every four years for more than eleven centuries — longer than any Greek city-state lasted.'],
  a:[['传统认为始于公元前776年,希腊人后来用「第几届奥林匹亚」来纪年,这是他们唯一共用的纪年方式(各城邦的月名与岁首始终没统一)','Traditionally dated from 776 BC; Greeks later counted years by Olympiads, the one reckoning they all shared, though their month names and new years never agreed'],
     ['赛会期间宣布「神圣休战」,让各城邦的选手与观众能安全往返——不是停止战争,而是保证通行','A sacred truce was proclaimed so athletes and spectators could travel safely — not an end to war, but a guarantee of passage'],
     ['393年前后狄奥多西一世禁绝异教献祭,赛会此后逐渐停办;不过没有哪一道敕令点名禁过它,另一派认为真正的原因是地方财政不再供养','Theodosius I banned pagan sacrifice around 393 and the games faded out after that — though no edict names them, and another view holds that what really ended them was cities no longer paying for them']],
  s:['它把「各自为政的城邦其实同属一个文化」这件事变成了每四年一次的实物证明——共同的神、共同的语言、共同的纪年,而政治上从未统一。1896年顾拜旦在雅典重办,现代奥运会取的正是这个意思:比赛之外还有一层「暂时把敌意放下」的约定。古代赛会的竞技者只有男性自由民,但战车赛加冕的是马主而不是御者——斯巴达的基尼斯卡因此在前396与前392年两度夺冠,是奥林匹亚第一位女性优胜者。现代这一条早已不同。','It turned the fact that quarrelling city-states nevertheless shared one culture into something demonstrable every four years — the same gods, the same language, the same way of counting years, and no political unity whatever. When Coubertin revived it at Athens in 1896 that was exactly the point being borrowed: an agreement, alongside the contest, to set hostility down for a while. Only free men competed in the ancient games, but the chariot crown went to the owner rather than the driver — which is how Cynisca of Sparta won it twice, in 396 and 392 BC, the first woman victor at Olympia. The modern games long ago dropped the restriction.'] },
'圣索菲亚大教堂': { g:'obj', c:'拜占庭', y:[537,2025], n:['圣索菲亚大教堂','Hagia Sophia'],
  t:['一个直径三十多米的穹顶悬在四十扇窗上,底下的人看不出它靠什么撑着。','A dome more than thirty metres across floats on a ring of forty windows, and from below you cannot see what holds it up.'],
  a:[['532年动工、537年落成,只用了五年;不过今天看到的穹顶不是那一个——原穹顶558年在地震中坍塌,改成更陡、带肋的形式后于562年重建','Begun in 532 and finished in 537, just five years — though the dome overhead is not that one: the original fell in an earthquake in 558 and was rebuilt steeper and ribbed by 562'],
     ['穹顶底部一圈开窗,阳光进来在下面看像是浮着;这个做法此后被反复模仿','The ring of windows at the base of the dome lets in light that makes it seem to hover, and the trick has been copied ever since'],
     ['1453年改为清真寺,1935年改为博物馆,2020年再改回清真寺——同一栋建筑的身份换过三次','A mosque from 1453, a museum from 1935, and a mosque again from 2020 — the same building has changed identity three times']],
  s:['它是东罗马工程能力的顶点,也是此后一千年里所有大穹顶的参照物:奥斯曼的建筑师锡南一生都在回应它,伊斯坦布尔那些著名的清真寺是与它的对话。它同时是一件难题的实物:一栋建筑同时属于两种宗教的历史,归属之争至今未止。1985年作为伊斯坦布尔历史区的一部分列入世界遗产。','It is the summit of what East Roman engineering could do, and the reference point for every great dome for a thousand years after: the Ottoman architect Sinan spent his career answering it, and the famous mosques of Istanbul are a conversation with it. It is also a hard problem made of stone — one building belonging to the history of two religions, and the argument over it has not ended. It has been part of a World Heritage site since 1985.'] },
'大学': { g:'inst', c:'中世纪西欧', y:[1088,1500], n:['大学','The university'],
  t:['师生自己结成的行会:自定课程、自授学位、自己管自己。','A guild formed by teachers and students: it set its own curriculum, granted its own degrees, and governed itself.'],
  a:[['博洛尼亚(传统上定为1088年)、巴黎、牛津相继出现,拉丁文 universitas 本义就是「行会、共同体」','Bologna, traditionally dated 1088, then Paris and Oxford; the Latin universitas simply meant a guild or corporation'],
     ['关键的一步是「学位」:一份由这个共同体签发、在别处也认的资格证明,老师因此可以跨城任教','The decisive invention was the degree: a qualification issued by the corporation and recognised elsewhere, which let a master teach in another city'],
     ['它有自治特权与自己的法庭,与所在城市时常冲突——1229年巴黎的师生曾集体罢课出走两年','It held privileges and courts of its own and clashed with its host cities; in 1229 the masters and students of Paris walked out for two years']],
  s:['把它说成「世界上第一所大学」并不准确:更早的高等教育机构不少,摩洛哥的卡鲁因(859年)与开罗的爱资哈尔都比它久远。欧洲这一套的特别之处在于法人结构——一个不依附于某位老师、某座寺院或某个国王而独立存在的团体,人换了它还在。正是这一点让它能连续运转近千年,并被后来的整个世界照抄。','Calling it the first university in the world is not accurate: older institutions of higher learning exist, and al-Qarawiyyin in Morocco (859) and al-Azhar in Cairo are both older. What was distinctive about the European form is its corporate structure — a body that exists independently of any particular teacher, monastery or king, and survives the replacement of all its members. That is what let it run for the better part of a thousand years, and what the rest of the world later copied.'] },
'透视法': { g:'tech', c:'文艺复兴意大利', y:[1413,1600], n:['线性透视法','Linear perspective'],
  t:['向正前方远去的线都交在一个点上——画面因此有了深度。','The lines running straight away from you all meet at a single point, and the picture acquires depth.'],
  a:[['约1413年布鲁内莱斯基在佛罗伦萨做了一次公开演示:在木板上画洗礼堂,板上钻孔,观者从背面透过孔看镜中的画,与真实景象重合','Around 1413 Brunelleschi staged a demonstration in Florence: he painted the baptistery on a panel, bored a hole through it, and had viewers look through from behind at the painting in a mirror, matching the real building'],
     ['1435年阿尔贝蒂写成《论绘画》,把这套做法写成可教的规则——从此它不再是某个人的诀窍','In 1435 Alberti wrote On Painting and turned the practice into teachable rules, so that it stopped being one man\'s trick'],
     ['它本质上是几何:定一条地平线、一个灭点,其余按比例推——所以画家在这个时期开始学数学','It is geometry underneath: fix a horizon and a vanishing point and the rest follows by proportion — which is why painters in this period began learning mathematics']],
  s:['它改变的不只是画得像不像,而是画面与观看者的关系:透视法假定有一个站在特定位置的人在看,整幅画是为那一双眼睛组织的。这个「唯一视点」的想法后来渗进制图、舞台设计、建筑图纸,直到今天的三维渲染仍在用同一套几何。它也有代价——中国与波斯绘画走的是另一条路,不锁定视点,散点构图能在一幅长卷里装下一整条河,那是另一种取舍,不是没学会。','What it changed is not only how convincing a picture looks but the relation between picture and viewer: perspective assumes someone standing in one particular place, and organises everything for that single pair of eyes. The idea of one fixed viewpoint went on into mapmaking, stage design and architectural drawing, and the same geometry is still running inside three-dimensional rendering today. It had a cost — Chinese and Persian painting did not fix the viewpoint at all, and a scattered-point handscroll can hold an entire river. That is a different trade-off, not a failure to learn.'] },
'驿站系统': { g:'inst', c:'蒙古帝国', y:[1234,1368], n:['站赤(驿站系统)','The yam post system'],
  t:['每隔一段路设一站,备好马与草料——换马不换人,消息就能跑得比军队快。','A station every stage of the road, stocked with horses and fodder: change mounts, not riders, and news outruns armies.'],
  a:[['1234年前后窝阔台把它制度化:沿主要道路每三四十到六十公里设一站,视地形而定,备马匹、食宿与向导','Ögedei systematised it around 1234: a station every thirty to sixty kilometres along the main roads, depending on the ground, with horses, food, lodging and guides'],
     ['凭「牌子」(蒙语 gerege)通行,牌上刻着可调用的资源等级,没有牌子的人不能使用','Travel required a paiza, an inscribed tablet stating what you were entitled to draw; without one you could not use the system'],
     ['马可·波罗之前,柏朗嘉宾与鲁不鲁乞已亲身乘驿站往返并写进报告;马可·波罗的记述最详细、传播也最广','Carpini and Rubruck had already ridden the relays and written them up before Marco Polo came; his account is the fullest and by far the most widely read']],
  s:['蒙古帝国大到没有任何一支军队能守住全境,它能维持统治靠的是「消息比敌人快」——驿站是这套逻辑的物理实现。它同时也是商路与情报网:元朝的疆域管理、后来俄国的驿马制度、乃至清代的驿站,都在同一条思路上。副作用同样真实:沿线百姓要负担供养,史料里对此抱怨甚多。','The Mongol empire was too large for any army to hold everywhere, and what held it together was that news moved faster than enemies — the post stations were that idea made physical. They doubled as trade route and intelligence network, and the same thinking runs on into Yuan administration, the later Russian post horses and the Qing courier stations. The cost was just as real: the households along the roads had to feed and supply it, and the sources are full of their complaints.'] },
'兀鲁伯天文台': { g:'obj', c:'帖木儿帝国', y:[1420,1449], n:['兀鲁伯天文台','The observatory of Ulugh Beg'],
  t:['一个帖木儿家的王子把半座山挖开,埋进一具巨大的量角器,用它量星星。','A Timurid prince cut into a hillside to bed an enormous protractor, and used it to measure the stars.'],
  a:[['1420年代建于撒马尔罕,核心是一段半径约四十米的巨型石制六分仪,一半埋在地下以保持稳定','Built at Samarkand in the 1420s around a stone sextant of some forty metres\' radius, half of it sunk into the ground to hold steady'],
     ['1437年编成《新古拉干历数书》,列出一千余颗恒星的位置,精度在望远镜发明前少有能及','The star tables of 1437 give positions for more than a thousand stars, at an accuracy hard to match before the telescope'],
     ['兀鲁伯本人1449年被自己的儿子杀害,天文台随后被毁,直到1908年才被重新发掘出来','Ulugh Beg was killed by his own son in 1449 and the observatory was destroyed soon after; it was dug up again only in 1908']],
  s:['他既是统治者又是亲自计算的天文学家,这个组合在任何时代都罕见。星表经由伊斯坦布尔与阿拉伯文抄本西传,17世纪在牛津刊印,长期是欧洲天文学家的参照数据之一。它也是一个提醒:这类工作依赖的不只是才智,还有一个愿意为它掏钱的政权——政权一变,四十米的象限仪也保不住。','He was both the ruler and the man doing the arithmetic, a combination rare in any century. His tables travelled west through Istanbul and in Arabic manuscript, were printed at Oxford in the seventeenth century, and long served European astronomers as a reference. It is also a reminder that work like this rests on more than talent: it needs a state willing to pay for it, and when the state changed hands, not even a forty-metre quadrant survived.'] },
'《医典》': { g:'idea', c:'萨曼王朝', y:[1025,1650], n:['《医典》','The Canon of Medicine'],
  t:['伊本·西那把当时能找到的医学知识整理成一部书,十二世纪译成拉丁文后,欧洲的医学生读了它四百多年。','Ibn Sina gathered the medicine of his day into one book, and once it was put into Latin in the twelfth century, European medical students read it for over four hundred years.'],
  a:[['约1025年成书,五卷,把希腊的盖伦、印度与波斯的医方和他自己的临床观察编成一个可检索的体系','Finished around 1025 in five books, welding Galen, Indian and Persian practice and his own clinical observation into one searchable system'],
     ['它的长处是结构:分门别类、逐症对应,让人能查而不是从头读——这是教科书区别于文集的地方','Its strength is its structure: classified and indexed by condition, so it could be consulted rather than read through — which is what makes a textbook rather than an anthology'],
     ['12世纪由克雷莫纳的杰拉德译成拉丁文,此后长期是欧洲大学医科的标准教材,直到17世纪仍在重印','Translated into Latin by Gerard of Cremona in the twelfth century, it became the standard medical text in European universities and was still being reprinted in the seventeenth']],
  s:['它的影响不在于每条结论都对——很多是错的,后来被解剖学与实验一条条推翻——而在于它示范了「把散落的知识整理成可以传授的体系」这件事本身。中世纪的知识传递靠抄本,一部结构清晰的书比一百篇散论更容易活下来,这也是它压过同代著作的原因。伊本·西那在欧洲以拉丁名 Avicenna 为人所知。','Its importance is not that every conclusion in it was right — many were wrong, and anatomy and experiment overturned them one by one — but that it demonstrated the act of organising scattered knowledge into something teachable. Knowledge travelled by copied manuscript, and one well-structured book survives more easily than a hundred loose treatises, which is why it outlasted its contemporaries. Ibn Sina is known in Europe by his Latin name, Avicenna.'] },
'楔形文字': { g:'tech', c:'苏美尔·阿卡德', y:[-3300,75], n:['楔形文字','Cuneiform'],
  t:['用一根削尖的芦苇在软泥板上按出楔形的印子——已知最早的成体系文字之一。','Wedge-shaped marks pressed into soft clay with a cut reed — among the earliest systems of writing known.'],
  a:[['约公元前3300年出现在乌鲁克,最初是记账用的符号:几只羊、几袋大麦','It appears at Uruk around 3300 BC, at first as accounting marks — so many sheep, so many sacks of barley'],
     ['泥板便宜、干了极耐久,还烧不坏——两河的城市烧毁时,反倒把档案烤成了陶,所以留下来的比纸多得多','Clay was cheap, lasted once dry, and fire did not destroy it — when a Mesopotamian city burned, its archives were baked hard, which is why far more survives than from any paper culture'],
     ['它先后被用来写苏美尔语、阿卡德语、赫梯语、埃兰语与古波斯语——不同语言共用一套符号系统','It was used in turn for Sumerian, Akkadian, Hittite, Elamite and Old Persian — different languages sharing one set of signs']],
  s:['19世纪靠贝希斯敦铭文释读成功——那块崖刻用三种语言写同一件事,与罗塞塔石碑的作用相同。释读的结果是把两河流域从《圣经》里的地名变成了有账本、有诉状、有情书、有学生作业的具体社会。已知最晚的一块楔形文字泥板写于公元75年,是一份天文记录:这套文字用了三千多年才退场。','It was cracked in the nineteenth century through the Behistun inscription, a cliff carving giving the same text in three languages — the Rosetta Stone of Mesopotamia. What decipherment produced was a society with ledgers, lawsuits, love letters and schoolboys\' exercises, where before there had been place names in the Bible. The latest dated cuneiform tablet known was written in AD 75, an astronomical record: the script had lasted more than three thousand years.'] },
'金字塔': { g:'obj', c:'古埃及', y:[-2670,-1800], n:['金字塔','The pyramids'],
  t:['给国王造的坟,大到此后三千八百年里没有更高的人造物。','Tombs for kings, and so tall that nothing built by people passed them for the next three thousand eight hundred years.'],
  a:[['约公元前2670年,伊姆霍特普为左塞王建阶梯金字塔,是从阶梯到方锥的起点','Around 2670 BC Imhotep built the step pyramid for Djoser — the beginning of the road from stairway to true pyramid'],
     ['吉萨的胡夫金字塔约建于公元前2560年,原高约一百四十七米,用石块二百三十万块上下','The Great Pyramid of Khufu at Giza, built about 2560 BC, stood some 147 metres and took on the order of 2.3 million blocks'],
     ['修建者不是奴隶:吉萨旁的工匠村留下了面包坊、接骨愈合的伤者,大金字塔的减压室里还有红漆写的工作队名号,是有组织、有供养的劳役','The builders were not slaves: the workers\' village beside Giza has yielded bakeries and healed fractures, and the relieving chambers of the Great Pyramid carry gang names daubed in red — organised, fed, conscripted labour']],
  s:['它把「一个社会能把多少剩余投进一件不生产任何东西的事」推到了极限,而这件事本身就是国家能力的证明:采石、运输、测量、供养上万人几十年,任何一环断了都建不成。金字塔的时代其实很短,新王国起埃及改葬帝王谷的岩窟墓——因为金字塔太显眼,盗墓者也知道它在哪。','It pushed to the limit the question of how much surplus a society can put into something that produces nothing — and the doing of it is itself the proof of state capacity: quarrying, hauling, surveying and feeding tens of thousands for decades, with no link in the chain allowed to fail. The age of pyramids was actually short; from the New Kingdom on, Egyptian kings were cut into the rock of the Valley of the Kings instead — a pyramid is visible from far away, and grave robbers could see it too.'] },
'《汉谟拉比法典》': { g:'inst', c:'古巴比伦', y:[-1755,-1750], n:['《汉谟拉比法典》','The Code of Hammurabi'],
  t:['把二百八十二条判例刻在一块两米多高的黑石头上,立在神庙里给人看。','Two hundred and eighty-two rulings cut into a block of black stone over two metres high, set up in a temple where people could see it.'],
  a:[['约公元前1754年立,玄武岩石柱,顶部浮雕是太阳神沙玛什把权杖交给汉谟拉比——法的来源被说成是神','Set up around 1754 BC on a basalt stele whose relief shows the sun god handing Hammurabi the rod — the law is presented as coming from a god'],
     ['以「以眼还眼」著称,但刑罚按身份分等:同一件事,伤害贵族、平民与奴隶的代价完全不同','Famous for an eye for an eye, but the penalties are graded by rank: the same act cost quite different amounts depending on whether the victim was noble, commoner or slave'],
     ['1901年在苏萨出土——那里不是巴比伦,是公元前12世纪埃兰人把它当战利品掳走的地方','It was dug up at Susa in 1901 — not a Babylonian city, but where Elamite raiders carried it off as loot in the twelfth century BC']],
  s:['它不是最早的法典(更早的乌尔纳姆法典已有残篇),重要的是它被公开刻在石头上:规则脱离了执法者的口头裁断,变成任何人都能指着说「上面是这么写的」的东西。这一步与罗马的《十二铜表法》是同一回事,相隔一千三百年、彼此并无关系,却都从「把规则公示出来」开始。石柱现藏卢浮宫。','It is not the earliest law code — fragments of Ur-Nammu\'s are older — and what matters is that it was cut in stone in public: the rules came out of the enforcer\'s mouth and became something anyone could point at and say, it says so there. That step is the same one Rome took with the Twelve Tables thirteen centuries later and quite independently, and both began by putting the rules where they could be seen. The stele is in the Louvre.'] },
'22个字母': { g:'tech', c:'腓尼基', y:[-1050,-300], n:['腓尼基字母','The Phoenician alphabet'],
  t:['二十二个符号,一个音一个字母——识字不再需要背几千个字。','Twenty-two signs, one letter to a sound: literacy stopped requiring thousands of characters.'],
  a:[['约公元前11世纪定型,只有辅音、没有元音,读的时候靠语境补','It settled into shape around the eleventh century BC with consonants only and no vowels, which the reader supplied from context'],
     ['腓尼基人是商人,一套好学好写的记账符号对他们最有用——文字这次是被生意推着走的','The Phoenicians were traders, and what served them was a set of marks quick to learn and quick to write; this time writing was pushed along by business'],
     ['希腊人借去后把几个用不上的辅音改作元音,alpha、beta 就来自 aleph、bet——「字母表」这个词本身就是这么来的','The Greeks borrowed it and turned several unneeded consonants into vowels; alpha and beta come from aleph and bet, which is where the word alphabet itself comes from']],
  s:['今天世界上多数字母文字都能追到这里:希腊字母经伊特鲁里亚到拉丁字母(也就是本页英文用的这套),另一支经阿拉米字母到希伯来文与阿拉伯文。它改变的不是能写什么,而是谁能写:楔形文字与埃及圣书体都需要多年专门训练,而二十二个符号一个孩子几周就能学会——识字第一次有可能走出书吏阶层。','Most alphabetic scripts in the world today trace back to here: Greek through Etruscan into the Latin letters this page\'s English is set in, and another line through Aramaic into Hebrew and Arabic. What it changed was not what could be written but who could write: cuneiform and Egyptian hieroglyphs took years of training, while twenty-two signs can be learned by a child in weeks — and literacy could, for the first time, leave the scribal class.'] },
'代数': { g:'idea', c:'阿拉伯哈里发', y:[820,1600], n:['代数','Algebra'],
  t:['不再一题一解,而是把「未知数」当成一个可以搬来搬去的东西来处理。','Instead of solving each problem afresh, treat the unknown as a thing you can move around.'],
  a:[['约820年花剌子米在巴格达写成一部讲「还原与对消」的书,书名里的 al-jabr 就是 algebra 的来源','Around 820 al-Khwarizmi wrote a book in Baghdad on restoring and balancing; the al-jabr of its title is where the word algebra comes from'],
     ['他的办法是把方程整理成几种标准形式,再给每种形式一套通用解法——重点是「通用」','His method was to reduce equations to a handful of standard forms and give each form a general recipe — the point being that it was general'],
     ['他的名字经拉丁化成为 algorithmus,今天的 algorithm(算法)一词由此而来','His own name, Latinised as algorithmus, is where the word algorithm comes from']],
  s:['不解「这一道」而解「这一类」——这一步之后,数学处理的对象不再只是数,还包括「关于数的规则」本身。他的书12世纪译成拉丁文,与印度传来的十进制数字一起成为欧洲算学的底子。要说清楚的是:algebra 出自书名、algorithm 出自人名,这是两件事,常被混着讲。','Solving not this problem but this kind of problem — and after that step mathematics is working not only on numbers but on the rules about numbers. His book was translated into Latin in the twelfth century and, together with the decimal digits arriving from India, became the foundation of European calculation. Worth stating plainly: algebra comes from the book\'s title and algorithm from the man\'s name — two different things, often muddled together.'] },
'吴哥窟': { g:'obj', c:'高棉·吴哥', y:[1113,2025], n:['吴哥窟','Angkor Wat'],
  t:['一般认为是世界上占地最大的宗教建筑:先是毗湿奴神庙,后来改成佛寺,从没被真正废弃过。','Generally reckoned the largest religious monument in the world by area: first a Vishnu temple, later a Buddhist one, and never truly abandoned.'],
  a:[['约1113—1150年苏利耶跋摩二世在位期间建成,朝西——多数高棉神庙朝东,为什么它朝西至今有争论','Built under Suryavarman II, who reigned from about 1113 to 1150, and facing west, where most Khmer temples face east; why is still argued over'],
     ['砂岩垒成,石块之间几乎不用灰浆;外围护城河周长约五点五公里,浮雕沿着一圈187×215米的回廊铺开,总面积近千平方米','Sandstone laid almost without mortar; the moat around it runs some 5.5 km, and the reliefs run right around a gallery of 187 by 215 metres, close to a thousand square metres of carving'],
     ['高棉1431年放弃吴哥迁都,但这座寺一直有僧人住着——所谓19世纪「被发现」,是欧洲人的说法','The Khmer left Angkor for a new capital in 1431, yet monks went on living here; its nineteenth-century discovery was a European way of putting it']],
  s:['它是柬埔寨的国家象征,国旗中央画的就是它——以一座建筑物入国旗的国家极少。1992年列入世界遗产。近年的激光雷达测绘显示,与它同期的水利系统与城市范围远大于此前的认知:吴哥不只是一片神庙,而是一座靠水网组织起来的大城,而水网的失效可能正是它衰落的一环。','It is the national symbol of Cambodia and sits in the middle of the flag — very few countries put a building there. It became a World Heritage site in 1992. Recent lidar survey has shown that the waterworks and the settlement around it were far larger than anyone thought: Angkor was not a cluster of temples but a city organised around water, and the failure of that water system may be part of why it declined.'] },
'喃字': { g:'tech', c:'大越', y:[1200,1920], n:['喃字','Chữ Nôm'],
  t:['越南人用汉字的零件拼出自己的字,一个字往往一半表音、一半表意。','The Vietnamese built their own characters out of Chinese parts — typically half of one for sound, half for meaning.'],
  a:[['约13世纪成形:借汉字的偏旁组合,写出汉语里根本没有的越南语词','It took shape around the thirteenth century, combining Chinese components to write Vietnamese words that had no Chinese equivalent'],
     ['要读喃字得先认汉字,所以它始终是识字者的第二套字,没能普及到平民','Reading it required knowing Chinese characters first, so it stayed a second script for the already literate and never reached ordinary people'],
     ['阮攸用喃字写成的《金云翘传》被视为越南文学的顶峰,今天的越南人读的是拉丁字母转写本','Nguyen Du\'s Tale of Kieu, written in chữ Nôm, is held to be the summit of Vietnamese literature; Vietnamese today read it transcribed into the Latin alphabet']],
  s:['19世纪末起法国殖民当局先在南圻推行拉丁字母的「国语字」、1910年推广到北圻,喃字几十年内退出日常使用,今天能读的人极少。它留下的是一个提醒:借来的文字要装下自己的语言,总得先拆开重组。朝鲜的吏读、日本的万叶假名做的是同一件事,而三家走向了不同结局——日本演化出假名,朝鲜另造了训民正音,越南换成了字母。','From the late nineteenth century the French administration pushed a Latin-based script, first in the south and from 1910 in the north, and within decades chữ Nôm dropped out of daily use; very few can read it now. What it leaves behind is a reminder: a borrowed script has to be taken apart and rebuilt before it will hold your own language. Korea\'s idu and Japan\'s man\'yōgana were doing the same thing, and the three ended differently — Japan evolved the kana, Korea made a new alphabet outright, and Vietnam swapped in Latin letters.'] },
'石柱敕令': { g:'obj', c:'孔雀王朝', y:[-257,-232], n:['阿育王石柱敕令','The Ashokan edicts'],
  t:['阿育王把自己的诏令刻在石柱和岩壁上,让路过的人都能读到——包括「我后悔了」这一句。','Ashoka had his decrees cut into pillars and rock faces where anyone passing could read them — including the one that says he was sorry.'],
  a:[['约公元前3世纪中期刻成,分布在今印度、尼泊尔、巴基斯坦、阿富汗,用当地通行的语言与文字写','Cut around the middle of the third century BC and spread across what is now India, Nepal, Pakistan and Afghanistan, each in the local language and script'],
     ['石柱高十余米、重数十吨,柱身是一整块石头凿出来、打磨得能反光,顶上的狮子柱头是另一块石头,用金属榫接上去','The pillars stand over ten metres and weigh tens of tonnes; the shaft is one piece of stone polished until it shines, and the lion capital on top is a second stone, pinned on with a metal dowel'],
     ['第十三号岩刻里他写下:征服羯陵伽后见到死伤,深感悔恨,此后改行「法」(dhamma)——这是极罕见的、统治者自述后悔的官方文本','In the thirteenth rock edict he records that the slaughter after conquering Kalinga filled him with remorse, and that he turned thereafter to dhamma — an official text in which a ruler says he regrets what he did, which is very rare']],
  s:['这批石刻是印度最早的、能确切断代的成篇文字记录,阿育王本人也是靠它们才从传说回到信史:19世纪普林塞普释读了这种婆罗米文字,人们才知道那些自称「天爱喜见王」的诏令出自谁。萨尔纳特石柱顶端的四狮柱头是今天印度的国徽,柱头下方的法轮画在国旗正中——一个公元前3世纪的物件,直接变成了现代国家的标志。','These are the earliest securely dated body of writing from India, and Ashoka himself only came back from legend into history through them: it was Prinsep\'s decipherment of the Brahmi script in the nineteenth century that revealed who the king who called himself Beloved-of-the-Gods and Of-Gracious-Mien had been. The four-lion capital from the pillar at Sarnath is now the state emblem of India, and the wheel beneath it sits at the centre of the flag — a third-century-BC object turned straight into the insignia of a modern country.'] },
'"零"与十进制': { g:'idea', c:'笈多王朝', y:[400,1200], n:['「零」与十进制','Zero and the decimal system'],
  t:['把「什么都没有」也写成一个数字,于是十个符号就够写出所有的数。','Write down nothing as a number of its own, and ten symbols become enough to write every number there is.'],
  a:[['先是用一个点或小圈标出空位,再进一步把它当成能参与运算的数——628年婆罗摩笈多写下了零的加减乘规则(除法他也试了,但那一条是错的)','First a dot or small circle marked an empty place, then it became a number you could calculate with: in 628 Brahmagupta set down rules for adding, subtracting and multiplying with zero — he tried division too, and got that one wrong'],
     ['与它配套的是位值制:同一个符号摆在不同位置代表不同大小,所以不必为每个数量另造符号','Its other half is place value: the same symbol means different amounts depending on where it sits, so you never need a new symbol for a bigger number'],
     ['经阿拉伯世界西传,欧洲称之为「阿拉伯数字」,而阿拉伯人自己称之为「印度数字」','It reached Europe through the Arab world, which is why Europeans call the digits Arabic — while the Arabs themselves called them Indian']],
  s:['有了零和位值,竖式计算才成立,代数与此后的一切计算才变得可行:拿罗马数字做一次乘法就知道差别有多大。玛雅人也独立用过零,但那一套没有传出中美洲。欧洲接受它花了几百年——斐波那契1202年的《计算之书》大力推荐,而一些城市一度以防伪为由禁止商人使用,直到15世纪才真正普及。','With zero and place value you can do arithmetic in columns, and algebra and everything computational after it becomes possible: try one multiplication in Roman numerals and the difference is obvious. The Maya used a zero independently, but that system never left Mesoamerica. Europe took centuries to accept it — Fibonacci\'s book of 1202 argued hard for it, some cities banned merchants from using it for fear of forgery, and it only became normal in the fifteenth century.'] },
'泰姬陵': { g:'obj', c:'莫卧儿帝国', y:[1632,1653], n:['泰姬陵','The Taj Mahal'],
  t:['沙贾汗为亡妻建的陵墓,建了二十多年,白色大理石会随一天里的光线变色。','A tomb the emperor Shah Jahan built for his dead wife over two decades; its white marble changes colour with the light through the day.'],
  a:[['1632年动工、约1653年完成,为1631年难产去世的王后穆姆塔兹·玛哈尔而建','Begun in 1632 and finished around 1653, for the empress Mumtaz Mahal, who died in childbirth in 1631'],
     ['主体白色大理石,嵌着来自多地的宝石;四座宣礼塔略向外倾斜,一般认为是为了地震时不倒向主体','White marble inlaid with stones brought from many places; the four minarets lean very slightly outward, generally thought so that an earthquake would topple them away from the tomb'],
     ['「沙贾汗还想在河对岸建一座黑色的」流传极广,唯一的来源是1665年到过阿格拉的一位法国旅行家;而河对岸挖出来的是一座花园,所谓黑色大理石遗迹其实是变了色的白石','The story that Shah Jahan meant to build a black twin across the river is widely told; its only source is a French traveller who visited Agra in 1665, and what was actually dug up across the river is a garden — the supposed black marble turned out to be discoloured white']],
  s:['它把波斯的陵墓形制、印度的石作工艺与伊斯兰的几何装饰合成了一种新东西,通常被视为印度-伊斯兰建筑的顶点。1983年列入世界遗产,今天是印度最主要的旅游收入来源之一,也因此长期要对付空气污染让大理石泛黄的问题。沙贾汗晚年被儿子奥朗则布囚禁在阿格拉堡,据说从那里的窗口能望见这座陵。','It fuses a Persian tomb plan, Indian stonework and Islamic geometry into something new, and is usually taken as the high point of Indo-Islamic architecture. A World Heritage site since 1983, it is one of India\'s largest sources of tourist revenue, and for the same reason has spent decades fighting the way air pollution yellows its marble. Shah Jahan spent his last years imprisoned by his son Aurangzeb in the fort at Agra, from whose windows, it is said, the tomb can be seen.'] },
'犍陀罗佛像艺术': { g:'obj', c:'贵霜帝国', y:[50,400], n:['犍陀罗佛像艺术','Gandhara Buddhist sculpture'],
  t:['佛开始有了人的样子——而那张脸上带着希腊雕像的影子。','This is where the Buddha began to be shown as a person — and the face carries an echo of Greek sculpture.'],
  a:[['一般认为早期佛教不造佛像,而用足印、法轮、菩提树代表佛;公元1—2世纪起才出现人形的佛','Early Buddhism is generally held not to have depicted the Buddha at all, using a footprint, a wheel or the bodhi tree instead; human figures appear only from the first and second centuries AD'],
     ['犍陀罗(今巴基斯坦西北与阿富汗东部)在亚历山大东征后长期处在希腊化影响下,佛像的卷发、衣褶与面部处理都用了希腊雕塑的办法','Gandhara — today\'s northwest Pakistan and eastern Afghanistan — lay for centuries under Greek influence after Alexander, and the wavy hair, the folds of cloth and the modelling of the face are all done in Greek ways'],
     ['究竟是犍陀罗先造出人形佛像,还是南边的秣菟罗先造,学界至今没有定论——两地风格差别很大,可能各自发展','Whether Gandhara or Mathura to the south made the first human Buddha is still unsettled; the two styles differ so much that they may have developed separately']],
  s:['贵霜横跨中亚与北印度,这批造像沿丝路东传,经于阗、敦煌进入中国,再到朝鲜与日本——东亚寺庙里佛像的样子,源头能一路追到这里。它是一次看得见的跨文明合成:希腊的造型手法、印度的宗教内容、中亚的商路,三者缺一不可,而这三样凑在一起只是因为一个帝国恰好同时压着这几片地方。','The Kushans straddled Central Asia and northern India, and these images travelled east along the Silk Road through Khotan and Dunhuang into China, then to Korea and Japan — the look of a Buddha in an East Asian temple traces back to here. It is a fusion you can see with your eyes: Greek modelling, Indian religion, Central Asian trade routes, none of the three dispensable — and the three met only because one empire happened to sit on all of them at once.'] },
'甲骨文': { g:'tech', c:'商', y:[-1250,-1046], n:['甲骨文','Oracle-bone script'],
  t:['刻在龟甲与牛肩胛骨上的字,是商王问神的记录——也是汉字能一路读到今天的起点。','Characters cut into turtle shells and ox shoulder blades, recording what Shang kings asked of their gods — and the beginning of a script still read today.'],
  a:[['商王遇事先占卜:把要问的事刻在甲骨上,烧灼取兆,再把应验与否刻上去,所以留下来的是一份份问答记录','Shang kings divined before acting: the question was cut into the bone, heat was applied to crack it, and the outcome was cut in afterwards — so what survives is a run of questions and answers'],
     ['1899年王懿荣最早认出这些「龙骨」上刻的是文字(药铺买药时偶然发现的说法流传极广,但出自后人追述);此后在安阳殷墟陆续出土逾十五万片','In 1899 Wang Yirong was the first to recognise that these dragon bones carried writing — the much-repeated story that he spotted it in a pharmacy is a later retelling — and more than 150,000 pieces have since come out of the ruins at Anyang'],
     ['已发现的单字约四千多个,其中已释读的约三分之一,真正无争议的更少——大量人名地名至今读不出来','Some four thousand distinct characters have been found and roughly a third have been identified, fewer still beyond dispute; a great many names of people and places still cannot be deciphered']],
  s:['它把商朝从传说变成了可查的历史:此前《史记》所记的商王世系无从验证,而甲骨上的先王名号与之大体吻合。字形上今天的汉字与它一脉相承——「日」「月」「水」「火」这些字,三千年前的写法与现在仍能对上,这是小孩最容易亲眼看出的一条连续性。2017年甲骨文列入《世界记忆名录》。','It turned the Shang from legend into checkable history: the king list in the Records of the Grand Historian had no independent confirmation until the ancestral names on the bones broadly matched it. And the shapes carry straight through to today — sun, moon, water, fire are still recognisably the same characters after three thousand years, which is the easiest continuity for a child to see with their own eyes. The bones were added to the Memory of the World register in 2017.'] },
'大运河': { g:'tech', c:'隋', y:[605,1855], n:['大运河','The Grand Canal'],
  t:['六年里挖通五大水系,让南方的粮食能一路运到北方的都城。','Five river systems joined in six years, so that grain from the south could reach the capital in the north by water.'],
  a:[['605年起分段开凿,以洛阳为中心,南到余杭、北抵涿郡,全长约两千七百公里','Digging began in 605 in sections, centred on Luoyang and running from Hangzhou in the south to near Beijing in the north — some 2,700 km in all'],
     ['它不是从零挖起:把春秋以来各段旧渠连缀、疏浚、取直,再补上中间缺口','It was not dug from nothing: older canals going back centuries were linked, dredged, straightened and the gaps between them filled in'],
     ['代价极大——短期内征发民夫数百万,是隋朝二世而亡的原因之一','The cost was enormous: millions were conscripted to dig in a few years, one of the reasons the Sui fell in its second reign']],
  s:['南方产粮、北方驻军建都,这个矛盾靠一条水道解决了一千三百年:唐宋的漕运、元明清的漕粮都走它。元朝把河道取直改经山东、不再绕行洛阳,今天说的「京杭大运河」是那一次改线的结果。1855年黄河改道冲断山东段,海运(1826年起已试行)从此取代漕河成为主路。2014年列入世界遗产,南段至今通航。','Grain grew in the south while armies and capitals sat in the north, and one waterway held that contradiction together for thirteen centuries — the Tang, Song, Yuan, Ming and Qing all shipped their tax grain along it. The Yuan straightened the route through Shandong and stopped detouring via Luoyang, which is why the canal is now called the Beijing–Hangzhou Grand Canal. When the Yellow River shifted course in 1855 it cut the Shandong section, and sea transport — tried since 1826 — took over as the main route. It became a World Heritage site in 2014, and the southern reaches still carry boats.'] },
'交子': { g:'inst', c:'北宋', y:[1023,1160], n:['交子(纸币)','Jiaozi (paper money)'],
  t:['一般认为是世界上最早由官府发行的纸币,出现在四川。','Generally taken to be the earliest paper money issued by a government, and it appeared in Sichuan.'],
  a:[['四川用铁钱,重而不便——买一匹布要背上百斤钱,商人于是互相开具可兑换的凭票,这就是交子的由来','Sichuan used iron coin, heavy and awkward: a bolt of cloth cost tens of kilos of it, so merchants began writing each other redeemable notes — and that is where jiaozi came from'],
     ['1023年官府设「益州交子务」接管发行,定下面额、准备金与三年一换的期限','In 1023 the state set up a bureau at Yizhou to take the issue over, fixing denominations, a reserve, and a three-year term after which notes were exchanged'],
     ['后来财政吃紧、发行量失控,交子贬值;南宋改发「会子」,走了同样一条路','Later, under fiscal pressure, the issue ran out of control and the notes lost value; the Southern Song replaced them with huizi, which went the same way']],
  s:['纸币的逻辑在这里第一次被完整地试过一遍:它省下的是搬运成本,靠的是发行方的信用,而信用一旦被财政透支就会崩——这三句话此后在元朝的交钞、明朝的大明宝钞上原样重演。马可·波罗在元朝见到纸币时大为惊讶,他的记述是欧洲人最早听说这件事的渠道之一;欧洲自己发行纸币要再等几百年。','The whole logic of paper money was worked through here for the first time: it saves the cost of carrying value, it runs on the issuer\'s credit, and it collapses once that credit is spent on the deficit — three sentences that played out again, unchanged, with the Yuan and Ming issues. Marco Polo was astonished to find paper money in Yuan China, and his account is one of the first ways Europeans heard of the idea; Europe would not issue its own for centuries.'] },
'活字印刷': { g:'tech', c:'北宋', y:[1041,1450], n:['活字印刷','Movable type'],
  t:['一个字一个印,排完一版拆开还能再用——毕昇用胶泥做的。','One character, one piece of type: take the page apart when you are done and use them again. Bi Sheng made his out of clay.'],
  a:[['约1041—1048年间,布衣毕昇以胶泥刻字、火烧使硬,排在铁板上用松脂固定,印完加热取下','Between about 1041 and 1048 Bi Sheng, a commoner, cut characters in clay and fired them hard, set them on an iron plate in pine resin, and warmed the plate to release them afterwards'],
     ['这件事几乎只见于沈括《梦溪笔谈》的记载;此外他留下的只有一方1990年在湖北英山出土、被鉴定为宋代的墓碑','Almost the whole account rests on one passage in Shen Kuo\'s Dream Pool Essays; the only other thing he left is a gravestone unearthed in Hubei in 1990 and dated to the Song'],
     ['汉字数量太多,拆排一版的成本未必低于雕版,所以中国此后仍以雕版为主','With so many characters to sort, setting a page was not obviously cheaper than cutting a woodblock, and China went on printing mostly from blocks']],
  s:['「可以重排」这个想法本身是印刷史的分水岭,而它在哪种文字里最省力,决定了它在哪里铺开:朝鲜13世纪已用金属活字,现存最早的金属活字印本《直指》印于1377年,早于古腾堡约七十年;字母只有几十个的欧洲,活字的成本优势最大。古腾堡1450年前后的做法与东方有没有关联,至今没有直接证据,学界看法不一,多数倾向于各自独立想到——但两边解决的是同一个问题。','The idea that a page can be re-set is the watershed in printing history, and where it spread was decided by which scripts it saved the most work in. Korea was using metal type in the thirteenth century, and the Jikji of 1377 — the oldest surviving book printed from metal type — predates Gutenberg by about seventy years. In Europe, with a few dozen letters, the saving was greatest of all. Whether Gutenberg around 1450 owed anything to the East is unproven, and scholars are divided, most leaning toward independent invention — but both were answers to the same problem.'] },
'训民正音': { g:'tech', c:'朝鲜半岛诸王朝', y:[1443,2025], n:['训民正音(韩文)','Hunminjeongeum, the Korean alphabet'],
  t:['世宗大王为不识汉字的百姓造了一套字,照嘴的形状画。','King Sejong made an alphabet for the ordinary people who could not read Chinese characters — its letters drawn from the shape of the mouth.'],
  a:[['1443年制成、1446年颁行,书名就叫《训民正音》,意思是「教百姓正确的字音」','Completed in 1443 and promulgated in 1446 under a title meaning the correct sounds for the instruction of the people'],
     ['辅音的字形照发音时舌与唇的样子造,元音用天、地、人三个符号组合——它是少数留下了设计说明的文字','Consonant shapes copy the tongue and lips in the act of speaking, and vowels are built from three marks standing for heaven, earth and man; few scripts anywhere come with an explanation of their own design'],
     ['士大夫长期抵制,称它为「谚文」(俗字),直到20世纪才真正成为正式书写系统','The scholar class resisted it for centuries as a vulgar script, and it became the standard way of writing only in the twentieth century']],
  s:['世界上多数文字是长期演化出来的,说不清谁在哪一年造了它;而这一套有确切的年份、明确的创制者、以及一份讲清楚为什么这样设计的文献——这在文字史上极其少见。设计目标也写得很直白:让识字这件事不再是少数人的特权。今天韩国把颁行日定为公休的「韩文日」,《训民正音》解例本1997年列入《世界记忆名录》。','Most writing systems grew by drift, with no way to say who made them or when; this one has a date, a named author, and a document explaining why each letter looks as it does — which is very rare. Its stated purpose was just as plain: to stop literacy being the privilege of a few. Korea now keeps the day of its promulgation as a public holiday, and the explanatory edition entered the Memory of the World register in 1997.'] },
'科举制': { g:'inst', c:'隋', y:[587,1905], n:['科举制','The examination system'],
  t:['用考试而不是出身来选官,这套办法在中国用了一千三百年。','Choosing officials by examination rather than birth — a method used in China for thirteen centuries.'],
  a:[['587年隋文帝下诏由各州分科举人,605年隋炀帝增设进士科;唐朝定型为常设科目,宋朝加上糊名与誊录,尽量让阅卷者认不出是谁的卷子','In 587 the first Sui emperor ordered the provinces to send up candidates by category, and in 605 the second added the jinshi degree; the Tang made it a regular institution, and the Song added sealed names and recopied papers so examiners could not tell whose work they were marking'],
     ['考的主要是儒家经典与文章,不考专业技能——它选的是「会治理的人」而不是「会做事的人」','What it tested was mainly the Confucian classics and composition, not technical skill: it selected men judged able to govern, not able to do'],
     ['1905年废除。此前一千三百年间,它是普通人家进入统治阶层几乎唯一的通道','Abolished in 1905; for the thirteen centuries before that it was almost the only route by which an ordinary family could enter the governing class']],
  s:['它把「谁来当官」这件事从血统手里拿走,交给了一场可以准备的考试——这在世界上出现得很早。朝鲜、越南都仿行过;18世纪起欧洲人通过传教士的记述了解到它,英国1855年建立文官考试制度时,当时的讨论里多次提到中国的例子。它也有代价:考试内容长期不变,读书人的精力被引向经典与文章,与技术、商业无关。','It took the question of who governs out of the hands of birth and gave it to an examination anyone could prepare for — very early by world standards. Korea and Vietnam adopted versions of it, and from the eighteenth century Europeans learned of it through missionary accounts; when Britain set up its civil service examinations in 1855 the Chinese example came up repeatedly in the debate. It had a price: the syllabus barely changed, and scholarly effort was drawn toward classics and composition, away from technology and trade.'] },
'造纸术': { g:'tech', c:'东汉', y:[105,1400], n:['造纸术','Papermaking'],
  t:['把树皮、麻头、破布煮烂、捞成薄片——知识从此变得便宜。','Bark, hemp and rags boiled down and lifted out as a thin sheet — and knowledge became cheap.'],
  a:[['105年蔡伦向朝廷进献改良的造纸法。纸此前已经存在,他的贡献是把原料换成便宜易得的东西并把工艺标准化','In 105 Cai Lun presented an improved method to the court; paper already existed, and his contribution was to switch to cheap, common materials and standardise the process'],
     ['取代了竹简与丝帛:一部书从「一车竹简」变成可以拿在手里','It replaced bamboo slips and silk — a book went from a cartload of slats to something you could hold'],
     ['传说751年怛罗斯之战的被俘工匠把它带到撒马尔罕,不过那一带此前已有纸,学界现在多认为是沿丝路慢慢传开的;此后经巴格达、大马士革到北非与西班牙,12世纪进入欧洲','By tradition prisoners taken at the battle of Talas in 751 carried it to Samarkand, though paper was already in use there and most scholars now think it spread gradually along the Silk Road; from there it went to Baghdad and Damascus, then North Africa and Spain, and Europe in the twelfth century']],
  s:['它降低的是记录与传播的成本,而这个成本一降,别的事情才可能发生:雕版与活字印刷、科举需要的大量书籍、伊斯兰世界的翻译运动、欧洲的宗教改革传单。中国用纸比欧洲早了一千年,这一千年里书籍的普及程度差距很大。今天的纸仍然是同一个原理:把植物纤维打散,再让它们重新交织成片。','What it lowered was the cost of recording and moving information — and once that cost fell, other things became possible: block printing and movable type, the mountains of books the examination system required, the translation movement in the Islamic world, the pamphlets of the European Reformation. China had paper a thousand years before Europe, and over that millennium the difference in how widely books circulated was very large. Paper today works on the same principle: break plant fibre apart, then let it knit back together as a sheet.'] },
'罗马法': { g:'idea', c:'罗马帝国', y:[-450,533], n:['罗马法','Roman law'],
  t:['先把规则写下来给所有人看——这件事本身就是它的起点。','It began with writing the rules down where everyone could see them.'],
  a:[['约公元前450年《十二铜表法》刻成公示,平民第一次能知道法官依据什么判案','Around 450 BC the Twelve Tables were inscribed and put on public display, so ordinary citizens could see on what basis judgments were made'],
     ['共和后期随着对外扩张发展出「万民法」——前242年起专设一名裁判官处理罗马人与外邦人之间的诉讼,逐渐提炼出不依赖身份的通用规则','As Rome expanded in the late Republic a law of nations grew up — from 242 BC a praetor was appointed specifically for suits between Romans and foreigners, and from it came general rules that did not depend on who you were'],
     ['528年查士丁尼下令编纂,529年《法典》颁行,533年《学说汇纂》与《法学阶梯》完成,千年积累的法条、判例与法学家意见被整理成一部《民法大全》','Justinian ordered the compilation in 528; the Code was issued in 529 and the Digest and Institutes completed in 533, gathering a thousand years of statutes, cases and jurists\' opinions into a single body of civil law']],
  s:['11世纪《民法大全》在意大利被重新发现,博洛尼亚大学以研究它起家,此后成为欧洲大陆法系的共同底本;今天大陆法系国家的民法结构、许多术语与原则仍可追到那里。「法律面前人人平等」「无罪推定」这类说法的来源比它复杂得多,但罗马法是把规则从习惯与个人裁断中剥离出来、写成条文并公开的最早尝试之一。','The compilation was rediscovered in Italy in the eleventh century, the University of Bologna grew up around studying it, and it became the common foundation of the civil-law tradition on the continent; the structure of civil codes today, and many of their terms and principles, still trace back to it. Ideas like equality before the law and the presumption of innocence have more tangled origins than this, but Roman law is among the earliest attempts to lift rules out of custom and personal judgment, write them down, and make them public.'] },
'司母戊鼎': { g:'obj', c:'商', y:[-1200], ca:1, yk:'m', n:['后母戊鼎(旧称司母戊鼎)','The Houmuwu ding'],
  t:['八百三十二公斤的一口大方鼎,是现存最大的先秦青铜器——而它是用来盛祭品的。','A rectangular cauldron weighing 832 kilograms, the largest surviving pre-Qin bronze — and it was made to hold an offering.'],
  a:[['1939年出土于河南安阳,当时为避免被日军掠走而重新埋回地下,抗战胜利后才起出','Excavated at Anyang in Henan in 1939 and reburied to keep it from being seized during the war, it was lifted again only after 1945'],
     ['用数十块陶范拼合一次浇铸,需要同时熔化一吨以上的铜液——这本身就是一次大规模的组织行动','Cast in one pour from dozens of assembled clay mould sections, which meant melting more than a tonne of metal at once — an act of organisation as much as of craft'],
     ['腹内壁铸有三字铭文,是商王为祭祀母亲所作;鼎的名字就来自这三个字,读法至今仍有「司」与「后」两说','Three characters are cast inside: a Shang king\'s dedication to his mother. The vessel is named after them, and whether the first should read si or hou is still argued']],
  s:['同时期的西亚与欧洲主要把青铜用于兵器和工具,商周却把冶金的最高水平投进了祭祀用器——这个选择本身就说明这个社会认为什么最重要。用几个鼎、几个簋对应什么身份,有严格规定,「问鼎中原」「一言九鼎」这类说法都从这套等级来。器上的铭文是双重遗产:既记录了青铜工艺,也是汉字早期形态的实物证据。','In West Asia and Europe at the same date bronze went mainly into weapons and tools; Shang and Zhou China put its highest metallurgy into vessels for sacrifice — and that choice says plainly what the society thought mattered most. How many cauldrons and food vessels a person might use was fixed by rank, and several Chinese idioms about legitimate authority come straight from that graded set. The cast inscriptions are a double bequest: a record of the craft, and physical evidence of an early stage of Chinese writing.'] },
'统一文字度量衡': { g:'inst', c:'秦', y:[-221,-210], n:['统一文字度量衡','One script, one set of measures'],
  t:['把全国的字、尺、斗、秤都改成同一套——这件事做完之后,两千年里再没有人推翻它。',
     'The script, the foot-rule, the grain measure and the balance all made one — and in two thousand years no one undid it.'],
  a:[['六国的字各写各的,一个「马」字有好几种写法。秦以本国的篆书为底整理出小篆,李斯等人编了字表当范本;公文与民间同时通行更好写的隶书,汉字后来走的是隶书这条路。',
      'Each of the six kingdoms wrote its own characters — the word for horse had several forms. Qin took its own seal script as the base and worked out the small seal, with Li Si and others compiling model lists; meanwhile the quicker clerical script was spreading, in official documents as well as everyday use, and it is from that, not the seal, that later Chinese writing descends.'],
     ['上海博物馆有一件商鞅方升:战国秦孝公十八年(前 344 年)商鞅监制的标准量器,容积二百零二毫升。一百二十多年后秦统一,又在它底部加刻了始皇的诏书——同一件量器上刻着两个时代的字,标准没换,只是重新宣布了一次。',
      'In the Shanghai Museum there is a bronze measure made under Shang Yang in 344 BC, holding 202 millilitres. A hundred and twenty years later, after unification, the First Emperor\'s edict was cut into its base as well — one vessel carrying the writing of two ages: the standard had not changed, only been proclaimed again.'],
     ['《史记》说「舆六尺」,后世常据此说车轨定为六尺(也有学者认为「舆」指车厢、不是轨距);文字统一让政令在方言不同的地方都能读懂。这两样是让一个横跨几千里的国家能被「管起来」的技术前提。',
      'The Shiji says the carriage box was six chi, from which the axle width is usually said to have been fixed at six — though some scholars read the word as the box itself rather than the gauge — and one script let an order be read where the spoken language differed. Together they were what made a realm thousands of li across administrable at all.']],
  s:['汉承秦制,这套标准也一并接手;此后每个王朝改元、改历、改官制,却极少动文字和度量衡的根本。今天中国人能直接读两千年前的碑刻,与这次统一直接有关——同一时期的地中海世界,拉丁语后来分化成了好几种语言。',
     'The Han took the Qin system over whole, and while later dynasties changed reign titles, calendars and offices, they rarely touched the basis of the script or the measures. That a Chinese reader today can read an inscription two thousand years old follows directly from this — in the Mediterranean of the same centuries, Latin went on to split into several languages.'] },

'郡县制': { g:'inst', c:'秦', y:[-221,-207], n:['郡县制','Commanderies and counties'],
  t:['官员由皇帝任免、不许世袭——这一条把「封建」换成了「官僚」,后来两千年都照这个来。',
     'Officials appointed and dismissed by the emperor, and no inheriting the post — the switch from fiefs to bureaucracy that lasted two thousand years.'],
  a:[['统一那年,丞相王绾建议把皇子分封到燕、齐、楚故地,李斯反对,理由是周朝分封的诸侯几代之后就互相打起来了。始皇采纳李斯,全国设三十六郡,后来增至四十余郡。',
      'In the year of unification the chancellor Wang Wan proposed giving the princes fiefs in the old lands of Yan, Qi and Chu; Li Si objected that the Zhou\'s enfeoffed lords had fallen to fighting each other within a few generations. The First Emperor took Li Si\'s side: thirty-six commanderies at first, later over forty.'],
     ['郡设守(民政)、尉(军事)、监(监察)三个长官,互不统属;县以下有乡、里。所有人由中央任免、领俸禄,不能把位子传给儿子。',
      'Each commandery had three officers — a governor for civil affairs, a commandant for the army, an inspector for oversight — none subordinate to the others; below the county came the district and the hamlet. All were appointed from the centre, paid a salary, and could not pass the post to a son.'],
     ['怎么保证远在千里之外的官员照办?靠「上计」:每年年终,郡守要把本郡的户口、垦田、赋税、刑狱、盗贼数字汇总上报中央,朝廷据此考核他做得好不好,决定升降。这套一年一次的数字问责,此后历代都在用。',
      'How was an official a thousand li away held to it? By the annual accounting: at the end of each year a commandery governor had to report his households, land under plough, taxes, criminal cases and banditry to the centre, and was assessed on those figures and promoted or demoted accordingly. Every dynasty after kept some version of this yearly reckoning by numbers.']],
  s:['汉初一度回到郡国并行,吴楚七国之乱后又收回来;此后历代名称改了很多次(州、道、路、行省),但「官员由中央任免、不世袭」这条没变。它也是科举得以运转的前提:有了要人来当的官位,才需要一套挑人的办法。',
     'The early Han went back for a while to a mix of commanderies and kingdoms, and pulled back again after the revolt of the seven kingdoms; the names changed many times afterwards — circuits, routes, provinces — but not the principle that officials are appointed from the centre and do not inherit. It is also what made the examination system possible: only a state that must fill posts needs a way of choosing who fills them.'] },

'驰道长城': { g:'obj', c:'秦', y:[-220,-210], n:['驰道长城','The highways and the Wall'],
  t:['三十万人在北边把旧墙连起来,同时几十万人在南边修路——同一个国家,同一个十年。',
     'Three hundred thousand men joining up old walls in the north while hundreds of thousands built roads in the south — one country, one decade.'],
  a:[['长城不是从头新建:战国的燕、赵、秦三国各有自己的北墙,蒙恬前 215 年率三十万人把它们连缀加固,《史记》说「起临洮,至辽东,延袤万余里」。它是夯土与石块砌的,和今天游客看到的明代砖长城不是一回事。',
      'The Wall was not built from nothing: Yan, Zhao and Qin each had northern walls of their own, and in 215 BC Meng Tian with three hundred thousand men joined and strengthened them — from Lintao to Liaodong, over ten thousand li, says the Shiji. It was rammed earth and stone, not the brick wall of Ming date that tourists see today.'],
     ['它也不是一道孤墙,而是一套体系:墙之外有烽燧传警、有障城驻兵、有亭鄣屯戍。今天在甘肃临洮、宁夏固原、内蒙古包头一带还能看到夯土残段,风化成一道道土垄,不注意会当成田埂。',
      'Nor was it a single wall but a system: beacon towers to pass the alarm, forts to hold troops, watch-posts and garrison stations. Weathered stretches of its rammed earth can still be seen around Lintao in Gansu, Guyuan in Ningxia and Baotou in Inner Mongolia, worn down into low ridges that a passer-by might take for field banks.'],
     ['两样东西是一件事的两面:路让军队与粮草能快速北上,墙让北方的骑兵不能快速南下。代价是民力——秦亡的原因里,长城、直道、宫室、戍边同时开工被反复提到。',
      'The two are one thing seen from both sides: roads let armies and grain move north quickly, the wall kept horsemen from moving south quickly. The price was men — the Wall, the Straight Road, the palaces and the frontier garrisons all going at once is named again and again among the reasons the Qin fell.']],
  s:['长城在此后两千年被历代重修改线,东段有明长城直接叠压在燕赵旧线上,而河套、阴山一段的秦长城比明长城北出两百公里以上。1987 年长城列入世界遗产。而那条直道的路基,因为高原上少有人扰动,反而比长城更完整地留了下来。',
     'The Wall was rebuilt and re-routed by dynasty after dynasty for two thousand years, in the east the Ming wall sits directly on the old Yan and Zhao line, while along the Ordos and the Yin mountains the Qin wall runs more than two hundred kilometres north of it. It became a World Heritage site in 1987. The bed of the Straight Road, little disturbed up on the plateau, has in some stretches come down to us more intact than the Wall itself.'] },

'丝绸之路': { g:'idea', c:'西汉', y:[-138,9], n:['丝绸之路','The Silk Road'],
  t:['一个使者被匈奴扣了十年才逃出来,回来带的不是盟约,是一张地图。',
     'An envoy held ten years by the Xiongnu got away at last, and what he brought home was not an alliance but a map.'],
  a:[['前 138 年张骞奉命西行,要联络大月氏夹击匈奴。他在路上被扣十余年,娶妻生子,后来逃脱西行,到大宛、康居、大月氏、大夏——大月氏已经不想打仗了,任务失败。',
      'In 138 BC Zhang Qian was sent west to enlist the Yuezhi against the Xiongnu. He was held more than ten years on the way, married and had a son there, then escaped and went on to Ferghana, Sogdiana, the Yuezhi and Bactria — who no longer wanted a war. The mission failed.'],
     ['他带回的是情报:西边有多少国家、多远、出什么。《史记》记他在大夏见到蜀地的布和邛竹杖,推断出另有一条经身毒(印度)的商路——这是中国人第一次知道西域的样子。',
      'What he brought back was intelligence: how many states lay west, how far, what they had. The Shiji records that in Bactria he saw cloth and bamboo staves from Sichuan and reasoned that another road ran through India — the first time China knew what the west looked like.'],
     ['此后汉朝设河西四郡、通西域都护,商队开始往来:丝绸西去,苜蓿、葡萄、石榴、汗血马东来。「丝绸之路」这个名字要晚得多——1877 年德国地理学家李希霍芬才起的。',
      'The Han then set up four commanderies in the Gansu corridor and a protectorate in the western regions, and the caravans began: silk going west, alfalfa, grapes, pomegranates and the blood-sweating horses coming east. The name Silk Road is far younger — coined by the German geographer Ferdinand von Richthofen in 1877.']],
  s:['它从来不是一条路,而是一张随着政治与气候变化的网;走这条路的人也不只运货,佛教、景教、摩尼教都是沿它进来的。2014 年中国、哈萨克斯坦、吉尔吉斯斯坦联合申报的「长安—天山廊道路网」列入世界遗产,共 33 处遗址。',
     'It was never one road but a net that shifted with politics and climate, and what travelled it was not only goods: Buddhism, the Church of the East and Manichaeism all came in along it. In 2014 China, Kazakhstan and Kyrgyzstan jointly inscribed the Chang\'an–Tianshan corridor on the World Heritage list — thirty-three sites in all.'] },

'独尊儒术': { g:'idea', c:'西汉', y:[-134,9], n:['独尊儒术','Confucianism made the state doctrine'],
  t:['一个读书人给皇帝上了三道对策,此后两千年,中国的官从读同一批书的人里出。',
     'A scholar answered the emperor in three memorials, and for two thousand years afterwards China\'s officials came from among the readers of one set of books.'],
  a:[['前 134 年董仲舒对策,主张「诸不在六艺之科孔子之术者,皆绝其道,勿使并进」——朝廷只承认儒家一派为官学。后世常引的「罢黜百家,表章六经」八个字,其实是班固在《汉书》里对汉武帝的评语,不是董仲舒的原话。汉武帝采纳,设五经博士,前 124 年立太学,起初只有博士弟子五十人。',
      'In 134 BC Dong Zhongshu urged that whatever did not fall within the six arts and the way of Confucius should be cut off and not allowed to advance — that the state should recognise the Confucians alone. The phrase usually quoted, dismiss the hundred schools and honour the six classics, is in fact Ban Gu\'s later verdict on the emperor in the Han History, not Dong\'s own words. Emperor Wu agreed, appointed academicians for the five classics, and in 124 BC founded the imperial academy, at first with fifty students.'],
     ['他讲的儒学已经不是孔子那一套:掺进了阴阳五行与「天人感应」——天灾是上天对皇帝的警告。这一条既抬高了皇权(受命于天),也给了读书人批评皇帝的合法工具。',
      'What he taught was no longer Confucius plain: yin-yang and the five phases had been worked in, and with them the idea that heaven responds to the ruler — a disaster is heaven\'s warning to the emperor. That both raised the throne (it holds a mandate) and gave scholars a lawful way to criticise it.'],
     ['太学的规模一路涨:西汉末年三千人,到东汉质帝本初元年(146 年)已有三万余生。士人由此成为一个靠读书进身的阶层,后来的察举与科举都建在这个基础上。',
      'The academy grew: three thousand by the end of the Western Han, and over thirty thousand by 146 under Emperor Zhi. A class of men who rose by study came into being, and the later systems of recommendation and examination were built on it.']],
  s:['它的影响不止于中国:朝鲜、日本、越南的官学与考试制度都以此为底本。也有代价——学术自此有了正统与异端之分,诸子百家争鸣的局面不再有。今天说「儒家文化圈」,起点就在这一次。',
     'The effect reached beyond China: the state schools and examinations of Korea, Japan and Vietnam were all built on this model. There was a cost — learning now had an orthodoxy and a heresy, and the contention of the hundred schools did not come again. What is meant today by the Confucian world begins here.'] },

'《史记》': { g:'obj', c:'西汉', y:[-104,-91], n:['《史记》','The Records of the Grand Historian'],
  t:['他受了最难堪的刑罚,活下来把书写完——中国此后两千年的正史,都照他的体例写。',
     'He took the most shameful of punishments and lived to finish the book — and every dynastic history for the next two thousand years follows his form.'],
  a:[['司马迁继父职任太史令,前 104 年前后开始写。前 99 年他为投降匈奴的李陵辩护,触怒武帝,被处宫刑;他在给友人的信里说,忍下来是因为书还没写完。',
      'Sima Qian took over his father\'s post as court astrologer and began writing about 104 BC. In 99 BC he spoke up for the general Li Ling, who had surrendered to the Xiongnu, angered the emperor and was castrated; in a letter to a friend he said he bore it because the book was not finished.'],
     ['全书一百三十篇、约五十二万六千字,从黄帝写到汉武帝。他创的体例是「纪传体」:本纪(帝王)、世家(诸侯)、列传(人物)、表、书(制度)——用人物来组织历史,而不是按年月流水。',
      'It runs to a hundred and thirty chapters and some 526,000 characters, from the Yellow Emperor to Emperor Wu. The form he invented organises history by people rather than by year: annals for rulers, hereditary houses for the great families, biographies, tables and treatises on institutions.'],
     ['他不只写胜利者:项羽入本纪、陈胜入世家、刺客与游侠、商人、医者、滑稽之人各有列传;写到本朝的事也不回避。「究天人之际,通古今之变,成一家之言」是他自己的说法。',
      'He did not write only the winners: Xiang Yu the defeated has an annal, the rebel Chen Sheng a hereditary house, and there are biographies of assassins, knights errant, merchants, physicians and jesters; he did not shy from his own dynasty either. To fathom heaven and man, to trace change through the ages, to make a work of his own — his own words for what he was doing.']],
  s:['此后的二十四史全部沿用纪传体,《史记》是其中唯一的通史,也公认写得最好。鲁迅称它「史家之绝唱,无韵之《离骚》」。它同时是史料与文学:很多今天耳熟能详的场面——鸿门宴、荆轲刺秦、垓下之围——是他写下来的样子。',
     'The twenty-four dynastic histories that follow all use his form; his alone covers the whole span, and is reckoned the best written. Lu Xun called it the historians\' swan-song, a Li Sao without rhyme. It is source and literature at once: many of the scenes anyone in China can recount — the banquet at Hongmen, the assassin sent against the king of Qin, the last night at Gaixia — are as he set them down.'] },

'行中书省': { g:'inst', c:'元', y:[1271,1368], n:['行中书省','The branch secretariats'],
  t:['本来是中央派出去办事的临时机构,派着派着就固定下来——今天中国的「省」,名字是这么来的。',
     'It began as the central chancellery sending men out on temporary duty; the duty became permanent, and that is where the Chinese word for province comes from.'],
  a:[['中央的最高行政机构叫中书省。地方上事情多、路又远,就派中书省的官员到某地「行中书省事」——代表中书省在那里办公,本是临时差遣。',
      'The chief organ of central government was the Zhongshu Sheng, the secretariat. Where the business was heavy and the distance great, officials were sent to "act for the secretariat" in a given region — a temporary posting.'],
     ['元朝把它坐实成常设的一级政区,到 1330 年前后定型为十个行省(岭北、辽阳、河南江北、陕西、四川、甘肃、云南、江浙、江西、湖广),大都周边的腹里由中书省直辖,吐蕃地区由宣政院管。',
      'The Yuan made it a standing tier of administration, settling by about 1330 into ten branch secretariats — Lingbei, Liaoyang, Henan-Jiangbei, Shaanxi, Sichuan, Gansu, Yunnan, Jiangzhe, Jiangxi and Huguang — with the metropolitan region around Dadu under the secretariat itself, and Tibet under a separate commission.'],
     ['划界的原则是「犬牙交错」:故意让一个省跨过山川险要,不与天然地形重合,免得地方凭险自立。汉中划归陕西而不划四川,就是这么来的。',
      'The boundaries were drawn to interlock like dog\'s teeth: a province was made to straddle the mountain passes and river lines rather than follow them, so that no region could shut itself in behind natural defences. That is why Hanzhong went to Shaanxi and not to Sichuan.']],
  s:['明清沿用并调整,今天中国省级政区的轮廓大体在元明之际定型;江西、湖广、四川、云南、甘肃这些名字都是那时留下的。「省」这个字原意是宫禁中的官署,经过这一步才变成一级地方政区的名称——越南 1831 年仿清制设省,用的就是这个词;日本的「省」是更早从唐朝尚书省学去的中央部门,朝鲜的一级政区叫「道」,都不是这一支。',
     'The Ming and Qing kept the system with adjustments, and the outline of China\'s provinces today largely settled between the Yuan and the Ming; Jiangxi, Huguang, Sichuan, Yunnan and Gansu are names from then. The word sheng originally meant an office inside the palace, and only through this step came to mean a province — Vietnam took the word for its provinces in 1831; Japan\'s sheng — shō — is a central ministry borrowed much earlier from the Tang, and Korea\'s first-level divisions were called do. Neither descends from this.'] },

'青花瓷': { g:'obj', c:'元', y:[1300,1368], n:['青花瓷','Blue-and-white porcelain'],
  t:['中国的白瓷、波斯的钴蓝、伊斯兰世界的买家——这三样凑齐,才有了青花。',
     'Chinese white porcelain, Persian cobalt, and buyers in the Islamic world: blue-and-white needed all three.'],
  a:[['颜料是进口的:一种叫「苏麻离青」的钴料从波斯来,发色浓艳带铁锈斑,国产钴料做不出那个蓝。景德镇的高岭土白瓷则是本地的看家本领。',
      'The colour was imported: a cobalt known as sumali blue came from Persia, giving a deep tone flecked with iron-rust that native cobalt could not match. The white porcelain body, from Jingdezhen\'s kaolin, was the local strength.'],
     ['现存最早有确切纪年的青花是「大维德瓶」——一对象耳瓶,颈上写着至正十一年(1351 年)供奉的题记,现藏伦敦大英博物馆。',
      'The earliest blue-and-white with a firm date is the pair of David Vases, their necks inscribed with a dedication of the eleventh year of Zhizheng — 1351 — now in the British Museum in London.'],
     ['早期青花的器形与纹样多为外销订制:大盘大罐是给中东人围坐分食用的,尺寸远大于中国人自己的餐具。土耳其托普卡帕宫藏元明瓷器数千件;阿德比尔神庙那批八百多件,1935 年后移藏德黑兰的伊朗国家博物馆。',
      'The early shapes and patterns were made for export: the great dishes and jars were for diners in the Middle East eating from a shared vessel, far larger than anything used at a Chinese table. Topkapı Palace in Turkey holds some thousands of Yuan and Ming pieces; the eight hundred-odd from the Ardabil shrine were moved after 1935 to the National Museum in Tehran.']],
  s:['它是一件典型的「合作品」:本地的土与火、外来的颜料、外来的市场。明清两代青花成为中国瓷器的主流,并被荷兰代尔夫特、日本有田、伊朗、越南反复仿制——今天全世界的「中国瓷器」印象,大半是这一种蓝白。',
     'It is a made-together thing: local clay and fire, foreign colour, foreign market. Blue-and-white became the mainstream of Chinese porcelain under the Ming and Qing and was imitated again and again at Delft in Holland, Arita in Japan, and in Iran and Vietnam — most of what the world pictures as Chinese porcelain is this blue on white.'] },

'元曲': { g:'idea', c:'元', y:[1279,1368], n:['元曲','Yuan drama'],
  t:['读书人当不成官,就去给戏班子写本子——中国戏曲最好的一批剧本出在这几十年。',
     'Scholars who could not become officials wrote for the theatre instead — and the best plays in Chinese drama come from these decades.'],
  a:[['元初停开科举近八十年,1313 年才下诏恢复、1315 年首次开考,读书人的出路窄了,不少人转去写杂剧。杂剧一本四折,一人主唱,唱词讲究平仄格律,说白与动作穿插——它是当时城市里最受欢迎的娱乐。',
      'The examinations were suspended for nearly eighty years in the early Yuan, restored by decree in 1313 and first held in 1315; with the usual road closed, many educated men turned to writing plays. A zaju runs four acts with one singing role, its arias strictly patterned, speech and action between — the most popular entertainment in the cities of the day.'],
     ['关汉卿的《窦娥冤》写一个被冤杀的女子,临刑发下三桩誓愿:血溅白练、六月飞雪、大旱三年——后来全都应验。王实甫的《西厢记》写少女与书生私订终身,是中国最有名的爱情戏。',
      'Guan Hanqing\'s Injustice to Dou E has a woman wrongly executed swear three oaths on the scaffold — that her blood will not touch the ground, that snow will fall in midsummer, that drought will hold three years — and all three come true. Wang Shifu\'s Romance of the Western Chamber, in which a girl and a student pledge themselves without their families, is the best-loved love story on the Chinese stage.'],
     ['现存元杂剧约一百六十种,作者姓名可考的有百余人。演出场所叫「勾栏瓦舍」,在大都、杭州这样的城市里成片经营,观众是市民而不是宫廷。',
      'Some hundred and sixty Yuan zaju survive, with over a hundred named authors. They were played in the covered galleries of the entertainment quarters, clustered in cities like Dadu and Hangzhou, before townspeople rather than a court.']],
  s:['明清的传奇、以及后来的京剧,剧目与曲牌都大量取自元曲;《窦娥冤》《西厢记》至今还在演。一个常被提起的对照是:同一时期的欧洲,戏剧还主要在教堂与广场上演宗教剧。',
     'The chuanqi plays of the Ming and Qing, and Peking opera after them, took their plots and tune-titles largely from here; Dou E and the Western Chamber are still staged. A comparison often drawn: in the Europe of the same years, drama was still mostly religious, played in churches and market squares.'] },

'郑和宝船': { g:'obj', c:'明', y:[1405,1433], n:['郑和宝船','Zheng He\'s treasure ships'],
  t:['七次下西洋,最远到非洲东岸——比哥伦布早八十七年,船队规模也大得多。',
     'Seven voyages, as far as the coast of East Africa — eighty-seven years before Columbus, and on a far greater scale.'],
  a:[['1405 到 1433 年共七次,每次两万七千人上下、大小船只两百余艘,其中最大的宝船每次四十到六十余艘。航线从南京、太仓出发,经占城、爪哇、马六甲、锡兰、古里,最远到忽鲁谟斯、亚丁、木骨都束(今索马里摩加迪沙)与麻林(今肯尼亚一带)。',
      'Seven fleets between 1405 and 1433, each of some twenty-seven thousand men and over two hundred vessels, of which the great treasure ships numbered between forty and sixty. They sailed from Nanjing and Taicang by way of Champa, Java, Malacca, Ceylon and Calicut, and at the farthest reached Hormuz, Aden, Mogadishu and the Malindi coast of today\'s Kenya.'],
     ['宝船到底多大有争议:《明史》记「修四十四丈、广十八丈」(按明营造尺约 138 米,若按裁衣尺则是 151 米),按木船结构这个尺寸很难成立;1957 年南京宝船厂遗址出土一根长 11 米的舵杆,是现有最硬的实物线索,但据它反推的船长各家算法差别很大。',
      'How big the treasure ships were is disputed: the Ming History gives forty-four zhang by eighteen — about 138 metres by the builder\'s foot, or 151 by the tailor\'s — which is hard to reconcile with wooden hull construction. An eleven-metre rudder post excavated in 1957 at the Nanjing shipyard site is the firmest physical evidence, but the lengths reckoned back from it differ widely.'],
     ['船队带回长颈鹿、狮子、鸵鸟,当时被当作瑞兽「麒麟」进献;也带回沿途的地理知识——《郑和航海图》记下四十多个国家的航路、针位与牵星高度。',
      'The fleets brought back giraffes, lions and ostriches, presented at court as auspicious beasts; and they brought back knowledge — the Zheng He charts record the routes, compass bearings and star altitudes for over forty countries.']],
  s:['1433 年之后朝廷停止了远航,原因是多重的:耗费巨大、北方边防吃紧、朝中反对。此后几十年海禁趋严,而同一个世纪末葡萄牙人绕过好望角来到印度洋。这一停一进常被并置讨论,但两件事的动机并不相同:郑和的船队是国家仪典与朝贡体系的延伸,不是去找市场的。',
     'After 1433 the court stopped the voyages, for several reasons at once: the cost, pressure on the northern frontier, opposition at court. Sea bans tightened over the decades that followed, and in the last years of the same century the Portuguese came round the Cape into the Indian Ocean. The two are often set side by side, though the motives were not the same: Zheng He\'s fleets were an extension of state ceremony and the tribute system, not a search for markets.'] },

'紫禁城': { g:'obj', c:'明', y:[1406,1420], n:['紫禁城','The Forbidden City'],
  t:['一座住了明清两朝二十四位皇帝的宫城,今天是世界上现存规模最大的木结构建筑群。',
     'A palace city that housed twenty-four emperors of two dynasties, and is today the largest surviving complex of wooden buildings in the world.'],
  a:[['1406 年永乐帝下诏营建,1420 年建成,1421 年迁都北京。木料从四川、湖广的深山运来,金砖(细密的方砖)在苏州烧制,汉白玉从北京房山采,冬天泼水成冰道把巨石拖进城。',
      'The Yongle emperor ordered it built in 1406; it was finished in 1420 and the capital moved to Beijing in 1421. Timber came from the mountains of Sichuan and Huguang, the fine floor bricks were fired at Suzhou, the white marble quarried at Fangshan, and in winter the great stones were dragged over ice roads made by sluicing water on the ground.'],
     ['整体沿一条南北中轴线布局,前朝三大殿(太和、中和、保和)办公,后廷是居住区。现存建筑九百余座、房屋八千余间——「九千九百九十九间半」是民间说法,不是实数。',
      'It is laid out on a single north-south axis, with the three great halls of state in front and the residential quarters behind. Some nine hundred buildings survive, with over eight thousand bays — a bay being the space between four pillars, not a room in the modern sense — the popular figure of nine thousand nine hundred and ninety-nine and a half is a saying, not a count.'],
     ['木结构最怕火:三大殿在明清两代多次遭雷击焚毁重建,现存太和殿是清康熙年间(1695—1697)重建的。1925 年故宫博物院成立,1987 年列入世界遗产。',
      'Fire is the enemy of timber: the three halls burned and were rebuilt several times under both dynasties, and the Hall of Supreme Harmony standing today dates from a rebuilding of 1695–97. The Palace Museum was founded in 1925, and the complex became a World Heritage site in 1987.']],
  s:['它是中国古代宫殿制度的集大成:中轴线、前朝后寝、左祖右社,这些规矩上溯到《周礼》。今天故宫博物院藏品一百八十多万件,大部分是清宫旧藏;另有一批 1948 年运往台湾,现藏台北故宫。两岸的这两座博物院,收的是同一批东西。',
     'It is the summing-up of the Chinese palace tradition: the central axis, court in front and living quarters behind, ancestral temple to the left and altar of soil to the right — rules that go back to the Rites of Zhou. The Palace Museum today holds over 1.8 million objects, most of them from the Qing palace collection; another portion was moved to Taiwan in 1948 and is in the Palace Museum in Taipei. The two museums hold what was once one collection.'] },

'阳明心学': { g:'idea', c:'明', y:[1508,1529], n:['阳明心学','Wang Yangming\'s learning of the mind'],
  t:['他被贬到贵州的驿站,半夜想通一件事:道理不在书里,在你自己心里。',
     'Banished to a post station in Guizhou, he saw it in the middle of the night: the principle is not in the books, it is in your own mind.'],
  a:[['1508 年王守仁因得罪宦官被贬为龙场(今贵州修文)驿丞。那里瘴疠丛生、语言不通,他在石棺里躺着想「圣人处此,更有何道」,忽然悟到「圣人之道,吾性自足」——史称龙场悟道。',
      'In 1508 Wang Shouren, having offended a powerful eunuch, was demoted to run a post station at Longchang in what is now Guizhou. The place was malarial and the language strange; lying in a stone coffin he asked himself what a sage would do here, and saw suddenly that the way of the sage is complete within one\'s own nature.'],
     ['他讲三件事:心即理(道理在心里,不在外物)、知行合一(知道而不做,等于没知道)、致良知(每个人心里那点是非之心,推到底就是最高的道理)。这是对当时官方的程朱理学「格物穷理」的正面反驳。',
      'He taught three things: that principle is in the mind and not in things outside; that knowing and doing are one, and to know without doing is not to know; and that the innate sense of right in every person, followed to its end, is the highest principle. This was a head-on answer to the state orthodoxy of investigating things to exhaust their principle.'],
     ['他不是只坐着讲学:平定江西的匪乱、四十余天平定宁王之乱,是明代少有的能带兵的文官。他的语录由弟子编成《传习录》。',
      'He did not only lecture: he put down the banditry of Jiangxi, and crushed the rebellion of the Prince of Ning in some forty days — one of the few Ming civil officials who could take the field. His sayings were collected by his students as the Records for Practice.']],
  s:['心学在晚明流行极广,也被批评为流于空疏。它对日本的影响格外深:幕末的西乡隆盛、吉田松阴都读阳明,明治维新的一批人自称阳明学徒。今天在贵州修文还有龙场悟道的遗址,日本、韩国都有阳明学的研究会。',
     'The learning of the mind spread widely in the late Ming and was also criticised as loose and unrigorous. Its effect in Japan was especially deep: Saigo Takamori and Yoshida Shoin read him, and a number of the men of the Meiji Restoration called themselves his students. The site at Longchang in Guizhou can still be visited, and there are societies for Yangming studies in both Japan and Korea.'] },

'奠定现代版图与多民族格局': { g:'inst', c:'清', y:[1644,1912], n:['奠定现代版图与多民族格局','The territorial and multiethnic frame'],
  t:['今天中国地图的大致轮廓,和境内的民族格局,基本是这两百多年里定下来的。',
     'The rough outline of the map of China today, and the mix of peoples inside it, largely settled in these two and a half centuries.'],
  a:[['清朝对不同地区用不同办法:汉地沿用明制的省与科举,蒙古编盟旗,西藏设驻藏大臣与金瓶掣签,新疆先设伊犁将军、1884 年建省,东北长期封禁。同一位君主在不同人眼里是不同的角色:汉人称皇帝、蒙古人称大汗、藏传佛教尊为文殊菩萨的化身,连他用的印玺与文书语言都随对象换。',
      'The Qing governed different regions differently: provinces and examinations as under the Ming in China proper, leagues and banners in Mongolia, imperial residents and the golden urn in Tibet, a military governor at Ili and from 1884 a province in Xinjiang, and a long closure of Manchuria to settlement. One ruler was a different figure to each: emperor to the Chinese, khan to the Mongols, an incarnation of Manjushri to Tibetan Buddhists — and even the seals he used and the language of his documents changed with the audience.'],
     ['边界也第一次以条约的形式画下来:1689 年中俄《尼布楚条约》是中国签的第一个近代边界条约,用满、俄、拉丁三种文字写成,划定黑龙江以北的界线。',
      'Borders were also drawn by treaty for the first time: the Treaty of Nerchinsk with Russia in 1689, written in Manchu, Russian and Latin, fixed the line north of the Amur — the first modern boundary treaty China signed.'],
     ['1759 年平定准噶尔与回部之后版图达到最大,约一千三百万平方公里。此后一百多年通过一系列条约不断收缩,而 1912 年之后的中国、以及今天中国的民族构成与行政区划,大体承接自这个框架。',
      'The realm reached its greatest extent after the Dzungar and Muslim campaigns of 1759, at some 13 million square kilometres. It shrank through a century of treaties thereafter — but the China that followed 1912, and the mix of peoples and the administrative map of today, descend in outline from this frame.']],
  s:['这也是一份复杂的遗产:多民族国家的整合方式、边疆的治理传统、以及近代一系列不平等条约留下的边界争议,都从这里来。清朝自己用的国名是「大清」,而「中国」一词在《尼布楚条约》的满文与拉丁文本里已经用来指这个国家的整体——这是它第一次出现在国际条约上。',
     'It is a complicated inheritance: how a state of many peoples was held together, how the frontiers were governed, and the border disputes left by a century of unequal treaties all come from here. The dynasty called itself the Great Qing, while the word Zhongguo — China — was already used in the Manchu and Latin texts of the Nerchinsk treaty for the state as a whole, its first appearance in an international treaty.'] },

'《红楼梦》': { g:'obj', c:'清', y:[1754,1791], n:['《红楼梦》','Dream of the Red Chamber'],
  t:['一部没写完的小说,养活了一门叫「红学」的学问——研究它的人比研究很多朝代的人还多。',
     'An unfinished novel that gave rise to a discipline of its own — more people study it than study some entire dynasties.'],
  a:[['曹雪芹出身江宁织造世家,少年时家道抄没,晚年在北京西郊「举家食粥」时写这本书。他自己说「披阅十载,增删五次」,写到八十回未完就去世了。',
      'Cao Xueqin came of a family that had held the imperial silk works at Nanjing; it was confiscated when he was young, and he wrote the book in his last years in the western suburbs of Beijing, the household living on gruel. Ten years of reading and revising, five times rewritten, he said; he died with eighty chapters done and the book unfinished.'],
     ['它先以手抄本流传,书名有《石头记》《情僧录》等好几个。今天存世的早期抄本有十来种,以最早发现的那几种最要紧:甲戌本只剩十六回,却带着大量朱笔批语,署名「脂砚斋」——这个人显然认识作者,批语里常写「作者曾有此事」,却至今不知道是谁。',
      'It went about first in manuscript, under several titles. A dozen or so early manuscripts survive, and the earliest matter most: one of them has only sixteen chapters left but carries a mass of red-ink commentary signed Zhiyanzhai — someone who plainly knew the author, who writes things like the author himself went through this, and whose identity is still unknown.'],
     ['书写一个大家族从鼎盛到抄没,四百多个有名有姓的人物,里面的诗、菜、药方、衣料、园林都写得极细。研究它的学问叫「红学」,从版本考据到人物索隐,一百多年不衰。',
      'It follows a great house from its height to its confiscation, with over four hundred named characters, and sets down the poems, dishes, prescriptions, fabrics and gardens in minute detail. The study of it — Redology — has run for over a century, from textual scholarship to the hunt for real people behind the characters.']],
  s:['它常被称作中国古典小说的顶点,与《三国演义》《水浒传》《西游记》并称四大名著。它写的不是英雄或神怪,而是一群年轻人在一座园子里的日常与败落——这在中国小说里是新的。曹雪芹生前没有看到它出版。',
     'It is commonly called the summit of the Chinese classical novel, one of the four great books with the Three Kingdoms, the Water Margin and the Journey to the West. Its subject is neither heroes nor demons but a group of young people in a garden, their days and their ruin — new in Chinese fiction. Cao Xueqin never saw it printed.'] },

'四十年高速增长': { g:'inst', c:'共和国', y:[1978,2018], n:['四十年高速增长','Forty years of fast growth'],
  t:['一个大国的经济连续四十年年均增长接近百分之十——这在有统计以来的大型经济体里没有先例。',
     'Forty years of growth averaging close to ten per cent a year — no major economy on record has done it.'],
  a:[['起点通常从 1978 年算:农村包产到户、设经济特区、允许私营与外资、价格逐步放开。1979 年深圳还是个边陲县城,2020 年代已是常住人口一千七百多万的城市。',
      'The usual starting point is 1978: household contracting in the countryside, special economic zones, private and foreign firms allowed, prices freed step by step. Shenzhen was a border county town in 1979; by the 2020s it was a city of over seventeen million.'],
     ['变化的量级不容易感觉到,举两个数:1978 年全国粮食产量三亿吨出头、人均不到四百公斤;2020 年代粮食产量约六亿五千万吨,而同期从事农业的人口反而少了一大半。1978 年全国民用汽车保有量约一百三十六万辆,今天光是一年新卖出的就有两千多万辆。',
      'The scale of the change is hard to feel; two figures give it. In 1978 the grain harvest was a little over 300 million tonnes, under 400 kg a head; in the 2020s it is about 650 million tonnes, with less than half as many people farming. In 1978 there were about 1.36 million motor vehicles in the whole country; today more than twenty million are sold in a single year.'],
     ['代价与难题同样明显:环境污染、区域与城乡差距、房地产与地方债务、人口老龄化。增速在 2010 年代逐步回落,官方表述从「高速增长」改为「高质量发展」。',
      'The costs are as plain: pollution, the gaps between regions and between town and country, property and local government debt, an ageing population. Growth slowed through the 2010s, and the official phrase changed from fast growth to high-quality development.']],
  s:['这段增长的规模让它成为经济史上被研究最多的案例之一,而对它的解释至今没有共识:是市场化改革、是人口红利、是加入全球分工、还是政府主导的投资?几种解释各有证据。对普通人来说,最直接的变化是一代人之内从缺粮到过剩、从自行车到高铁。',
     'The sheer size of it has made this one of the most studied episodes in economic history, and there is still no agreement on the explanation: marketisation, a demographic dividend, entry into the global division of labour, state-led investment — each account has its evidence. For ordinary people the plainest change is that within one lifetime the country went from short of grain to surplus, from bicycles to high-speed rail.'] },

'数亿人脱贫': { g:'inst', c:'共和国', y:[1981,2020], n:['数亿人脱贫','Hundreds of millions out of poverty'],
  t:['按世界银行的口径,过去四十年全球减少的极端贫困人口里,很大一部分在中国。',
     'By the World Bank\'s measure, a large share of the world\'s fall in extreme poverty over forty years happened here.'],
  a:[['按世界银行早年用的每天 1.9 美元极端贫困线(2011 年购买力平价),中国的贫困人口从 1981 年的八亿多降到 2010 年代末的接近于零。这条线是国际比较用的、不是中国自己的标准,而且世行自己也在改:2022 年改成 2.15 美元,2025 年又改成 3 美元。',
      'By the World Bank\'s older extreme poverty line of $1.90 a day at 2011 purchasing power parity, the number of poor in China fell from over eight hundred million in 1981 to close to zero by the end of the 2010s. That line is for international comparison, not China\'s own standard — and the Bank keeps changing it: to $2.15 in 2022 and to $3.00 in 2025.'],
     ['中国自己的标准是农村贫困线(2010 年不变价每人每年 2300 元)。按这条线,2012 年底农村贫困人口 9899 万,2020 年底官方宣布全部脱贫。',
      'China\'s own measure is a rural poverty line of 2,300 yuan a year at 2010 prices. By it there were 98.99 million rural poor at the end of 2012, and at the end of 2020 the government announced that all had been lifted out.'],
     ['前期主要靠经济增长与农村改革带动,后期是「精准扶贫」:建档立卡到户、驻村工作队、异地搬迁、产业与教育帮扶。学界对最后阶段的贡献比例、以及脱贫标准的口径仍有讨论。',
      'The early decades ran mostly on growth and rural reform; the last stage was targeted: households registered one by one, work teams stationed in villages, relocation from unviable places, help with livelihoods and schooling. Scholars still debate how much the final stage contributed, and how the threshold should be read.']],
  s:['无论用哪条线,这都是有记录以来规模最大的一次贫困人口下降。它也改变了全球的贫困地图:1980 年代世界上大部分极端贫困人口在东亚,今天集中在撒哈拉以南非洲与南亚。',
     'By whichever line, it is the largest fall in the number of poor people on record. It also redrew the world\'s poverty map: in the 1980s most of the extreme poor were in East Asia, and today they are concentrated in sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia.'] },

'行省制': { g:'inst', c:'波斯(阿契美尼德)', y:[-522,-330], n:['行省制','The satrapies'],
  t:['把帝国切成二十来块,每块派三个人互相盯着——这套办法后来被反复抄。',
     'Cut the empire into a score of pieces and put three men in each to watch one another — an arrangement copied again and again.'],
  a:[['每个行省(萨特拉庇)派一位总督管民政与税收,另设将领管驻军,再派「王之眼」不定期巡查——三者互不统属,直接对国王负责。',
      'Each satrapy had a governor for civil affairs and taxes, a separate commander for the garrison, and the King\'s Eye who came round to inspect — none answering to the others, all answering to the king.'],
     ['贡赋按行省定额,而各省缴的东西不一样:埃及缴谷物,巴比伦缴银子与男童,印度那一省按希罗多德的说法缴砂金。行政通用语不是波斯语而是阿拉美语,地方保留自己的法律、宗教与文字。',
      'Tribute was assessed by satrapy, and each paid in what it had: grain from Egypt, silver and boys from Babylon, gold dust from the Indian province, if Herodotus is to be believed. The language of administration was not Persian but Aramaic, and each region kept its own law, religion and script.'],
     ['帝国不要求你变成波斯人。埃及的行省用埃及的办法,巴比伦用巴比伦的,犹太人被允许回耶路撒冷重建圣殿——只要纳税和出兵。',
      'The empire did not ask you to become Persian. Egypt was run in the Egyptian way and Babylon in the Babylonian, and the Jews were let go home to rebuild their temple — so long as the taxes came in and the levies turned out.']],
  s:['亚历山大灭波斯后原样接手了这套行省,塞琉古、帕提亚、萨珊、乃至后来的伊斯兰帝国都在用同一个思路;英语 satrap 一词至今还有「替别人管一方的长官」的意味。它的弱点也在这里:总督兼掌一方,中央一弱就成了割据的起点——公元前 4 世纪西部几个大总督接连叛乱,正是帝国崩塌的前奏。',
     'Alexander took the satrapies over as they stood, and the Seleucids, Parthians, Sasanians and later the Islamic empires all worked on the same idea; the English word satrap still carries the sense of a governor ruling for someone else. The weakness lay in the same place: a governor with a whole province in hand becomes the starting point for secession as soon as the centre weakens — and the revolts of the western satraps in the fourth century BC were the prelude to the fall.'] },

'御道驿传': { g:'inst', c:'波斯(阿契美尼德)', y:[-520,-330], n:['御道驿传','The Royal Road and its relays'],
  t:['它不是一条路,是一串驿站——希罗多德数过:从萨迪斯到苏萨,一百一十一个。',
     'It was not a road so much as a string of stations — Herodotus counted them: a hundred and eleven from Sardis to Susa.'],
  a:[['希罗多德把全程一段段列了出来:哪一段几个驿站、几个帕拉桑(波斯里程单位),中间要渡哪条河、过哪道有守军的关卡。这是古代少见的一份逐段行程表,今天研究者就靠它复原路线。',
      'Herodotus set the whole route down stage by stage: how many stations and how many parasangs in each stretch, which rivers had to be crossed, which guarded gates passed. It is a rare thing from antiquity — an itinerary given segment by segment — and it is what modern scholars reconstruct the route from.'],
     ['驿站不是旅店,是国家设施:备好换乘的马、粮草与住处,凭官方文书才能取用。路上还有守军把住渡口与山口,既保护商旅也检查往来的人。',
      'The stations were not inns but state installations: fresh horses, fodder and lodging, drawn on by written authorisation. Troops held the fords and the passes, protecting traffic and checking who was on the move.'],
     ['路本身多为夯实的土路,只在必要处铺石。土耳其戈尔迪翁附近发掘出一段路基,宽约六米、两侧有路缘石与排水——这是这条大道少数留下实物的地方之一。',
      'The road itself was mostly rammed earth, paved only where it had to be. A stretch of its bed was excavated near Gordion in Turkey, about six metres wide with kerbstones and drainage on either side — one of the few places where the great road survives as something you can stand on.']],
  s:['亚历山大东征走的就是这条路;此后帕提亚、萨珊、以至伊斯兰帝国的驿传都在同一条线上重建。这套「路 + 驿站 + 凭证」的组合解决的是所有大帝国的同一个问题:命令传得比敌人快多少,国土就能有多大。罗马的驿传、蒙古的站赤、明清的驿站,做法几乎一样——彼此未必有传承,而是同一个问题只有这一种答案。',
     'Alexander marched east along it, and the Parthians, Sasanians and later the Islamic empires rebuilt their post services on the same line. The combination of road, relay station and travel warrant answers the question every large empire faces: how far an order can outrun an enemy is how large the realm can be. The Roman cursus publicus, the Mongol yam and the post stations of the Ming and Qing all work the same way — not necessarily borrowed one from another, but because the problem admits of only one answer.'] },

'宗教宽容': { g:'idea', c:'波斯(阿契美尼德)', y:[-539,-330], n:['宗教宽容','Toleration'],
  t:['不毁别人的神庙,反而出钱修——这不是善心,是一套省钱的统治办法。',
     'Not pulling down other people\'s temples but paying to repair them — not kindness so much as a cheaper way to rule.'],
  a:[['居鲁士入巴比伦后没有洗劫神庙,反而把被前朝集中到都城的各地神像送回原处;圆柱铭文里他说自己让被掳的人各归其地。',
      'When Cyrus took Babylon he did not sack the temples; he sent home the divine images that the previous king had gathered into the capital, and says on his cylinder that he let deported peoples return to their own places.'],
     ['《旧约》记居鲁士下诏准许犹太人返回耶路撒冷重建圣殿,并归还被掳走的器皿;《以斯拉记》说大流士后来又核查档案、确认了这道诏令并出资支持。',
      'The Hebrew Bible records his decree letting the Jews go back to Jerusalem to rebuild the temple, and returning the vessels taken from it; Ezra says Darius later had the archives searched, confirmed the decree and paid towards the work.'],
     ['在埃及,波斯王以法老的身份出现、供奉埃及的神;这套做法不是普世的原则——反叛的地方照样被严厉镇压,后来的君主对埃及的态度也变过。',
      'In Egypt the Persian king appeared as pharaoh and made offerings to Egyptian gods. It was not a universal principle: rebellious places were put down hard, and later kings did not always treat Egypt the same way.']],
  s:['把它说成现代意义上的「宗教自由」是时代错置——它更接近一种统治成本的计算:让人保留自己的神,统治就便宜得多。但效果是真的:一个从印度河到爱琴海、语言与信仰各不相同的地方,能靠这套办法维持两百年。犹太人对居鲁士的评价至今特殊——《以赛亚书》称他为「受膏者」,这是唯一被这样称呼的外邦君主。',
     'To call it religious freedom in the modern sense is an anachronism — it is closer to a calculation of what rule costs: let people keep their gods and governing them is much cheaper. But the effect was real: a realm from the Indus to the Aegean, of many tongues and many faiths, was held together this way for two centuries. Jewish tradition still treats Cyrus as a special case — Isaiah calls him an anointed one, the only foreign ruler so named.'] },

'横跨三洲的行政与米利特体系': { g:'inst', c:'奥斯曼帝国', y:[1453,1839], n:['横跨三洲的行政与米利特体系','Ruling three continents, and the millet system'],
  t:['一个信伊斯兰教的帝国,让希腊正教、亚美尼亚教会和犹太社群各管各的人——各自的法庭、学校和税。',
     'A Muslim empire that let the Greek Orthodox, the Armenians and the Jews each govern their own — their own courts, their own schools, their own taxes.'],
  a:[['1453 年拿下君士坦丁堡后,穆罕默德二世任命了新的希腊正教牧首;亚美尼亚教会与犹太社群后来也各有首领。要说明的是,「米利特」作为一套成文制度的名称要到 19 世纪才通用,把它整套上溯到 1453 年是后世的追述。各社群按自己的宗教法处理婚姻、继承与内部纠纷,非穆斯林缴人头税(吉兹亚)以代兵役——德夫希尔梅征去的少年是例外,他们入伍时即改信伊斯兰教。',
      'After taking Constantinople in 1453 Mehmed II appointed a new Greek Orthodox patriarch; the Armenian church and the Jewish community later had heads of their own. It should be said that millet as the name of a codified system only came into general use in the nineteenth century, and projecting the whole of it back to 1453 is a later reading. Each community settled marriage, inheritance and its own disputes by its own religious law, and non-Muslims paid the jizya in place of military service — the boys taken by the devshirme were the exception, and became Muslims on entering service.'],
     ['1492 年西班牙驱逐犹太人,奥斯曼苏丹下令各地接纳,大批人迁到萨洛尼卡与伊斯坦布尔——萨洛尼卡此后四百年是地中海最大的犹太人城市之一。',
      'When Spain expelled its Jews in 1492 the sultan ordered that they be received, and large numbers settled at Salonica and Istanbul; Salonica was for four centuries afterwards one of the largest Jewish cities in the Mediterranean.'],
     ['行政上靠德夫希尔梅(从基督徒村庄征募少年,培养成近卫军与官僚)与蒂玛(骑兵领封地税收、自备装备出征)两套制度,把三个大洲上互不相同的地方接进同一台机器。',
      'The administration ran on two systems: the devshirme, which took boys from Christian villages and made them into guards and officials, and the timar, by which a horseman drew the taxes of a fief and equipped himself for war. Between them they hooked very different places on three continents into one machine.']],
  s:['米利特不是平等:非穆斯林在法律地位、服饰、骑马与作证上都有限制。但把它放回同时代看——欧洲正在为宗教打三十年战争、驱逐异端与犹太人——它的宽度是罕见的。它的终点也值得记:19 世纪民族主义兴起后,原本按宗教划分的社群变成了按民族划分的独立诉求,坦齐马特改革想改成人人平等的国民,已经来不及了。',
     'The millet was not equality: non-Muslims were restricted in legal standing, in dress, in riding, in giving testimony. But set beside its own time — Europe fighting the Thirty Years\' War over religion, expelling heretics and Jews — its breadth was unusual. Its ending is worth remembering too: when nationalism came in the nineteenth century, communities defined by religion turned into nations demanding states, and the Tanzimat attempt to make everyone equal subjects came too late.'] },

'开辟新航路': { g:'tech', c:'西班牙·葡萄牙帝国', y:[1415,1522], n:['开辟新航路','Opening the sea routes'],
  t:['一百年时间,两个小国把「地球是可以绕过去的」从设想变成了航海图。',
     'In a hundred years two small countries turned the idea that the globe can be sailed round into a set of charts.'],
  a:[['先要解决的是回程:非洲西岸吹的是东北信风,船南下容易、北上难。葡萄牙人摸索出「沃尔塔」——回航时不贴岸,而是先向西北深入大西洋兜一个大圈,借另一股风回来。轻快的卡拉维尔帆船用三角帆,能顶着风走之字。',
      'The first problem was getting home: the wind off West Africa blows from the north-east, so running south is easy and coming back is not. The Portuguese worked out the volta — instead of hugging the coast on the return, standing far out into the Atlantic to the north-west and catching a different wind. The light caravel with its lateen sails could work to windward.'],
     ['再要解决的是定位:纬度靠正午测太阳高度或夜里测北极星,葡萄牙人编出太阳赤纬表让水手能算;经度当时无解,要到 18 世纪的航海钟才办到。',
      'The second problem was knowing where you were: latitude could be had from the sun at noon or the pole star at night, and the Portuguese drew up declination tables so a seaman could work it out; longitude had no answer until the marine chronometer of the eighteenth century.'],
     ['航路图与针位被当作国家机密。1488 年迪亚士绕过好望角、1498 年达·伽马到印度、1492 年哥伦布向西撞上美洲、1519—1522 年麦哲伦船队完成环球——出发时五条船二百多人,回来时一条船十八人。',
      'Charts and bearings were state secrets. Dias rounded the Cape in 1488, da Gama reached India in 1498, Columbus sailing west struck the Americas in 1492, and the Magellan expedition of 1519–22 went round the world — five ships and over two hundred men out, one ship and eighteen men home.']],
  s:['这不是一次发现,是一套可重复的技术:风系、船型、天文定位与海图合起来,才让远洋航行从冒险变成生意。此后三百年的世界贸易、殖民与物种交换,都建在这套技术上。它也有另一面——同样的船带去了天花与奴隶贸易。',
     'This was not a discovery but a repeatable technique: wind systems, hull design, celestial navigation and charts together turned ocean voyaging from an adventure into a business. Three centuries of world trade, colonisation and the exchange of species were built on it. There is the other side too: the same ships carried smallpox and the slave trade.'] },

'首个全球帝国': { g:'inst', c:'西班牙·葡萄牙帝国', y:[1494,1820], n:['首个全球帝国','The first global empire'],
  t:['1494 年两个国家在一张纸上画了一条线,把还没见过的地球分成两半。',
     'In 1494 two countries drew a line on a piece of paper and split a globe they had not yet seen.'],
  a:[['《托尔德西里亚斯条约》把佛得角群岛以西 370 里格处的经线定为分界:线以东归葡萄牙,以西归西班牙。巴西东部那块凸出的海岸恰好落在线的东边,葡萄牙语因此是今天南美最大国家的语言——今日巴西的绝大部分是后来越线扩张出去的。',
      'The Treaty of Tordesillas set the line 370 leagues west of the Cape Verde islands: east of it to Portugal, west to Spain. The eastern bulge of Brazil fell just on that side, which is why the largest country in South America speaks Portuguese — most of today\'s Brazil was pushed out west of the line later.'],
     ['管理办法是副王:美洲设新西班牙(墨西哥城)与秘鲁(利马)两个副王区,副王代国王行事;传教士与总督同船而行,教会既是精神机构也是行政机构。',
      'It was governed through viceroys: New Spain at Mexico City and Peru at Lima, each viceroy standing in for the king; missionaries sailed with the governors, and the church was an arm of administration as much as of the spirit.'],
     ['1565 年起大帆船已经在跑,1571 年马尼拉建城把亚洲这头的终点定下来,航线才连成闭环:美洲的白银经马尼拉大帆船到亚洲换丝绸瓷器,再经欧洲回流——同一枚八里亚尔银元,可能在墨西哥铸造、在广州付账。',
      'The galleons had been sailing since 1565, and with the founding of Manila in 1571 the Asian end was fixed and the routes closed into a circle: American silver went across the Pacific on the galleon to buy Asian silk and porcelain, and came round through Europe again — the same piece of eight might be struck in Mexico and spent in Canton.']],
  s:['它是第一个「日不落」帝国,也是第一个把四大洲绑进同一条价格链的政治体:安第斯山里挖出的银子,能影响明朝的税制。代价是本地人的世界:美洲原住民人口在一个世纪内因疾病与劳役锐减,非洲人被成批运过大西洋填补劳力。这两件事和「全球」这个词是同一件事的两面。',
     'It was the first empire on which the sun did not set, and the first polity to tie four continents into one chain of prices: silver dug in the Andes could move the tax system of Ming China. The cost was the world of the people already there: the native population of the Americas collapsed within a century from disease and forced labour, and Africans were shipped across the Atlantic in numbers to replace them. Those two facts and the word global are the same thing seen from two sides.'] },

'成文宪法': { g:'inst', c:'美国', y:[1787,2025], n:['成文宪法','A written constitution'],
  t:['一份四千多词的文件,管一个国家管了两百多年——世界上还在用的成文国家宪法里,它最老。',
     'A document of some four thousand words that has run a country for over two centuries — the oldest written national constitution still in force.'],
  a:[['1787 年费城制宪会议,五十五名代表关起门来开了四个月。争得最凶的是大州与小州怎么分权,最后妥协成两院:众议院按人口、参议院每州两席。',
      'Fifty-five delegates met behind closed doors at Philadelphia for four months in 1787. The fiercest fight was between large states and small, settled by a compromise of two chambers: the House by population, the Senate two seats to every state.'],
     ['它的核心设计是让权力互相咬住:立法、行政、司法三权分立,总统可以否决法案、国会可以三分之二推翻否决、法院可以判法律违宪。1803 年马伯里诉麦迪逊案确立了法院的这项权力——宪法本身并没有明写。',
      'Its core is powers that catch on one another: legislature, executive and judiciary separated, the president able to veto a bill, Congress able to override by two-thirds, the courts able to strike a law down as unconstitutional. That last power was established by Marbury v. Madison in 1803 — the constitution does not spell it out.'],
     ['它留了修改的口子:1791 年一次通过十条修正案(权利法案),此后两百多年只再通过十七条。第十三条(1865)废除奴隶制——这一条是打完内战才改得动的。',
      'It left a way to change itself: ten amendments at once in 1791, the Bill of Rights, and only seventeen more in the two centuries since. The thirteenth, in 1865, abolished slavery — and it took a civil war to get it.']],
  s:['它被反复借鉴,也被反复批评。借鉴的是「把规则写下来、并规定怎么改规则」这个思路——19 世纪以后新建的国家几乎都有一部成文宪法。批评的是它写成时把奴隶制、原住民与女性排除在外,而修改的门槛高到让纠正需要几代人。原件保存在华盛顿的国家档案馆,每天有人排队去看那四页纸。',
     'It has been borrowed from and criticised in equal measure. What is borrowed is the idea of writing the rules down and writing down how to change them — nearly every state founded since the nineteenth century has a written constitution. What is criticised is that as written it left out the enslaved, the native peoples and women, and set the bar for amendment so high that putting that right took generations. The original is in the National Archives in Washington, and people queue every day to look at the four sheets.'] },

'登月': { g:'tech', c:'美国', y:[1969], n:['登月','The Moon landing'],
  t:['1969 年 7 月 20 日,两个人踩在月球上,地球上大约六亿人在电视机前看着。',
     'On 20 July 1969 two men stood on the Moon, and something like six hundred million people watched it on television.'],
  a:[['起因是输在起跑线上:1957 年苏联发射第一颗人造卫星、1961 年送第一个人进太空,1961 年 5 月肯尼迪向国会宣布,美国要在这个十年结束前把人送上月球再安全带回。',
      'It began from being behind: the Soviets put up the first satellite in 1957 and the first man in space in 1961, and in May 1961 Kennedy told Congress that America would land a man on the Moon and bring him home before the decade was out.'],
     ['阿波罗计划高峰期雇了四十万人、两万余家承包商与大学参与,花费按当时币值约 254 亿美元。土星五号火箭高 110 米,至今仍是把人送出地球轨道的最大火箭。',
      'At its height Apollo employed four hundred thousand people across some twenty thousand firms and universities, at a cost of about $25.4 billion in the money of the day. The Saturn V stood 110 metres tall and is still the largest rocket ever to carry people beyond Earth orbit.'],
     ['阿波罗 11 号的登月舱计算机内存只有几十千字节,着陆时还因为超载报警;阿姆斯特朗手动操纵,在燃料剩下几十秒时落地。计划结束时带回了 382 公斤月岩,分给全世界一百多个实验室研究,今天还有一部分封存着没动过——留给以后更好的仪器。',
      'The lunar module computer had a few tens of kilobytes of memory and threw overload alarms during the descent; Armstrong flew the last part by hand and touched down with well under a minute of fuel to spare. The programme brought back 382 kilograms of rock, shared out to more than a hundred laboratories around the world; some of it is still sealed and untouched, kept for better instruments than we have yet.']],
  s:['它是冷战的产物,也是人类第一次离开自己的星球站到另一个天体上。带回的月岩改写了月球起源的研究(今天主流的「大碰撞说」建立在它们之上);而 1972 年之后,再没有人在月面上落过脚——2026 年有人载人绕月飞了一圈,但没有落地。地面上留下的东西倒是还在:月面的反射镜至今被地球上的激光测距使用,用来测量地月距离,精度到厘米。',
     'It was a product of the Cold War and also the first time human beings left their own planet and stood on another body. The rocks brought back rewrote the study of the Moon\'s origin — the giant-impact hypothesis now generally accepted rests on them. And since 1972 no one has set foot on it — a crew flew round the Moon in 2026, but did not land. Something left there is still working: the retroreflectors on the surface are still used by lasers from Earth to measure the distance to the Moon, to within centimetres.'] },

'互联网': { g:'tech', c:'美国', y:[1969,2025], n:['互联网','The Internet'],
  t:['它最初是几台大学计算机之间的连线,第一次传输就死机了——只传出去两个字母。',
     'It began as a link between a few university computers, and the first transmission crashed — two letters got through.'],
  a:[['1969 年 10 月 29 日,加州大学洛杉矶分校向斯坦福研究所发出第一条信息,想输入 LOGIN,系统在传完 L 和 O 之后崩溃。这套网络叫 ARPANET,由美国国防部高级研究计划局出资。',
      'On 29 October 1969 a machine at UCLA sent the first message to Stanford Research Institute: the operator meant to type LOGIN, and the system crashed after the L and the O. The network was ARPANET, paid for by the Defense Department\'s advanced research agency.'],
     ['关键不是那根线,是规则:1974 年瑟夫与卡恩提出 TCP/IP,让不同厂家、不同技术的网络能互相说话——「互联网」这个词指的正是「网络之间」。它不设中心,任何一段断了,数据绕路走。',
      'What mattered was not the wire but the rules: in 1974 Cerf and Kahn set out TCP/IP so that networks of different makes and technologies could talk to one another — the word internet means between networks. It has no centre; if a link goes down the packets go round.'],
     ['1989—1991 年,在瑞士的欧洲核子研究中心工作的英国人蒂姆·伯纳斯-李发明了万维网(网页、链接、浏览器),1993 年那套软件被宣布免费开放、任何人可用——这一步才让普通人用上它。',
      'Between 1989 and 1991 Tim Berners-Lee, an Englishman working at CERN in Switzerland, invented the World Wide Web — pages, links, a browser — and in 1993 the software was put into the public domain for anyone to use. That is the step that brought it to ordinary people.']],
  s:['它是一个反复被讲错的故事:互联网不是为了核战争后仍能通信而造的(那是 1960 年代另一项研究的动机,常被混为一谈),万维网也不是美国造的。它真正特别的地方在于:出钱的是国家,做事的是大学,而最后决定它样子的,是「协议公开、任何人可接入」这个选择。今天全球约六十亿人在用同一套 1974 年定下的规则(国际电信联盟 2025 年数)。',
     'It is a story often told wrong: the internet was not built to survive a nuclear war — that was the motive of a different piece of 1960s research, and the two are constantly conflated — and the Web was not American. What is genuinely unusual about it is that the state paid, the universities built, and what settled its shape in the end was a choice: publish the protocol, let anyone connect. Some six billion people now use rules laid down in 1974 (ITU figure for 2025).'] },

'首颗人造卫星': { g:'tech', c:'俄罗斯·苏联', y:[1957], n:['首颗人造卫星','The first artificial satellite'],
  t:['一个直径 58 厘米的铝球,绕着地球「哔——哔——」响了三个星期,把整个世界吓了一跳。',
     'An aluminium sphere 58 centimetres across went round the Earth beeping for three weeks, and gave the whole world a fright.'],
  a:[['1957 年 10 月 4 日发射,重 83.6 公斤,球上四根天线,里面只有电池和两台无线电发射机——它没带科学仪器,唯一的任务是让人听见它(信号的长短里编进了舱内温度与气压)。',
      'Launched on 4 October 1957, it weighed 83.6 kilograms: a sphere with four aerials, and inside it batteries and two radio transmitters. It carried no scientific instruments; its only task was to be heard — though the length of its pulses encoded the temperature and pressure inside.'],
     ['信号在 20 和 40 兆赫,普通业余无线电爱好者用自家设备就能收到——这是刻意的设计。它绕地球一圈 96 分钟,三个星期后电池耗尽,1958 年 1 月坠入大气层烧毁。',
      'It transmitted on 20 and 40 megahertz, so that any amateur with his own set could pick it up — a deliberate choice. It circled the Earth every 96 minutes, went silent when the batteries ran down after three weeks, and burned up on re-entry in January 1958.'],
     ['它是从 R-7 洲际导弹改的运载火箭发射的,这一点让美国最不安:能把 83 公斤送上轨道的火箭,也能把弹头送到美国。一个月后苏联又送上去一只叫莱卡的狗。',
      'It went up on a launcher adapted from the R-7 intercontinental missile, which was what alarmed Washington most: a rocket that can put 83 kilograms in orbit can put a warhead on America. A month later the Soviets sent up a dog, Laika.']],
  s:['这颗球引发的连锁反应比它本身大得多:美国次年成立 NASA、大改中小学的数学与科学课程,并加速了导弹与航天计划。「-nik」这个俄语后缀也进了英语,一时什么都能加。今天绕地球飞的卫星有上万颗,导航、气象、通信、遥感都靠它们——都是从这个只会「哔哔」响的铝球开始的。',
     'What it set off was far larger than the thing itself: the United States founded NASA the following year, overhauled school mathematics and science, and poured money into missiles and space. The Russian suffix -nik even entered English for a while, attachable to anything. There are tens of thousands of satellites overhead today, carrying navigation, weather, communications and imaging — all descended from an aluminium ball that could only beep.'] },

'首次载人航天': { g:'tech', c:'俄罗斯·苏联', y:[1961], n:['首次载人航天','The first man in space'],
  t:['1961 年 4 月 12 日,一个二十七岁的人绕地球飞了一圈,用了一百零八分钟。',
     'On 12 April 1961 a man of twenty-seven went once round the Earth, and it took a hundred and eight minutes.'],
  a:[['尤里·加加林乘东方一号升空,轨道最高约 327 公里。全程由地面和自动程序控制,他基本不操作——当时没人知道人在失重下还能不能正常思考和行动。',
      'Yuri Gagarin went up in Vostok 1, to a high point of about 327 kilometres. The flight was run from the ground and by automatic sequence; he did almost nothing himself — nobody yet knew whether a man could think or act properly in weightlessness.'],
     ['返回时他在七公里高度弹射出舱、自己跳伞落地,而不是坐在舱里落下。这一点当时对外没说——国际航空联合会的纪录要求飞行员与航天器一同着陆,苏联为了拿下纪录隐瞒了细节,直到 1970 年代才承认。',
      'On the way down he ejected at seven kilometres and came to earth by parachute rather than inside the capsule. That was not made public at the time: the international record rules required the pilot to land with his craft, and the detail was concealed to secure the record — admitted only in the 1970s.'],
     ['他落在萨拉托夫州的一片农田里,穿着橙色宇航服。据记载,最先见到他的是一位农妇和她的孙女,他说的第一句话是让她们别怕,自己是苏联人,刚从太空回来。',
      'He came down in a field in Saratov province in an orange suit. By the accounts, the first people he met were a farm woman and her granddaughter, and the first thing he said was not to be afraid — he was a Soviet man, just back from space.']],
  s:['他一夜之间成为全世界最有名的人,此后主要在做象征性的工作,1968 年在一次训练飞行中失事身亡,年仅三十四。4 月 12 日现在是「载人空间飞行国际日」。两年后苏联又把第一位女性(捷列什科娃)送上太空——比美国早了二十年。',
     'He became overnight the most famous man alive, spent the years after largely on ceremonial duty, and was killed in a training flight in 1968 at thirty-four. 12 April is now the International Day of Human Space Flight. Two years after him the Soviets sent up the first woman, Valentina Tereshkova — twenty years before the Americans did.'] },

'尼尼微图书馆': { g:'obj', c:'亚述', y:[-668,-612], n:['尼尼微图书馆','The library of Nineveh'],
  t:['一座图书馆被大火烧过之后,反而留了下来。','A library that survived because it burned.'],
  a:[['亚述巴尼拔给两河各地的城市写信,让他们把当地写过的东西抄一份送来;抄不来的就直接从巴比伦尼亚的神庙和私人手里取走。大英博物馆按「泥板」计有 30943 件,不过很多是同一篇文献的碎片——学者估计原来大约是一万篇',
      'Ashurbanipal wrote to cities across Mesopotamia telling them to send copies of everything written there, and what could not be copied was taken outright from temples and private hands in Babylonia. The British Museum counts 30,943 tablets, but many are fragments of the same text — scholars reckon the collection held some ten thousand works'],
     ['泥板本来只是晒干的,泡水就烂。前 612 年尼尼微被攻破、王宫烧了起来,火把泥板烤成了陶——今天还读得到,正是因为那场火;写在蜡板上的那部分就没有这个运气,全没了',
      'The tablets were only sun-dried and would have dissolved in water. When Nineveh fell in 612 BCE the palace burned, and the fire baked the clay hard — they can be read today because of it. What had been written on wax boards had no such luck and is entirely gone'],
     ['1849 年莱亚德先在辛那赫里布的宫里挖到一批,1852 年拉萨姆在亚述巴尼拔的宫里挖到另一批。当时没有留下出土记录,两批运到欧洲后彻底混在一起,到今天也分不清哪一块出自哪个房间',
      'Layard found one group in the palace of Sennacherib in 1849 and Rassam another in Ashurbanipal\'s in 1852. No record was made of where anything came out of the ground, and once in Europe the two were mixed beyond repair — no one can now say which room a given piece came from']],
  s:['1872 年,大英博物馆一位原本靠刻钞票版谋生的年轻人乔治·史密斯,在碎片里读出了一段大洪水的故事:神要毁掉世界,有人事先得到警告造了船,水退后放鸟出去找陆地。他 12 月 3 日把译文念给圣经考古学会听,在座的有当时的首相格莱斯顿。故事缺了一块,第二年《每日电讯报》出钱送他回尼尼微去找那块碎片——他真的找到了。这段文字就是《吉尔伽美什史诗》的第十一块泥板。',
     'In 1872 George Smith, a young man at the British Museum who had made his living engraving banknote plates, read a flood story out of the fragments: the gods meant to destroy the world, a man was warned and built a boat, and when the water fell he sent out birds to look for land. He read his translation to the Society of Biblical Archaeology on 3 December, with the sitting prime minister, Gladstone, in the audience. Part of the story was missing, so the next year the Daily Telegraph paid to send him back to Nineveh to look for it — and he found it. The text is Tablet XI of the Epic of Gilgamesh.'] },
'明治维新': { g:'inst', c:'日本', y:[1868,1912], n:['明治维新','The Meiji Restoration'],
  t:['一个国家决定把自己拆开重装,动手之前先派一百个人出去看别人是怎么装的。','A country set out to take itself apart and rebuild — and before doing so sent a hundred people abroad to see how others had done it.'],
  a:[['1871 年 12 月 23 日,一支约一百人的使节团从横滨出发,带队的是政府最高的那几个人:特命全权大使岩仓具视,副使大久保利通、木户孝允、伊藤博文。他们直到 1873 年 9 月 13 日才回来——新政府成立才三年,却让一半核心成员离开了将近两年',
      'On 23 December 1871 a mission of about a hundred sailed from Yokohama, led by the very top of the government: the ambassador Iwakura Tomomi with Okubo Toshimichi, Kido Takayoshi and Ito Hirobumi as his deputies. They did not return until 13 September 1873 — a government only three years old had let half its core leadership go abroad for nearly two years'],
     ['出发时的头号任务是改掉幕末签下的不平等条约,这件事彻底失败,还为此在美国多耗了几个月。任务办不成,他们就改成一路看:工厂、铁路、议会、学校、造船厂,走了美、英、法、比、荷、普鲁士、丹麦、瑞典、奥地利、意大利、瑞士。随行的久米邦武把见闻逐日记了下来',
      'The first aim was to renegotiate the unequal treaties signed at the end of the shogunate; it failed outright and cost them extra months in the United States. With the errand lost they turned to looking instead — factories, railways, parliaments, schools, shipyards — through the United States, Britain, France, Belgium, the Netherlands, Prussia, Denmark, Sweden, Austria, Italy and Switzerland. Kume Kunitake, travelling with them, wrote down what they saw day by day'],
     ['同船还有五十来人是随行的学生与随员,其中五个女孩留在美国读书。最小的津田梅子出发那年六岁;她 1900 年回日本办起了自己的女子学校',
      'Some fifty more travelled with them as students and attendants, five of them girls who stayed on in the United States to study. The youngest, Tsuda Umeko, was six the year she left; in 1900 she came home and founded a school for women of her own']],
  s:['回来的人得出的结论是:条约改不动,是因为国力还不够——所以先做国内。此后二十年是一连串自上而下的改造:征兵、学校、铁路、工厂、宪法。而条约里的领事裁判权直到 1899 年才完全废除,离使节团出发已经二十八年。「先把自己做起来,再回头谈条件」这条路,后来被不少后发国家反复参照。',
     'The lesson they brought home was that the treaties would not move because the country was not yet strong enough — so home came first. The two decades that followed brought conscription, schools, railways, factories and a constitution, one after another and all from the top. Extraterritoriality under the treaties was not fully ended until 1899, twenty-eight years after the mission sailed. That sequence — build yourself up first, renegotiate afterwards — has been looked to again and again by countries that industrialised late.'] },
'布吉毕姆养鳗水渠': { g:'tech', c:'澳大利亚原住民', y:[-4600,1788], ca:1, n:['布吉毕姆养鳗水渠','The eel channels of Budj Bim'],
  t:['贡迪杰马拉人把一片火山熔岩地改成了养鳗鱼的水利工程,时间比金字塔还早。','The Gunditjmara turned a lava field into waterworks for farming eels, and did it before the pyramids.'],
  a:[['这片地是布吉毕姆火山喷出来的,熔岩流大约在三万六千九百年前铺开(误差三千一百年)。熔岩地表凹凸不平、到处存得住水——正是这个地形让改水路成为可能:顺着熔岩的走向开渠、筑坝,把达洛特溪的水引进低洼处',
      'The ground is lava from the Budj Bim volcano, laid down around 36,900 years ago, give or take 3,100. A lava field is uneven and holds water in its hollows, and that is what made the engineering possible: channels cut and dams built along the run of the rock led Darlot Creek into the low ground'],
     ['渠道有几百米长,拦水的坝墙是玄武岩块砌的,有几十道。捕的是短鳍鳗(当地叫 kooyang),用编的笼子;捕上来常挂在马纳桉的树洞里用烟熏——熏过的鳗鱼存得住、带得走,不必当天吃完',
      'The channels run for hundreds of metres, and dozens of dam walls were built of basalt blocks. The catch was the short-finned eel, kooyang, taken in woven traps and often smoked in the hollows of manna gums — smoked eel keeps and travels, and need not be eaten the day it is caught'],
     ['1977 年一场大雨冲开地表,除了更多渠道,还露出玄武岩块砌的房屋地基——这说明人不是季节性路过,而是住在这里。已测出的年代约六千六百年前;另有研究据花粉分析主张更早,接近八千年',
      'Heavy rain in 1977 stripped back the surface and revealed more of the channels — and house foundations built of basalt blocks, which means people lived here rather than passing through with the seasons. The dated remains go back about 6,600 years; other work, based on pollen, argues for something closer to 8,000']],
  s:['贡迪杰马拉人 1989 年就自己提出要申报世界遗产,等了三十年:2019 年 7 月 6 日列入,依据是文化标准第三与第五条。这是澳大利亚第一处只因原住民文化价值而入选的世界遗产——卡卡杜和乌鲁鲁虽然早就在名录上,但当年主要是按自然价值报的,住在那里的人和他们的历史算附带项。',
     'The Gunditjmara put the place forward for World Heritage listing themselves in 1989 and waited thirty years: it was inscribed on 6 July 2019 under cultural criteria (iii) and (v). It is the first site in Australia listed for Aboriginal cultural values alone — Kakadu and Uluru had been on the list far longer, but were nominated chiefly for their natural values, with the people living there and their history counted as secondary.'] },
'现代大学': { g:'inst', c:'德意志', y:[1810,2025], n:['现代大学','The research university'],
  t:['1810 年柏林新办的一所大学,通常被认为是第一所把「教书」和「做研究」绑成一件事的学校。','A university founded in Berlin in 1810, generally reckoned the first to bind teaching and research into a single job.'],
  a:[['威廉·冯·洪堡 1810 年在柏林办起这所学校,主张是「教学与研究的统一」:老师在课上讲的,应当就是他自己正在研究的东西;而研究本身不受意识形态、经济、政治或宗教左右。他请来的头一批教授里有施莱尔马赫、萨维尼、费希特和尼布尔',
      'Wilhelm von Humboldt founded it in Berlin in 1810 on the principle of the unity of teaching and research: what a professor taught should be what he was himself investigating, and the investigation should answer to no ideology, economy, politics or church. Among the first men he appointed were Schleiermacher, Savigny, Fichte and Niebuhr'],
     ['配套的两样东西后来传遍全世界:研讨课——一小群人围着一个题目争论,而不是坐着听讲;以及实验室——学生自己上手做,而不只是看老师演示。学生还可以自己挑课、挑老师,这一条德语叫 Lernfreiheit,学习的自由',
      'Two things that came with it spread everywhere: the seminar, a small group arguing over one problem instead of sitting and listening; and the teaching laboratory, where students worked with their own hands rather than watching a demonstration. Students could also choose their courses and their teachers — Lernfreiheit, the freedom to learn'],
     ['中世纪的大学是师生结成的行会,管的是资格与自治;这一所加进去的是「新知识必须由学校自己生产」。1876 年创办的约翰斯·霍普金斯大学,是美国最早照这套办法办学的学校之一,美国的研究型大学与博士制度由此定型',
      'The medieval university had been a guild of masters and students, concerned with qualifications and self-government; what this one added was that new knowledge had to be produced inside the institution itself. Johns Hopkins, founded in 1876, was among the first American universities built on the model, and the American research university and its doctorate took shape from it']],
  s:['今天多数大学的默认设定——研究生跟着导师做课题、靠一篇原创论文拿学位——都是在这里定的型。不过要留一句:研究者指出,洪堡的设想从未被完整实现过,今天挂在他名下的那一整套说法,有相当一部分是后来的人回头追认与理想化的结果。1933 年之后德语学界大批学者被驱逐或流亡,这套模式的重心也随之移到了美国。',
     'The default arrangement in most universities today — a graduate student working under a supervisor and taking a degree on an original piece of work — was fixed here. One caution belongs with it: scholars point out that Humboldt\'s conception was never realised in full, and that much of what now travels under his name was read back into it later. After 1933 the expulsion and flight of scholars from the German-speaking world moved the centre of the model to the United States.'] },
'伊什塔尔门': { g:'obj', c:'新巴比伦', y:[-569,-539], ca:1, n:['伊什塔尔门','The Ishtar Gate'],
  t:['一整座城门用蓝釉砖砌成,砖上凸出来的动物是先在模子里压出来的。','A whole city gate of blue-glazed brick, its animals pressed in moulds before firing.'],
  a:[['尼布甲尼撒二世约在前 569 年把它建在巴比伦城北。门高约 15 米,地基还往下埋了 14 米;门外接着一条游行大道,长八百多米,最宽处约 20 米',
      'Nebuchadnezzar II built it on the north side of Babylon around 569 BCE. The gate stood some fifteen metres high with another fourteen metres of foundation below ground, and outside it ran a processional way over eight hundred metres long and up to twenty metres wide'],
     ['蓝色的釉仿的是青金石的颜色,那是当时最贵的石头。砖先压进木模、晒干,再入窑烧;一头狮子由四十六块模压的砖拼成,排十一层,砖缝用沥青封住。门面上交替排着穆什胡什(mušḫuššu)龙、野牛和狮子,分别属于马尔杜克、阿达德和伊什塔尔三位神',
      'The blue glaze imitates lapis lazuli, the most expensive stone of its day. Bricks were pressed into wooden forms, dried in the sun and fired; one lion is made of forty-six moulded bricks in eleven courses, and the joints were sealed with bitumen. Rows of mushhushshu dragons, aurochs and lions alternate across the face, belonging to the gods Marduk, Adad and Ishtar'],
     ['1902 到 1914 年,德国人科尔德威把碎砖一箱一箱运回柏林,在佩加蒙博物馆拼了起来。但拼出来的只是前面那道较小的门,高 14 米、宽 30 米;后面更大的那一道没有一起立起来,长期存放在库房',
      'Between 1902 and 1914 the German excavator Robert Koldewey shipped the broken brick back to Berlin crate by crate and it was reassembled in the Pergamon Museum. What stands there is only the smaller front gate, fourteen metres high and thirty wide; the larger one behind it was never raised with it and has long been kept in store']],
  s:['今天一提古巴比伦,多数人脑子里浮出来的画面就是这道蓝墙——而它立在柏林,不在巴比伦。遗址留在原地、遗物运往别处,是十九世纪末那一代考古的常规做法,至今仍是有争议的事。留在伊拉克的那座城,现在主要是土丘,以及后人重新砌起来的墙。',
     'For most people the picture that comes with the words ancient Babylon is this blue wall — and it stands in Berlin, not in Babylon. Site in one place and finds in another was standard practice for that generation of archaeologists, and it is argued over still. What is left on the ground in Iraq is mostly mounds, and walls rebuilt by later hands.'] },
'天命观': { g:'idea', c:'西周', y:[-1046,-256], n:['天命观','The Mandate of Heaven'],
  t:['一句用来解释「凭什么轮到我们」的话,后来变成了推翻政权的理由。','An argument for why it was their turn to rule — which later became the argument for overthrowing rulers.'],
  a:[['商人问事靠占卜,问的是祖先和「帝」,德行不在问题之内。周人灭商之后必须回答一个新问题:商为什么会输?他们给的答案是——上天把统治权交给谁,看的是德行,不看是谁家的后代。康王时铸的大盂鼎上已经写着「受天有大令」',
      'The Shang put their questions to ancestors and to Di by divination, and virtue did not come into it. Having destroyed the Shang, the Zhou had a new question to answer — why had the Shang lost? — and their answer was that Heaven grants the right to rule for conduct, not for descent. The Da Yu ding, cast under King Kang, already carries the words received from Heaven the great command'],
     ['这套说法自带一个把柄:既然能给,就能收回。周人自己在《诗经·大雅·文王》里写下「天命靡常」,又写「宜鉴于殷」——拿商朝当镜子照照自己。后来饥荒、地震、洪水都被读作上天不满意的信号',
      'The claim came with a handle on it: what can be given can be taken back. The Zhou wrote it themselves in the Book of Songs — the mandate is not constant — and added that Yin, the Shang, should be looked at as a mirror. Famine, earthquake and flood came to be read as signs that Heaven was displeased'],
     ['到《周易》的彖传里,这把刀已经反过来用了:「汤武革命,顺乎天而应乎人」——推翻一个王朝被写成了顺天应人的正当举动。「革命」两个字的本义,就是改掉天命',
      'By the commentaries on the Book of Changes the blade had been turned round: Tang and Wu changed the mandate, in accord with Heaven and in answer to the people — the overthrow of a dynasty written up as the right thing to do. The Chinese word for revolution, geming, means exactly that: to change the mandate']],
  s:['此后每一次改朝换代,新朝都要先论证前朝已经失了天命,这套论证成了固定程式。有意思的是它并没有被原样搬走:日本在七世纪末八世纪初照唐朝的样子立律令,几乎整套都学了,唯独没有接受「天命可以转移」这一条——天皇的正当性被安放在出身上,而不是德行上。',
     'Every change of dynasty afterwards opened by arguing that the previous one had lost the mandate, until the argument became a fixed form. It did not travel unchanged, though: when Japan built its law codes on the Tang model around the turn of the eighth century it took almost everything else and left this out — the emperor\'s legitimacy was placed in descent rather than in conduct.'] },
'科尔多瓦大清真寺': { g:'obj', c:'后倭马亚·安达卢斯', y:[785,2025], n:['科尔多瓦大清真寺','The Great Mosque of Córdoba'],
  t:['红白条纹的拱门排成一片柱子的森林——当了四百五十年清真寺,后来又当了将近八百年教堂。','A forest of columns under red-and-white striped arches — four and a half centuries a mosque, then nearly eight hundred years a cathedral.'],
  a:[['条纹不是画上去的,是砌出来的:红砖与白石一段段交替,拱本身就是双色的。785 年动工,此后两百年里三次扩建,每次都顺着原有的柱网往外长;扩到最后,它是当时伊斯兰世界最大的清真寺之一',
      'The stripes are not painted but built: red brick and pale stone alternate through the arch itself. Begun in 785 and enlarged three times over the next two centuries, each time along the existing grid of columns, it ended among the largest mosques in the Islamic world of its day'],
     ['961 年起哈卡姆二世做了最讲究的一次扩建:他向拜占庭皇帝请来镶嵌工匠,据后来的阿拉伯史书记载,皇帝还随人送来了成批的彩色玻璃料;米哈拉布(指示礼拜方向的壁龛)周围至今铺满金底的马赛克',
      'From 961 al-Hakam II made the most refined of the enlargements: he asked the Byzantine emperor for mosaic craftsmen, and later Arabic chronicles record that batches of coloured glass came with them; the mihrab — the niche marking the direction of prayer — is still surrounded by mosaics on gold ground'],
     ['它有一个建筑史上争论不休的怪处:米哈拉布不朝麦加,而是明显偏南。解释有好几种——沿用了老家大马士革清真寺的朝向、顺着罗马旧城的街网——至今没有定论',
      'It keeps one oddity architectural historians still argue over: the mihrab does not face Mecca but points well to the south. Explanations vary — following the orientation of the home mosque in Damascus, or the street grid of the old Roman town — and none has settled it']],
  s:['马蹄拱与红白条纹拱从这里传遍安达卢斯,又过海传入北非,成了这一带建筑的标志。这座建筑今天的正式身份是天主教主教座堂,但科尔多瓦人一直叫它「梅斯基塔」——西班牙语里的「清真寺」。一个名字里装着两段历史,城里人并不觉得矛盾。',
     'The horseshoe arch and the striped arch spread from here across al-Andalus and over the sea into North Africa, becoming the signature of the region\'s building. Officially the building today is a Catholic cathedral, but the people of Córdoba have always called it La Mezquita — Spanish for mosque. Two histories in one name, and the town sees no contradiction in it.'] },
'驿传制度': { g:'inst', c:'马穆鲁克', y:[1260,1517], n:['驿传制度','The barid courier network'],
  t:['马在地上跑,鸽子在天上飞,烽火在山头亮——三层通信网,只为苏丹一个人服务。','Horses on the roads, pigeons in the air, fire beacons on the hilltops — three layers of communication, all serving the sultan alone.'],
  a:[['「巴利德」不是马穆鲁克的发明——阿拔斯的哈里发早就办过官方驿传,后来废弛了。拜巴尔在与蒙古开战的年代把它重建起来:修桥补路,沿线设驿站养马,信使一路换马不换人,专送军情与政令',
      'The barid was not a Mamluk invention — the Abbasid caliphs had run an official post long before, and it had lapsed. Baybars rebuilt it in the years of the Mongol wars: bridges and roads repaired, relay stations keeping fresh horses along the routes, couriers changing mounts but never riders, carrying military intelligence and state orders only'],
     ['最急的消息走空中:信写在小纸条上,卷起来绑在信鸽身上,沿线鸽塔一站站接力。鸽子是登记在册的——哪只出自哪一窝、认哪条路,都有档案',
      'The most urgent news went by air: messages on small slips of paper, rolled and tied to carrier pigeons relayed from loft to loft along the routes. The pigeons were kept on the books — which loft each came from, which road it knew, all on file'],
     ['防蒙古的预警另有一层:从幼发拉底河一线到开罗,山头设烽火台,敌军一动,火光一座传一座,比任何骑手都快。三层各跑各的,互为备份——断了一层,消息还有两条路可走',
      'Warning against the Mongols had a layer of its own: beacon towers ran from the Euphrates line down to Cairo, and when an army moved, the fire passed from hilltop to hilltop faster than any rider. The three layers ran separately and backed one another up — cut one, and the news still had two roads home']],
  s:['这张网把埃及与叙利亚两大块领土捏成一个反应灵敏的整体,伊尔汗国几十年里屡攻叙利亚而不能得手,消息总比敌军先到一步是原因之一。王朝末年财政枯竭,驿站荒废,网先于国家散掉。词却留了下来:「巴利德」在今天的阿拉伯语里就是「邮政」——在阿拉伯国家寄信,用的还是这个词。',
     'The network pulled Egypt and Syria into one quick-reacting whole; that the Ilkhanate attacked Syria for decades without holding it owes something to word that kept arriving ahead of the armies it warned of. In the sultanate\'s last years the treasury dried up, the stations decayed, and the network fell apart before the state did. The word survived: barid is simply Arabic for post today — address a letter in an Arabic country and that is still the word on it.'] },
'山地要塞体系': { g:'tech', c:'马拉塔', y:[1646,1818], n:['山地要塞体系','The hill-fort system'],
  t:['平原上打不过,就把国家搬上山顶:都城在山上,金库在山上,水也存在山上。','Beaten on the plains, they moved the state itself to the hilltops: capital on a mountain, treasury on a mountain, water stored on the mountain too.'],
  a:[['希瓦吉十几岁就夺下了第一座山堡托尔纳(1646 年前后,各书记载略有出入),此后一生不停地夺城、修城、筑新城。西高止山帮了大忙:黑色玄武岩的悬崖本身就是城墙,工匠只需要把上得去人的缺口补上',
      'Shivaji took his first hill fort, Torna, in his teens — around 1646, the sources differ slightly — and spent the rest of his life taking, repairing and building forts. The Western Ghats did much of the work: cliffs of black basalt are walls ready made, and the masons only had to close the gaps a man could climb'],
     ['要塞的门道全在细节里:大门不开在正面,藏在突出的城垛后面,进门的路故意拐弯,战象没有直线可以助跑;山顶凿出连片的储水池接住雨季的雨,旱季照样守得住。都城赖加德本身就是一座八百多米高的山堡',
      'The craft was in the details: gates set off to the side behind projecting bastions, approach paths deliberately bent so a war elephant had no straight run; cisterns cut into the summit caught the monsoon rains and kept a garrison through the dry months. Raigad, the capital, was itself a hill fort over eight hundred metres up'],
     ['管理办法防的是人心:每座要塞由三名官员共管,一个主防务、一个管文书账目、一个管仓储,三人地位相当、互相牵制——想献城投敌,先得说服另外两个人',
      'The administration guarded against men, not just armies: each fort was run by three officers of equal standing — one for defence, one for records and accounts, one for stores — so betraying a fort meant persuading two colleagues first']],
  s:['1818 年第三次英马战争后,英国人特意炸毁了许多要塞的城门与工事——他们清楚这套体系的分量。2025 年入选世界遗产名录的十二座要塞里,十一座在马哈拉施特拉,另一座远在南方泰米尔纳德的金吉——那是这套体系伸得最远的一个脚印。',
     'After the third Anglo-Maratha war ended in 1818 the British made a point of blowing up gates and works at many of the forts — they understood what the system had been worth. Of the twelve forts that entered the World Heritage list in 2025, eleven are in Maharashtra and one is far south at Gingee in Tamil Nadu — the system\'s longest footprint.'] },
'唐蕃会盟碑': { g:'obj', c:'吐蕃', y:[823], n:['唐蕃会盟碑','The Tang–Tibetan treaty pillar'],
  t:['同一份和约,用汉藏两种文字刻在同一块石头上;当年立的三块碑,只有这一块还站着。','One peace treaty cut into one stone in two scripts, Chinese and Tibetan; of the three pillars set up, this is the only one still standing.'],
  a:[['821 年与 822 年,双方先后在长安与逻些两地设坛会盟,823 年刻石立碑。据记载同样的碑立了三块:长安一块、两国交界处一块、逻些一块——今天只剩逻些的这块,立在大昭寺门前',
      'In 821 and 822 the two courts held the oath ceremony first at Chang\'an and then at Lhasa, and in 823 the treaty was cut in stone. The records say three identical pillars were raised — at Chang\'an, on the border, and at Lhasa — and only the Lhasa stone remains, standing before the Jokhang'],
     ['正面是盟文,汉文在右、藏文在左,一句对一句;南北两侧刻着双方与盟官员的名单与职衔。约定的核心几句很朴素:各守本境,互不举兵,互不侵夺对方的土地与人口',
      'The front carries the treaty, Chinese on the right and Tibetan on the left, clause for clause; the two flanks carry the names and titles of the officials of both courts who swore. The heart of it is plain: each keeps to its own territory, neither raises arms, neither seizes the other\'s land or people'],
     ['碑文里两国以「甥舅」相称:赞普管唐朝皇帝叫舅舅——文成、金城两位公主先后嫁进吐蕃王室,一份国与国的和约,措辞写成了亲戚之间的约定',
      'In the text the two states address each other as nephew and uncle: the btsanpo calls the Tang emperor his uncle, for two Tang princesses had married into the Tibetan royal house — a treaty between states worded as an understanding between kin']],
  s:['这块碑后来多了一层谁也没预料到的用处:藏文是拼音文字,碑上用藏文拼写的汉地人名与官名,把 9 世纪汉语的读音冻结在了石头上,研究古汉语语音的学者至今要靠它。碑身如今围着护栏,从 823 年立起的那天起,它没有挪过地方。',
     'The pillar later acquired a use no one had planned: Tibetan is a phonetic script, and the Chinese names and titles spelled out in Tibetan on the stone froze the sounds of ninth-century Chinese, so scholars of historical Chinese phonology still depend on it. The stone stands behind a railing now, and it has not moved from the spot where it was raised in 823.'] },
'市舶司海贸': { g:'inst', c:'南宋', y:[971,1279], n:['市舶司海贸','The maritime trade offices'],
  t:['专管海外贸易的衙门:发执照、抽关税、替商船办祈风仪式——朝廷不下海,却把海上的钱管了起来。','The government offices for overseas trade: they issued licences, taxed cargoes and held wind-prayer rites for the merchant fleet — the court never sailed, but it took the sea\'s money in hand.'],
  a:[['市舶这摊事唐代已有雏形,971 年北宋在广州设市舶司,把它变成建制完整的衙门,泉州、明州等港陆续跟上。商船出海先领「公凭」(官方执照),回港先报到:货物按比例抽实物税,叫「抽解」,各时期大体在十分之一上下;贵重货物朝廷还要按官价先买走一部分',
      'The idea had Tang-era roots, but in 971 the Song set up a full trade office at Guangzhou, and Quanzhou, Ningbo and other ports followed. A ship needed an official licence before sailing and had to report back on return: cargoes paid a tax in kind, broadly around one part in ten depending on the period, and the state bought up part of the valuable goods first at official prices'],
     ['朝廷对这笔钱有多上心,皇帝自己说过:《宋会要》记下宋高宗的原话——市舶之利最厚,若措置合宜,所得动以百万计,「岂不胜取之于民」。对一个丢了半壁江山、军费沉重的朝廷,海上的税是救命钱',
      'How much the court cared, the emperor said himself: the Song administrative records preserve Gaozong\'s own words — the profit of the maritime offices is the richest of all, and well managed it runs to the millions, "is that not better than taxing the people?" For a court that had lost half its land and was bleeding money on war, the sea tax was a lifeline'],
     ['泉州的市舶司每年办两次祈风仪式:冬天送船队乘东北季风出洋,夏天迎船队随西南季风回港。官员们到城外九日山下的庙里,向掌管风信的神祈祷,事后把与会者的名字刻上崖壁——南宋的祈风石刻至今留存十方,最早一方刻于 1174 年',
      'At Quanzhou the office held wind-prayer rites twice a year: seeing the fleet out on the northeast monsoon in winter and welcoming it home on the southwest monsoon in summer. The officials went to a temple below Jiuri Hill outside town to pray to the god of the winds, then had the participants\' names cut into the cliff — ten of these Song inscriptions survive, the earliest dated 1174']],
  s:['这套制度元朝原样接手,泉州在元代升到顶点;明初转向海禁,市舶司几度废立,职能缩成了接待朝贡使团。回头看,它是前近代中国把「向海洋要收入」做成国家制度的那一段——这条路后来断了,断在哪里、为什么断,成了后世反复回看的问题。',
     'The Yuan took the system over as it stood, and Quanzhou reached its peak under them; the early Ming turned to sea bans, and the offices, abolished and restored by turns, shrank into receiving tribute missions. In hindsight this was the stretch when pre-modern China made revenue from the sea a working institution of state — a road later closed, and where and why it closed became a question later ages kept returning to.'] },
'经学院网络': { g:'inst', c:'阿尤布王朝', y:[1171,1260], n:['经学院网络','The madrasa network'],
  t:['一种自带经费的学校:租金付教授薪水,学生管吃管住——王朝拿它当国策,一所接一所地办。','Schools with their funding built in: rents paid the professors and students got board and lodging — and the dynasty made founding them a policy of state, one after another.'],
  a:[['每所经学院背后都有一份瓦克夫(宗教地产):创办人把店铺、浴场、田庄的租金永久捐出,契约里一条条写明用途——教授的薪俸、学生的口粮、房舍的维护,原则上永远不得挪用。学校因此不收学费,也不必年年向国库伸手',
      'Behind every madrasa stood a waqf, a religious endowment: the founder signed over the rents of shops, bathhouses and farmland for ever, the deed spelling out line by line what they paid for — professors\' salaries, students\' rations, upkeep of the buildings — never, in principle, to be diverted. So the schools charged no fees and asked the treasury for nothing'],
     ['办法是从东边学来的——塞尔柱人早几十年就在办,萨拉丁入主埃及后接了过来,家族与将领跟着建,一路铺开。学校教的核心是教法学,而毕业没有校方文凭,只有「伊贾扎」——老师个人签给学生的授课许可:认师不认校,一位名师的弟子散到各城,就是一张现成的学网',
      'The method was learned from the east — the Seljuks had been running such schools for decades, and Saladin took it up once he held Egypt, his family and commanders founding more all along the way. What they taught above all was law, and graduating brought no institutional diploma, only an ijaza — a licence to teach signed by the master in person: the school did not certify you, your teacher did, and one famous master\'s students scattered across the cities made a ready web of learning'],
     ['1242 年创办的萨利赫经学院走得最远:埃及第一所把逊尼派四大法学派同时开课的学校,四个学派各占一翼。创办它的苏丹萨利赫 1249 年在抵抗十字军的军中病逝,王妃沙贾尔·杜尔把他的陵墓接着盖进了学院——此后「学院连着创办人的圆顶陵墓」在开罗成了定式',
      'The Salihiyya, founded in 1242, went furthest: the first school in Egypt teaching all four Sunni schools of law at once, one wing for each. Its founder, Sultan al-Salih, fell ill and died in 1249 while resisting a crusade, and his widow Shajar al-Durr built his domed tomb onto the madrasa — after which a founder\'s tomb joined to his college became the Cairo pattern']],
  s:['经学院把学问变成了一件有恒产、有编制的事:法官、文书、教师从这里出,穷人家的孩子也能靠津贴读进这个阶层。马穆鲁克接手后把这套机制推到极盛,人人办学留名。有学者主张欧洲的学院制度借鉴过它,证据始终有争议;不争的是,开罗老城里那些连着圆顶陵墓的经学院,今天还立在街边。',
     'The madrasa made learning a thing with property and posts attached: judges, clerks and teachers came out of it, and a poor boy on a stipend could read his way into that class. The Mamluks took the machinery to its height, everyone founding schools to carry their names. Some scholars have argued that Europe\'s colleges borrowed from it — the evidence has never stopped being argued over; what is beyond argument is that the madrasas of old Cairo, domed tombs and all, still stand along the streets.'] },
'青铜舞王像': { g:'obj', c:'朱罗王朝', y:[950,1250], n:['青铜舞王像','The bronze Nataraja'],
  t:['一尊造出来就是为了出门的神像:庙里的石像永不挪窝,青铜的湿婆被抬出去巡游全城。','An idol cast to go out: the stone god never leaves the sanctum, but the bronze Shiva is carried through the streets for all to see.'],
  a:[['整尊像是一套完整的表达:一手执小鼓,鼓点是创世;一手托火焰,火是毁灭;第三只手掌心向外,意思是「别怕」;第四只手指向抬起的那只脚——解脱的出口在那里。脚下踩着的矮人,名字的意思是「遗忘与无知」',
      'The image is a complete statement: one hand holds the drum whose beat is creation, one holds the flame that is destruction, a third faces palm outward — fear not — and the fourth points to the lifted foot, where release lies. The dwarf under his foot bears a name meaning forgetfulness and ignorance'],
     ['铸法决定了每尊都是孤品:整尊像先用蜡雕成,裹上泥壳,烧掉蜡、灌进铜水,取像时必须打碎泥壳——一个模子只能用一次。朱罗铜像还是实心浇铸的,比空心铸法费铜得多,南印的神庙不在乎。今天通行的「标准舞王」形制,就是 10 世纪在朱罗定下来的',
      'The casting made every image unique: the figure was carved whole in wax, cased in clay, the wax burned out and bronze poured in — and the case had to be broken to free the statue, so no mould was ever used twice. Chola bronzes were cast solid besides, taking far more metal than hollow casting, and the southern temples did not mind. The canonical Nataraja form current today was fixed under the Cholas in the tenth century'],
     ['二十世纪初,雕塑家罗丹看到这尊像的照片,专门写文章赞叹它的动态;2004 年,印度政府把一尊近两米高的舞王像送给欧洲核子研究中心,立在园区里——物理学家拿湿婆之舞比喻基本粒子不停的生灭',
      'Early in the twentieth century the sculptor Rodin, shown photographs of the image, wrote an essay in praise of its movement; in 2004 the Indian government gave a Nataraja nearly two metres tall to CERN, where it stands on the grounds — physicists take Shiva\'s dance as a figure for the ceaseless birth and death of particles']],
  s:['名气带来另一面:朱罗铜像成了国际文物黑市上的抢手货,村庙的神像流进拍卖行与海外博物馆。2014 年澳大利亚国家美术馆归还的那尊舞王,就是从泰米尔纳德一座村庙里偷走的——追索还在继续,一尊一尊地要。',
     'Fame has its other face: Chola bronzes became prize goods on the antiquities black market, village-temple gods passing into auction houses and museums abroad. The Nataraja the National Gallery of Australia returned in 2014 had been stolen from a village temple in Tamil Nadu — and the recovery effort goes on, image by image.'] },
'变法运动': { g:'inst', c:'战国', y:[-420,-221], n:['变法运动','The reform movements'],
  t:['一场持续两百年的制度竞赛:七个国家轮番改制求生,改得最深的那个笑到了最后。','A two-hundred-year contest of institutions: seven states rebuilt themselves in turn to survive, and the deepest rebuild had the last laugh.'],
  a:[['第一轮在魏国:魏文侯用李悝,教农民把地力用尽(「尽地力之教」),又设「平籴」——丰年官府收购存粮,荒年平价放出,不让粮价大起大落。据后世史书记载,他还编成《法经》六篇,是成文法的早期范本;原书早已不传,这个记载本身的可靠性也有争论',
      'The first round was in Wei: Marquis Wen employed Li Kui, who taught farmers to wring the most from the land and set up balanced purchase — the state buying grain in good years and selling at fair prices in lean ones, so prices never swung wild. Later histories credit him with a six-part Canon of Laws, an early model of written statute; the text is long lost, and how far to trust the account is itself debated'],
     ['楚国的一轮最血腥:吴起替楚悼王削贵族——封君的爵禄传到第三代就收回,省下的钱练兵。前 381 年悼王一死,旧贵族当场发难,在灵堂上乱箭射杀吴起;他扑在悼王遗体上,射他的箭也射中了王尸。按楚法,伤害王尸罪及全族——新王即位后,因此被灭族的有七十多家',
      'The round in Chu was the bloodiest: Wu Qi, serving King Dao, cut the nobility down — titles and stipends reverted to the crown in the third generation, and the savings went to the army. The moment the king died in 381 BC the old nobles struck, shooting Wu Qi down with arrows in the funeral hall; he threw himself on the king\'s body, and the arrows that hit him struck the corpse too. Under Chu law, harming the royal body condemned the whole clan — and when the new king took the throne, more than seventy clans were wiped out for it'],
     ['变法的人大多没有好下场,法却未必跟着死:吴起死后,楚的改革大半停了;商鞅兵败身死、尸体被车裂示众,他立的法却留了下来——《韩非子》说得直白:「及孝公、商君死,惠王即位,秦法未败也」。改革能不能活过改革者,成了这场淘汰赛真正的分界线',
      'The reformers mostly came to bad ends; their laws did not always follow. With Wu Qi dead, most of Chu\'s reform stopped; Shang Yang died defeated and his body was torn apart by chariots for display, yet his laws stood — as the Han Feizi puts it bluntly, when Duke Xiao and the lord of Shang were dead, King Hui took the throne, and the laws of Qin did not fail. Whether a reform could outlive its reformer became the real dividing line of the elimination round']],
  s:['两千年后回看,这两百年像一场少有的对照实验:七个国家同时试验不同的改法,深浅不同,存亡立判,成败都留下了记录。后世也有分裂并立的时代,但像这样以变法为竞赛主轴的,再没有第二回。',
     'Looking back across two millennia, these two centuries read like a rare controlled experiment: seven states trying different reforms at once, to different depths, with survival keeping the score, and successes and failures alike on record. Later ages had their divisions too, but never again one whose contest turned on reform itself.'] },
'再统一': { g:'inst', c:'西晋', y:[280,311], n:['再统一','The reunification'],
  t:['三国的真正结局:一支从四川顺江而下的船队烧断拦江铁链,近百年的分裂收了场。','How the Three Kingdoms really ended: a fleet riding down from Sichuan burned through the chains across the Yangtze, and nearly a century of division closed.'],
  a:[['为这一仗,晋在四川造了多年大船。《晋书》说王濬造的连舫方一百二十步、可载两千余人,船上筑起楼橹、能骑马来往。吴军在长江险处横拉铁链、水下暗置铁锥;王濬先放大木筏把铁锥带走,再用灌满麻油的大火炬烧熔铁链,船队顺流直下',
      'Jin spent years building ships in Sichuan for this war. The Book of Jin says Wang Jun\'s joined vessels were a hundred and twenty paces on a side and carried over two thousand men, with towers on deck and room to ride a horse. The Wu defenders stretched iron chains across the narrows and hid iron spikes below the water; Wang Jun sent big rafts ahead to carry off the spikes, melted through the chains with great torches soaked in hemp oil, and ran the fleet downstream'],
     ['「势如破竹」这个成语就出自这场战争:有人主张来年再战,主帅之一的杜预说,如今兵威已振,譬如破竹,数节之后,迎刃而解。战局果然如此:279 年冬六路并进,王濬的船队 280 年年初发自成都,三月吴主孙皓出降,前后不过几个月',
      'The idiom "like splitting bamboo" comes from this war: when some argued for waiting a year, Du Yu, one of the commanders, said the army\'s momentum was like splitting bamboo — after the first few joints it parts at the touch of the blade. So it proved: six armies moved in the winter of 279, Wang Jun\'s fleet left Chengdu at the start of 280, Sun Hao surrendered in the third month, and the whole thing took a few months'],
     ['统一后的年号是「太康」,后来称「太康之治」:仗打完了,户口与垦田回升。但同一座洛阳城里,巨富石崇与外戚王恺公开斗富:王恺用麦芽糖水刷锅,石崇用蜡烛当柴烧;皇帝赐王恺一株二尺高的珊瑚树助阵,石崇随手击碎,搬出自家六七株三四尺高的还他',
      'The era after unification was Taikang, remembered as a decade of recovery: the wars over, registered households and tilled land climbed back. But in that same Luoyang the magnate Shi Chong and the imperial in-law Wang Kai competed in open extravagance: Wang Kai scoured his pots with malt syrup, Shi Chong burned candles for firewood; the emperor gave Wang Kai a coral tree two feet high to help his side, and Shi Chong smashed it with a flick and brought out six or seven of his own, three and four feet high']],
  s:['这次统一维持了三十一年:十年后诸王开战,304 年起北方各族相继建国,311 年洛阳陷落。此后中国重新裂成南北,直到 589 年隋灭陈才再次归一,中间隔了将近三百年。三国的故事家喻户晓,结局却常被讲丢——魏、蜀、吴谁都没赢,赢家是司马家;而司马家的胜利,也只撑了一代人。',
     'The reunification held for thirty-one years: the princes went to war within ten, from 304 the northern peoples began founding states of their own, and in 311 Luoyang fell. China split again into north and south and was not one country until Sui conquered Chen in 589, nearly three hundred years on. Everyone knows the Three Kingdoms story; the ending tends to get lost — Wei, Shu and Wu all lost, the Sima family won, and the Sima victory lasted one generation.'] },
'网格都市': { g:'tech', c:'特奥蒂瓦坎', y:[100,550], n:['网格都市','The gridded city'],
  t:['十万人口的大城,整体拧向同一侧:南北向的街道一律偏离正北 15.5 度。','A city of a hundred thousand twisted the same way throughout: every north–south street turned 15.5 degrees off true north.'],
  a:[['这个角度是刻意的:全城建筑统一偏东北约 15.5 度,东西向另有一组走向略偏 16.5 度,连后来加盖的房子也照此对齐。为什么偏偏是这个角度,学者提过几种解释——对准特定日子的日出日落方位、对准某些星辰的起落——都指向历法,尚无定论',
      'The angle was deliberate: the whole city tilts about 15.5 degrees east of north, with an east-west family of alignments at about 16.5, and even later buildings kept to it. Why that angle exactly has several proposed answers — sunrises and sunsets on particular dates, the rising and setting of certain stars — all pointing to the calendar, none settled'],
     ['放线的工具也找到了:一种凿在岩石和地面上的记号,两圈同心圆加一个十字,全由小凹点连成。城中心的一处与西边山坡上的一处连成直线,正是全城东西向的基准线;同样的记号在中美洲各地发现了几十处,最远的在几百公里外',
      'The surveying tools have been found: a mark cut into rock and floor, two concentric circles crossed by two lines, all made of small pecked pits. One in the city centre and one on a hillside to the west line up exactly on the city\'s east-west base line; marks of the same design turn up in dozens of places across Mesoamerica, the farthest hundreds of kilometres away'],
     ['这张网格有多大,现代人重新量过才知道:1960 年代起,考古队用航拍加逐街踏查,花了约十年把全城测绘成一张总图——网格覆盖约二十平方公里。城没有城墙,城的边界,就是网格淡出的地方',
      'How big the grid was, moderns learned by measuring it again: from the 1960s an archaeological project spent about ten years mapping the whole city by air photo and street-by-street survey — the grid covers some twenty square kilometres. There was no city wall; the edge of the city is simply where the grid fades out']],
  s:['「亡者大道」这个名字本身是个美丽的误会:后来阿兹特克人走进废墟,把大道两侧的平台错认成了坟冢,名字就这么来的。他们把这座城读错了,可这条被读错的大道至今笔直——站上月亮金字塔往南看,两千年前放的那条线,一眼望到头。',
     'The name Avenue of the Dead is itself a beautiful mistake: when the Aztecs came upon the ruins they mistook the platforms flanking the avenue for tombs, and the name followed. They misread the city — but the misread avenue is still dead straight: stand on the Pyramid of the Moon and look south, and the line laid down two thousand years ago runs true to the horizon.'] },

};
